#{[#edb/ref 17592186056005 "A **compound statement** is a sentence that consists of two or more statements separated by logical connectors, such as the word \"and\" or the word \"or.\"\n\nFor example, the following sentence is a compound statement:\n\n$P: 2$ is an integer **and** $2$ is positive \n\nThe first statement is \"$2$ is an integer,\" and the second is \"$2$ is positive.\" These two statements are separated by the logical connector \"and.\"\n\nThe following sentence is also a compound statement:\n\n$7$ is odd **or** $7$ is even \n\nIn this case, the first statement is \"$7$ is odd,\" and the second statement is \"$7$ is even.\" These two statements are separated by the logical connector \"or.\" \n\nWe can express the logical connectives \"and\" and \"or\" using symbols:\n\n- The word \"and\" is represented by the symbol \"$\\land$\". Notice that it resembles \"A\" for \"And.\"\n\n- The word \"or\" is represented by the symbol \"$\\lor$\".\n\nLet's translate the following sentence into symbolic form:\n\n$2$ is an integer **and** $2$ is positive \n\nTo start, we introduce the letters $A$ and $B$ to denote each individual statement:\n\n- $A:$ $2$ is an integer\n\n- $B:$  $2$ is positive\n\nThen, our sentence becomes\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{2 \\vphantom{p}\\textrm{ is an integer} }_A \\quad \\underbrace{\\vphantom{p}\\textrm{and}}_{\\land} \\quad \\underbrace{2 \\vphantom{p}\\textrm{ is positive} }_B,\n$$\n\nthat is, $A \\land B.$\n\nFinally, note the following terminology:\n\n- The statement \"$P$ and $Q$\" is called the **conjunction** of the statements $P$ and $Q.$\n\n- The statement \"$P$ or $Q$\" is called the **disjunction** of the statements $P$ and $Q.$" #edb/ref 13194139535324 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186056006 "We've already seen that $5^2$ means $5\\cdot 5,$ and that $5^3$ means $5\\cdot 5 \\cdot 5.$ We can extend this to have any number in the exponent, for example\n\n$$\n5^{\\color{blue}7} = \\underbrace{5\\cdot 5\\cdot 5\\cdot 5\\cdot 5\\cdot 5 \\cdot 5}_{\\large{\\color{blue}7} \\textrm{ copies of } 5}.\n$$\n\nThe **exponent** $({\\color{blue}7})$ says how many times the **base** $(5)$ is multiplied by itself.\n\nRecall that $5^2$ is said as \"five squared\" and that $5^3$ is \"five cubed.\" We don't have special names for larger exponents. However, we can say $5^7$  in several different ways:\n\n- \"five raised to the seventh power,\"\n\n- \"five raised to the power seven,\"\n\n- \"five to the seventh power,\"\n\n- \"five to the power seven.\"\n\nRaising to the power of one is special. Any number raised to the first power is itself. For example, $5^{\\color{blue}1}$ means one copy of $5,$ so $5^{\\color{blue}1} = 5.$" #edb/ref 13194139535148 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186056019 "As we already know, to evaluate numerical expressions with a mix of different operations, we must follow the order of operations (PEMDAS).\n\nThe same rules apply to numerical expressions with negative numbers. For example, let's compute the value of\n\n$$\n(-2) \\times (-3)^2.\n$$\n\nTo evaluate this expression, we use PEMDAS.  First, we compute the exponents:\n\n$$\n(-2)\\,\\times\\overbrace{(-3)^2}^{\\large\\color{blue} 9}  = (-2) \\times {\\color{blue} 9}\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, we multiply:\n\n$$\n(-2) \\times 9 = -18\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n(-2) \\times (-3)^2 = -18.\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139535213 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186056029 "Let's now consider the truth values of the logical connector \"and\". We have the following key idea:\n\n*The statement $A \\land B$ is true if $A$ is true **and** $B$ is true. Otherwise, it is false.* \n\nLet's determine the truth values of the following compound statement:\n\n$2$ is positive **and** $2$ is even.\n\nWriting this in symbols, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{2\\text{ is positive}}_{⏟}}_{A}\\,{{\\text{and}}_{⏟}}_{∧}\\,{{2\\text{ is even}}_{⏟}}_{B}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nor, simply\n\n$$\nA\\land B.\n$$\n\nSince $A$ is true and $B$ is true, this compound statement is true:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A∧B≡\\text{true}∧\\text{true}≡\\text{true}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this context, the symbol \"$\\equiv$\" means \"is logically equivalent to.\" \n\nLet's now consider a similar compound statement but with the $2$ replaced with a $3$ in the second statement.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{2\\text{ is positive}}_{⏟}}_{A}\\,{{\\text{and}}_{⏟}}_{∧}\\,{{3\\text{ is even}}_{⏟}}_{C}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can write this symbolically as follows:\n\n$$\nA\\land C\n$$\n\nSince $A$ is true and $C$ is false, this compound statement is false:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A∧C≡\\text{true}∧\\text{false}≡\\text{false}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139535324 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186056031 "When solving equations with fractional coefficients, there is a shortcut! We can complete division and multiplication in a single step by multiplying both sides of the equation by the reciprocal of the fractional coefficient.\n\nFor example, to solve the equation $\\dfrac{2x}{5}= 6,$ we first express the fraction as a coefficient:\n\n$$\n\\require{cancel}\n\t\\eqalign{\n    \t\t\\dfrac{2}{5}x &= 6\n\t}\n$$\n\nThe fractional coefficient is $\\dfrac{2}{5}$, and its reciprocal is $\\left(\\dfrac 2 5\\right)^{-1} = {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac 5 2}}.$ Because the product of a fraction and its reciprocal is equal to $1,$ we can multiply both sides of the equation by the reciprocal to isolate $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\require{cancel}\n\t\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\dfrac{2}{5}x &= 6 \\[5pt]\n\t\t\\underbrace{ {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac 5 2}}  \\cdot \\dfrac{2}{5} }_{\\large =\\, 1} x &= {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac 5 2}} \\cdot 6 \\[5pt]   \n\t\t1 x  &= \\dfrac{5\\cdot 6}{2} \\[5pt]   \n\t\tx  &= \\dfrac{30}{2} \\[5pt]   \n\n\t\tx &= 15\n\t}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139535252 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186056033 "Suppose we have the indexed family $\\{A_i\\}_{i\\in I}$ where $I$ is an index set. \n\nThe **union** of a family of indexed sets is the set containing all elements belonging to *at least one* set in the indexed family:\n\n$$\n\\bigcup_{i \\in I} A_i = \\big\\{ x\\in U \\: : \\: x \\in A_i \\:\\:\\textrm{for at least one } A_i,\\:\\: i \\in I \\big\\}\n$$\n\nwhere $U$ is a universal set.\n\nFor example, suppose we have the following family of indexed sets:\n\n$$\nX_i = \\{ -i, \\,0, \\, i \\}, \\qquad i\\in \\mathbb N\n$$\n\nWe wish to calculate the following union:\n\n$$\n\\bigcup_{i \\in \\mathbb N} X_i\n$$\n\nTo calculate our union, let's write down a few of our indexed sets:\n\n- If $i=1,$ we have $X_1 = \\{ -1, 0, 1 \\}.$\n\n- If $i=2,$ we have $X_2 = \\{ -2, 0, 2 \\}.$\n\n- If $i=3,$ we have $X_3 = \\{ -3, 0, 3 \\}.$\n\n- $\\cdots$\n\nand so on. \n\nNow, note the following:\n\n- It's clear that the $X_i$'s contain only integers. Therefore, any union of $X_i$'s will contain only integers. So, \n\n$$\n\\bigcup_{i \\in \\mathbb N} X_i  \\subseteq \\mathbb Z.\n$$\n\n- Notice that *any* integer $n$ belongs to our union. For instance, $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$ lies in $X_n=\\{ -n, 0, n \\}$ and, consequently, lies in the union of all $X_i$'s. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\bigcup_{i \\in \\mathbb N} X_i  \\supseteq \\mathbb Z.\n$$\n\nThus, using the fact that $A\\subseteq B$ and $B\\subseteq A$ if and only if $A=B,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{⋃}_{i\\in N}{X}_{i} & =\\overset{{X}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{{-1,0,1}}}\\cup \\overset{{X}_{2}}{\\overset{⏞}{{-2,0,2}}}\\cup \\overset{{X}_{3}}{\\overset{⏞}{{-3,0,3}}}\\cup ⋯=Z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since our index set $I = \\mathbb N$ in this example, we can also use the following notation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{⋃}_{i=1}^{\\infty }{X}_{i}=Z\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139533440 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186056046 "Suppose we have a finite set $S$ with cardinality $|S|.$ Then, the cardinality of $\\mathcal P(S)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\left|\\mathcal{P}(S)\\right| = 2^{\\left| S \\right|}.\n$$\n\nIn other words, a finite set with cardinality $|S|$ has $2^{|S|}$ subsets.\n\nFor example, for the set $S = \\{ a,b,c \\},$ we have $|S| = 3,$ and therefore\n\n$$\n\\left|\\mathcal{P}(S)\\right| = 2^{\\left| S \\right|} = 2^3 = 8.\n$$\n\nThis tells us that $S$ has $8$ subsets, and consequently, $\\mathcal{P}(S)$ has $8$ elements. \n\n$$\n\\mathcal{P}(S) = \\big\\{\n\\underbrace{\\emptyset}_1,\n\\underbrace{ \\{a\\}}_2,\n\\underbrace{ \\{b\\}}_3,\n\\underbrace{ \\{c\\}}_4,\n\\underbrace{ \\{ a,b \\}}_5,\n\\underbrace{ \\{ a,c \\}}_6,\n\\underbrace{ \\{ b,c \\}}_7,\n\\underbrace{ \\{ a,b,c \\}}_8 \n\\big\\}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- It's clear from our rule that if $S$ is finite, then $\\mathcal P(S)$ is finite.\n\n- It can be shown that if $S$ is infinite, then $\\mathcal P(S)$ is also infinite.\n\nWe'll discuss power sets of infinite sets at the end of this lesson and in future lessons." #edb/ref 13194139535319 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186056053 "We can extend the definition of the Cartesian product to include three or more sets.\n\nFor example, if we have three sets $A,B,$ and $C,$ then the Cartesian product $A\\times B\\times C$ is defined as\n\n$$\nA\\times B\\times C = \\big\\{(a, b, c) \\: : \\: a \\in A,\\, b \\in B, c\\in C \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nIn other words, $A\\times B\\times C$ contains all **ordered triples** $(a,b,c)$ such that $a$ belongs to $A, b$ belongs to $B,$ and $c$ belongs to $C.$\n\nFor example, if \n\n$$\nA=\\{{\\color{blue}{1}},{\\color{blue}{2}}\\},\\quad B=\\{{\\color{red}{x}}, {\\color{red}{y}}\\},\\quad \\quad C=\\{z\\},\n$$\n\nthen \n\n$$\nA\\times B\\times C = \\Big\\{({\\color{blue}{1}},{\\color{red}{x}},z),\\, ({\\color{blue}{1}},{\\color{red}{y}},z),\\, ({\\color{blue}{2}},{\\color{red}{x}},z),\\, ({\\color{blue}{2}},{\\color{red}{y}},z)\\Big\\}.\n$$\n\nWe can extend the cardinality rule as follows:\n\n$$\n|A\\times B\\times C| = |A|\\cdot|B|\\cdot |C|\n$$\n\nSo, in the case above,\n\n$$\n|A\\times B\\times C| = |A|\\cdot|B|\\cdot |C| = 2\\cdot2\\cdot 1 = 4.\n$$\n\nNothing stops us from defining the Cartesian product of any finite number of sets! So, if $A_1, A_2, \\ldots, A_n$ denotes a collection of $n$ sets, then\n\n$$\nA_1\\times A_2\\times\\cdots\\times A_n =  \\Big\\{(a_1, a_2, \\ldots, a_n) \\: : \\: a_i \\in A_i, 1\\leq i\\leq n \\Big\\},\n$$\n\nwhere $(a_1,a_2,\\ldots,a_n)$ is an **ordered tuple**. \n\nOnce again, the cardinality rule extends intuitively:\n\n$$\n|A_1\\times A_2\\times \\cdots \\times A_n| = |A_1|\\cdot|A_2|\\cdots |A_n|\n$$\n\nThe **Cartesian power** $A^n$ of a set $A$ for natural $n$ is defined as\n\n$$\nA^n = \\underbrace{A\\times A\\times\\cdots\\times A}_{n \\textrm{ times}} =  \\Big\\{(a_1, a_2, \\ldots, a_n) \\: : \\: a_i \\in A, 1\\leq i\\leq n \\Big\\}.\n$$\n\nFor example,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{3} & =A\\times A\\times A \\\\  & ={1,2}\\times {1,2}\\times {1,2} \\\\  & ={(1,1,1)\\,(1,1,2)\\,(1,2,1)\\,(1,2,2)\\,(2,1,1)\\,(2,1,2)\\,(2,2,1)\\,(2,2,2)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139533440 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186056062 "To multiply two-digit decimals, we can use the usual method of removing and replacing the decimal point.\n\nAs an example, let's learn how to compute $0.4 \\times 0.9$. We proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1.** First, ignore the decimal points and multiply as if both numbers were whole numbers:\n\n$$\n4 \\times 9 = 36\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** Then, insert the decimal point in the result so that number of decimal places is equal to the total number of decimal places in *both factors combined.*\n\nHere, there is $\\color{blue}1$ decimal place in $0.4$ and there is $\\color{blue}1$ decimal place in $0.9$, so their product will have a total of\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}1} + {\\color{blue}1} = 2\n$$\n\ndecimal places.\n\nWe take our value of $36$ and insert a decimal point to make a number with $2$ decimal places:\n\n$$\n0\\,\\overset{\\color{red}\\downarrow}{\\color{red}\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{.}}\\!\\!\\!\\underbrace{36}_{\\large{ {2} \\text{ digits}}}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n0.4 \\times 0.9 = 0.36 \\,\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139536831 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [#edb/ref 17592186056063 "We can use the same method to multiply decimals even when they are greater than $1$. For example, to compute\n\n$$\n4.7 \\times 0.8\n$$\n\nwe first ignore the decimal points and multiply as if both numbers were whole numbers. We ignore any leading ${\\color{red}{0}}$'s during the multiplication:\n\n$$\n5; 4\\begin{vmatrix}.7 \\\\ \\cdot\\end{vmatrix}0\\begin{vmatrix}.8 \\\\ 3\\end{vmatrix}7 \\mid 6\n$$\n\nWe now count the total number of decimal places in the two factors.\n\nThere is $\\color{blue}1$ decimal place in $4.7$ and there is $\\color{blue}1$ decimal place in $0.8$, so their product will have\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}1} + {\\color{blue}1} = 2\n$$\n\ndecimal places.\n\nWe take our value of $376$ and insert a decimal point to make a number with $2$ decimal places:\n\n$$\n3\\,\\overset{\\color{red}\\downarrow}{\\color{red}\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{.}}\\!\\!\\!\\underbrace{76}_{\\large{ {2} \\text{ digits}}}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n4.7 \\times 0.8 = 3.76 \\,\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139536831 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [#edb/ref 17592186056067 "Suppose that we have the two polynomials $6x^2 + x +1$ and $2x^2 - 3x + 6$, and we want to add them. To do that, we put a plus sign between the two, collect like terms, and then simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(6x^{2} + x + 1) + (2x^{2} - 3x + 6) &=  \\\\\n6x^{2} + x + 1 + 2x^{2} - 3x + 6 &=  \\\\\n6x^{2} + 2x^{2}_(⏟)_(x^{2}terms) + x - 3x^(⏞)^(xterms) + 1 + 6_(⏟)_(constants) &=  \\\\\n(6 + 2)x^{2} + (1 - 3)x + 7 &=  \\\\\n&= 8x^{2} - 2x + 7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139533346 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186056069 "Let's think about how to factor the following trinomial:\n\n$$\nx^2 + 7x + 12\n$$\n\nThis trinomial is neither a perfect square nor a difference of squares. In such cases, the factoring process requires a little trial and error.\n\nWe can factor this trinomial in four steps:\n\n- **Step 1:** Find the factor pairs of the constant term $(12)$:The factor pairs of $12$ are\n$(1, 12), (2, 6), (3, 4)$.\n- **Step 2:** Determine which of these factor pairs add up to the coefficient of the linear term $(7)$:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 + 12|= 13 & \\cdot \\\\ 2 + 6|= 8 & \\cdot \\\\ 3 + 4|= 7 & ✓\\end{bmatrix}$\n- **Step 3:** Using the factor pair identified in the previous step, write the linear term as $7x=3x+4x$:\n$x^{2} + 7x + 12|=; x^{2} + 3x + 4x_(⏟)_(7x) + 12$\n- **Step 4:** Factor the first two terms and the last two terms separately and then factor the expression completely:\n$x^{2} + 3x + 4x + 12|=; (x^{2} + 3x) + (4x + 12)|=; x(x + 3) + 4(x + 3)|=; (x + 4)(x + 3)$\n\nAnd we're done!\n\nBefore we continue, note the following:\n\n- The process carried out in step 4 is sometimes called **factoring by grouping.** This is because we group two sets of terms and factor them individually before factoring the entire expression.\n- We can check that our answer is correct by multiplying out the factors:\n$(x + 4)(x + 3)|= x(x + 3) + 4(x + 3); = x^{2} + 3x + 4x + 12; = x^{2} + 7x + 12✓$" #edb/ref 13194139533346 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186056072 "Let's now consider the truth values of the logical connector \"or\". We have the following key idea:\n\n*The statement $A \\lor B$ is true if $A$ is true, or $B$ is true, or both $A$ and $B$ are true. Otherwise, it is false.* \n\nLet's consider some examples:\n\n- First, consider the following statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{2\\text{ is positive}}_{⏟}}_{A}\\,{{\\text{or}}_{⏟}}_{∨}\\,{{2\\text{ is even}}_{⏟}}_{B}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A$ is true and $B$ is true, this compound statement is true:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A∨B≡\\text{true}∨\\text{true}≡\\text{true}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, consider the following statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{2\\text{ is positive}}_{⏟}}_{A}\\,{{\\text{or}}_{⏟}}_{∨}\\,{{3\\text{ is even}}_{⏟}}_{C}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A$ is true, this compound statement is true:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A∨C≡\\text{true}∨\\text{false}≡\\text{true}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Now, consider the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{-2\\text{ is positive}}_{⏟}}_{D}\\,{{\\text{or}}_{⏟}}_{∨}\\,{{2\\text{ is even}}_{⏟}}_{B}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $B$ is true, this compound statement is true:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D∨B≡\\text{false}∨\\text{true}≡\\text{true}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Finally, consider the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{-2\\text{ is positive}}_{⏟}}_{D}\\,{{\\text{or}}_{⏟}}_{∨}\\,{{3\\text{ is even}}_{⏟}}_{C}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $D$ and $C$ are both false, this compound statement is false:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D∨C≡\\text{false}∨\\text{false}≡\\text{false}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139535324 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186056075 "You might wonder why we need to define a universal set. Can't we just let the universal set be the set of everything?\n\nThe answer is no. If we don't specify a well-defined universal set, we encounter a nonsensical situation called **Russell's paradox.**\n\nFirst, suppose there exists a set $\\Omega$ that contains absolutely everything, including every mathematical object, every thought, every book, every person, animal, cell, and star in the universe, past, present, or future. \n\nRussell's paradox originates from the following idea:\n\n*Define $S$ as the set that contains all sets that are not elements of themselves.* \n\n$$\nS = \\{A\\in \\Omega\\,:\\, A\\notin A\\}\n$$\n\nMost sets you can think of are elements of $S.$ For example, the set $\\{ 1,2,3 \\}$ is in $S$ because $\\{ 1,2,3 \\}\\notin \\{ 1,2,3 \\}.$ \n\nIt takes a bit of thought to find sets that *aren't* members of $S.$ Such sets do exist, although typical examples are somewhat abstract: \n\n- The set containing all possible sets.\n\n- The set containing all possible thoughts.\n\n- The set $X,$ given by\n\n$$\nX = \\Big\\{\\underbrace{\\big\\{ \\left\\{\\{\\ldots \\}\\right\\}\\big\\}}_{X}\\Big\\}.\n$$\n\nHowever, what about $S$ itself? Is $S$ an element of $S?$ There are only two possibilities:\n\n- If $S\\in S,$ then by the definition of $S,$ we have that $S\\notin S.$ But this is a contradiction because we cannot have $S\\in S$ *and* $S\\notin S.$\n\n- If $S\\notin S,$ then by the definition of $S,$ we must have that $S\\in S.$ But this leads to the same contradiction as before!\n\nThe only way to resolve this is to conclude that $S\\notin \\Omega,$ but this is also a contradiction since $\\Omega$ contains absolutely everything!  So, there is no consistent answer to the question of whether $S$ itself is an element of $S.$ This is Russell's paradox, and when it was discovered, it shook the foundations of mathematics to its very core.\n\nLater, it was realized that if a set is defined using a predicate, we should ensure that all sets involved are subsets of some larger set $U$ (i.e., a universal set) where we can always determine which elements are members of $U$ and which are not, at least in principle.\n\nTo see how this resolves Russell's paradox, suppose $U$ is a universal set. Let's refine our definition of $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\nS = \\{A\\subseteq U\\,:\\,A\\notin A\\}\n$$\n\nWe'll assume $S$ is a set, so $S\\subseteq U$ for some well-defined $U.$ Once again, we'll ask the question of whether $S\\in S{:}$\n\n- If $S\\in S,$ then by the definition of $S,$ we have that $S\\notin S.$ But this is a contradiction.\n\n- If $S\\notin S,$ then by the definition of $S,$ we have that $S\\in S.$ But this is also a contradiction.\n\nThus, to resolve the paradox, we're left with no choice but to conclude that $S\\not\\subseteq U.$ This indicates that something is fundamentally wrong with our definition of $S,$ and no such set can exist because if it were a set, it would belong to a universal set." #edb/ref 13194139536042 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186060861 "We can use reference angles to evaluate trigonometric ratios for angles outside the first quadrant. \n\nTo demonstrate, let's determine the value of $\\cos (135^\\circ),$ corresponding to the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ on the unit circle, as shown below.\n\n![](images/be/bef50a070785e4bb86867ba6404942fd7eaa04b60720adfb04176bafe2fa2121.png)\n\nThe reference angle for $\\theta=135^\\circ$ is $\\theta_R=45^\\circ.$ Let's add this to our diagram.\n\n![](images/33/33b208e44aaa556a67ebaf7a6c1117b09adf863c56deb1bc1f5590e289062c4b.png)\n\nLet $P'$ be the image of $P$ under a reflection across the $y$-axis. This gives a pair of congruent angles of measure $45^\\circ,$ as shown.\n\n![](images/c4/c4875682498edaadc74a8af238fdf3293b4820025256df78e60867940061785e.png)\n\nAccording to the definition, $\\cos(135^\\circ)$ is the $x$-coordinate of the point $P.$ But this is the same as the $x$-coordinate of the point $P'$ *taken with the opposite sign.* \n\nThe $x$-coordinate of $P'$ equals the cosine of the reference angle. \n\n$$\n\\cos(45^\\circ)=\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\cos(\\, \\underbrace{135^\\circ}_{\\theta} \\,) = - \\cos(\\, \\underbrace{45^\\circ}_{\\theta_R} \\,) = -\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, we simplified the evaluation of $\\cos(135^\\circ)$ by evaluating the cosine of the corresponding reference angle $\\theta_R=45^\\circ$ *with an additional sign adjustment* for the final result. \n\nWe can use a similar argument to show that, since $y = \\sin\\theta,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\sin(135^\\circ) = \\sin(45^\\circ) = \\dfrac{\\sqrt 2}{2}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we do **NOT** change the sign because the $y$-coordinates of $P'$ and $P$ *are equal.*" #edb/ref 13194139535918 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186060894 "To understand why the derivative of sine is cosine, we need to use the definition of the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}} {\\textrm{d}x} (\\sin x) =  \\lim_{h\\rightarrow 0}\\dfrac{\\sin(x+h) - \\sin x}{h}\n$$\n\nNow, the so-called **addition formula for sine** states that\n\n$$\n\\sin(x+h) = \\sin(x)\\cos(h)+\\cos (x)  \\sin(h).\n$$\n\nBy using the addition formula for sine, we can rewrite our expression for the derivative as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(h)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(h)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{h} \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(h)-1)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(h)}{h} \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\frac{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(h)-1)}{h}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(h)}{h}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x){{{lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(h)-1)}{h}}_{⏟}}_{0}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x){{{lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(h)}{h}}_{⏟}}_{1} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the last step, we used two important results for trigonometric limits:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{h\\rightarrow 0} \\dfrac{1 - \\cos(h)}{h}=0 \\qquad \\text{and} \\qquad \\lim_{h\\rightarrow 0} \\dfrac{\\sin(h)}{h}=1.\n$$\n\nDeriving these results requires separate discussions, which we'll leave to future topics." #edb/ref 13194139536395 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186060895 "We can follow the same method for the cosine and use the definition of the derivative to show that $(\\cos x)'=-\\sin x.$ First, we start with the definition of the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}} {\\textrm{d}x} (\\cos x) =  \\lim_{h\\rightarrow 0}\\dfrac{\\cos(x+h) - \\cos x}{h}\n$$\n\nNow, the so-called **addition formula for cosine** states that\n\n$$\n\\cos(x+h) = \\cos(x)\\cos(h)- \\sin(x)\\sin(h).\n$$\n\nUsing the addition formula for cosine, we can rewrite our expression for the derivative as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(h)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(h)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)}{h} \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(h)-1)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(h)}{h} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x){{{lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(h)-1}{h}}_{⏟}}_{0}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x){{{lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(h)}{h}}_{⏟}}_{1} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOnce again, in the last step, we used the special limits for sine and cosine." #edb/ref 13194139536395 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186060921 "To understand the reasoning behind the formula for the dot product of two vectors, it's helpful to write the two vectors in terms of their elementary unit vectors and carry out the computation the long way.\n\nFor example, suppose we want to find the dot product $\\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{b}$ for $\\mathbf{a} = \\langle 3,-5 \\rangle$ and $\\mathbf{b}=\\langle 4,6 \\rangle.$\n\nExpressing the vectors in terms of their elementary unit vectors, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a=3i-5j,\\,b=4i+6j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using properties of the dot product we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & =(3i+(-5)j)\\cdot (4i+6j) \\\\  & =(3\\cdot 4)i\\cdot i+(3\\cdot 6)i\\cdot j+(-5\\cdot 4)j\\cdot i+(-5\\cdot 6)j\\cdot j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRemember that $\\mathbf{i},\\mathbf{j}$ are perpendicular, meaning the angle between them is $\\theta=90^\\circ$ and consequently their dot product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}i\\cdot j & =|\\,i\\,|\\cdot |\\,j\\,|\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}{90}^{∘} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 1\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, since the angle between a vector and itself is $\\theta=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}i\\cdot i & =|\\,i\\,|\\cdot |\\,i\\,|\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}{0}^{∘} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}j\\cdot j & =|\\,j\\,|\\cdot |\\,j\\,|\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}{0}^{∘} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}i\\cdot j & =j\\cdot i=0, \\\\ i\\cdot i & =j\\cdot j=1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand our computation reduces to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & =(3\\cdot 4)\\overset{=1}{\\overset{⏞}{i\\cdot i}}+(3\\cdot 6)\\overset{=0}{\\overset{⏞}{i\\cdot j}}+(-5\\cdot 4)\\overset{=0}{\\overset{⏞}{j\\cdot i}}+(-5\\cdot 6)\\overset{=1}{\\overset{⏞}{j\\cdot j}} \\\\  & =(3\\cdot 4)\\cdot 1+(3\\cdot 6)\\cdot 0+(-5\\cdot 4)\\cdot 0+(-5\\cdot 6)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =(3\\cdot 4)+(-5\\cdot 6).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & =⟨3,-5⟩\\cdot ⟨4,6⟩ \\\\  & =(3\\cdot 4)+(-5\\cdot 6).\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139533440 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186060923 "We have been using the following formula to compute the cross product of two vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\times b & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {b}_{3}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ {b}_{2} & {b}_{3}\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & {a}_{3} \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{3}\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & {a}_{2} \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2}\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =({a}_{2}{b}_{3}-{a}_{3}{b}_{2})i-({a}_{1}{b}_{3}-{a}_{3}{b}_{1})j+({a}_{1}{b}_{2}-{a}_{2}{b}_{1})k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBut where does this formula come from? Let's find out.\n\nSuppose we want to find the cross product $\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{b}$ for $\\mathbf{a}=a_1\\mathbf{i}+a_2\\mathbf{j}+a_3\\mathbf{k}$ and $\\mathbf{b}=b_1\\mathbf{i}+b_2\\mathbf{j}+b_3\\mathbf{k}.$ Using properties of the cross product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\times b & =({a}_{1}i+{a}_{2}j+{a}_{3}k)\\times ({b}_{1}i+{b}_{2}j+{b}_{3}k) \\\\  & =({a}_{1}\\cdot {b}_{1})i\\times i+({a}_{1}\\cdot {b}_{2})i\\times j+({a}_{1}\\cdot {b}_{3})i\\times k \\\\  & +({a}_{2}\\cdot {b}_{1})j\\times i+({a}_{2}\\cdot {b}_{2})j\\times j+({a}_{2}\\cdot {b}_{3})j\\times k \\\\  & +({a}_{3}\\cdot {b}_{1})k\\times i+({a}_{3}\\cdot {b}_{2})k\\times j+({a}_{3}\\cdot {b}_{3})k\\times k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the cross product of two parallel (collinear) vectors is equal to $\\mathbf 0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{i}\\times\\mathbf{i} = \\mathbf{j}\\times\\mathbf{j} = \\mathbf{k}\\times\\mathbf{k} = \\mathbf 0.\n$$\n\nAlso, using the definition of the cross product we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}i\\times j=k, & \\,j\\times i=-k, \\\\ j\\times k=i, & \\,k\\times j=-i, \\\\ k\\times i=j, & \\,i\\times k=-j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\times b & =({a}_{1}\\cdot {b}_{1}){{i\\times i}_{⏟}}_{=0}+({a}_{1}\\cdot {b}_{2}){{i\\times j}_{⏟}}_{=k}+({a}_{1}\\cdot {b}_{3}){{i\\times k}_{⏟}}_{=-j} \\\\  & +({a}_{2}\\cdot {b}_{1}){{j\\times i}_{⏟}}_{=-k}+({a}_{2}\\cdot {b}_{2}){{j\\times j}_{⏟}}_{=0}+({a}_{2}\\cdot {b}_{3}){{j\\times k}_{⏟}}_{=i} \\\\  & +({a}_{3}\\cdot {b}_{1}){{k\\times i}_{⏟}}_{=j}+({a}_{3}\\cdot {b}_{2}){{k\\times j}_{⏟}}_{=-i}+({a}_{3}\\cdot {b}_{3}){{k\\times k}_{⏟}}_{=0} \\\\  & =({a}_{2}{b}_{3}-{a}_{3}{b}_{2})i-({a}_{1}{b}_{3}-{a}_{3}{b}_{1})j+({a}_{1}{b}_{2}-{a}_{2}{b}_{1})k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe expression above is equal to the formula given for the cross product of two general vectors using the determinant." #edb/ref 13194139533440 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186060930 "If we want to multiply one square matrix by another (which has the same dimensions), we multiply each row in the first matrix by each column in the second. \n\nFor example, suppose we want to multiply two matrices \n$A = \\left\\lbrack\n    \\begin{matrix}\n        1 & 6  \\\\\n        4 & 3\n    \\end{matrix}\n    \\right\\rbrack$\nand\n$B = \\left\\lbrack\n    \\begin{matrix}\n        -3 & 0  \\\\\n        2 & -5\n    \\end{matrix}\n    \\right\\rbrack.$\n\nThe matrices $A$ and $B$ are both $2 \\times 2$ matrices, and their product will be another $2 \\times 2$ matrix $C,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign {\n\t\\underbrace{\n    \\left\\lbrack\n    \\begin{matrix}\n        1 & 6  \\\\\n        4 & 3\n    \\end{matrix}\n    \\right\\rbrack}_{A}\n\n\t\\underbrace{\n    \\left\\lbrack\n    \\begin{matrix}\n        -3 & 0  \\\\\n        2 & -5\n    \\end{matrix}\n    \\right\\rbrack}_{B}\n\n\t&= \n\t\\underbrace{\n    \\left\\lbrack\n    \\begin{matrix}\n        \\ast & \\ast   \\\\\n        \\ast & \\ast \n    \\end{matrix}\n    \\right\\rbrack\n\t}_{C}\n}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the entries of $C$ (shown as $\\ast$'s in the equation above).\n\nTo find $c_{11},$ the entry at the intersection of the 1st row and 1st column, we calculate the dot product of the 1st row of $A$ and the 1st column of $B\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\eqalign {\n    \\left\\lbrack\n    \\begin{matrix}\n        \\color{blue}{1} & \\color{blue}{6}  \\\\\n        4 & 3\n    \\end{matrix}\n    \\right\\rbrack\n\n    \\left\\lbrack\n    \\begin{matrix}\n        \\color{red}{-3} & 0  \\\\\n        \\color{red}{2} & -5\n    \\end{matrix}\n    \\right\\rbrack\n\n\t&= \n    \\left\\lbrack\n    \\begin{matrix}\n        {\\color{blue}{1}} \\cdot ({\\color{red}{-3}}) + {\\color{blue}{6}} \\cdot {\\color{red}{2}} & \\ast   \\\\\n        \\ast & \\ast\n    \\end{matrix}\n    \\right\\rbrack\n\n\t= \\left\\lbrack\n    \\begin{matrix}\n        9 & \\ast   \\\\\n        \\ast & \\ast\n    \\end{matrix}\n    \\right\\rbrack\n}\n$$\n\nTo find $c_{12},$ the entry at the intersection of the 1st row and 2nd column, we calculate the dot product of the 1st row of $A$ and the 2nd column of $B\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\eqalign {\n    \\left\\lbrack\n    \\begin{matrix}\n        \\color{blue}{1} & \\color{blue}{6}  \\\\\n        4 & 3\n    \\end{matrix}\n    \\right\\rbrack\n\n    \\left\\lbrack\n    \\begin{matrix}\n        -3 & \\color{red}{0}  \\\\\n        2 & \\color{red}{-5}\n    \\end{matrix}\n    \\right\\rbrack\n\n\t&= \n    \\left\\lbrack\n    \\begin{matrix}\n        9 & {\\color{blue}{1}} \\cdot {\\color{red}{0}} + {\\color{blue}{6}} \\cdot ({\\color{red}{-5}})   \\\\\n        \\ast & \\ast\n    \\end{matrix}\n    \\right\\rbrack\n\n\t= \\left\\lbrack\n    \\begin{matrix}\n        9 & -30   \\\\\n        \\ast & \\ast\n    \\end{matrix}\n    \\right\\rbrack\n}\n$$\n\nTo find $c_{21},$ the entry at the intersection of the 2nd row and 1st column, we calculate the dot product of the 2nd row of $A$ and the 1st column of $B\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\eqalign {\n    \\left\\lbrack\n    \\begin{matrix}\n        {1} & {6}  \\\\\n        \\color{blue}{4} & \\color{blue}{3}\n    \\end{matrix}\n    \\right\\rbrack\n\n    \\left\\lbrack\n    \\begin{matrix}\n        \\color{red}{-3} & 0  \\\\\n        \\color{red}{2} & -5\n    \\end{matrix}\n    \\right\\rbrack\n\n\t&= \n    \\left\\lbrack\n    \\begin{matrix}\n        9 & -30   \\\\\n        {\\color{blue}{4}} \\cdot ({\\color{red}{-3}}) + {\\color{blue}{3}} \\cdot {\\color{red}{2}} & \\ast\n    \\end{matrix}\n    \\right\\rbrack\n\n\t= \\left\\lbrack\n    \\begin{matrix}\n        9 & -30   \\\\\n        -6 & \\ast\n    \\end{matrix}\n    \\right\\rbrack\n}\n$$\n\nTo find $c_{22},$ the entry at the intersection of the 2nd row and 2nd column, we calculate the dot product of the 2nd row of $A$ and the 2nd column of $B\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\eqalign {\n    \\left\\lbrack\n    \\begin{matrix}\n        {1} & {6}  \\\\\n        \\color{blue}{4} & \\color{blue}{3}\n    \\end{matrix}\n    \\right\\rbrack\n\n    \\left\\lbrack\n    \\begin{matrix}\n        -3 & \\color{red}{0}  \\\\\n        2 & \\color{red}{-5}\n    \\end{matrix}\n    \\right\\rbrack\n\n\t&= \n    \\left\\lbrack\n    \\begin{matrix}\n        9 & -30   \\\\\n        -6 & {\\color{blue}{4}} \\cdot {\\color{red}{0}} + {\\color{blue}{3}} \\cdot ({\\color{red}{-5}})\n    \\end{matrix}\n    \\right\\rbrack\n\n\t= \\left\\lbrack\n    \\begin{matrix}\n        9 & -30   \\\\\n        -6 & -15\n    \\end{matrix}\n    \\right\\rbrack\n}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our final answer is\n\n$$\n\\eqalign {\n    \\left\\lbrack\n    \\begin{matrix}\n        {1} & {6}  \\\\\n        4 & 3\n    \\end{matrix}\n    \\right\\rbrack\n\n    \\left\\lbrack\n    \\begin{matrix}\n        -3 & 0  \\\\\n        2 & -5\n    \\end{matrix}\n    \\right\\rbrack\n\n\t= \\left\\lbrack\n    \\begin{matrix}\n        9 & -30   \\\\\n        -6 & -15\n    \\end{matrix}\n    \\right\\rbrack.\n}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139535613 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186060980 "Let's recall the table that relates metric units of length.\n\n| Name | Symbol | Conversion |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Kilometer | $\\textrm{km}$ | $1\\,\\textrm{km} = 1\\,000\\,\\textrm{m}$ |\n| Meter | $\\textrm{m}$ | Base Unit |\n| Centimeter | $\\textrm{cm}$ | $100\\,\\textrm{cm} = 1\\,\\textrm{m}$ |\n| Millimeter | $\\textrm{mm}$ | $1\\,000\\,\\textrm{mm} = 1\\,\\textrm{m}$ |\n\n**Important**: Symbols in the metric system follow a consistent set of **prefix rules**, which we explain below. If you find these difficult, *do not worry*! For now, you just need to remember the conversions shown in the table above. \n\nThe prefix rules are as follows:\n\n- The prefix \"$\\textrm k$\" stands for **kilo**, which means $1\\,000$. Therefore, $1\\,\\textrm{km} = 1\\,000\\,\\textrm{m}.$\n\n- The prefix \"$\\textrm c$\" stands for **centi**, which means $\\dfrac{1}{100}$. Therefore, $1\\,\\textrm{cm} = \\dfrac{1}{100}\\,\\textrm{m}.$ \n\n Notice that if we add $1\\,\\textrm{cm}$ to itself $100$ times, we get exactly $1\\,\\textrm{m}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{100\\text{ times}}{\\overset{⏞}{1\\,\\text{cm}+1\\,\\text{cm}+⋯+1\\,\\text{cm}}} & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{100}\\,\\text{m}+\\frac{1}{100}\\,\\text{m}+⋯+\\frac{1}{100}\\,\\text{m} & = \\\\ 100\\times \\frac{1}{100}\\,\\text{m} & = \\\\ \\frac{100\\times 1}{100}\\,\\text{m} & = \\\\ \\frac{100}{100}\\,\\text{m} & = \\\\ 1\\,\\text{m}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $100\\,\\textrm{cm}$ equals $1\\,\\textrm{m},$ as stated in the table.\n\n- The prefix \"$\\textrm m$\" stands for **milli**, which means $\\dfrac{1}{1\\,000}$. Therefore, $1\\,\\textrm{mm} = \\dfrac{1}{1,000}\\,\\textrm{m}.$ This means that $1\\,000\\,\\textrm{mm}$ equals $1\\,\\textrm{m},$ as shown in the table." #edb/ref 13194139534631 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186060981 "Let's construct a direct proof of the following statement:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{a \\equiv b}_{P(a,b)} \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad \\underbrace{a+2 \\equiv b+2}_{Q(a,b)} \\qquad (\\textrm{mod} \\: 5)\n$$\n\nWe start by assuming the antecedent $P$ is true.\n\n> *Assume that* $a \\equiv b \\: (\\textrm{mod} \\: 5)$.\n\nNext, we state what this means using the definition of congruence.\n\n> *By the definition of modular congruence,*\n> $5 \\mid (a-b) \\qquad\\Leftrightarrow\\qquad a-b = 5k$\n> *for some integer $k$.*\n\nNow, the goal is to show that\n\n$$\n(a+2) \\equiv (b+2) \\quad \\Leftrightarrow\\quad 5 \\mid \\big((a+2) - (b+2)\\big)\n$$\n\nSo, we write down the difference between $(a+2)$ and $(b+2)$ and show that it is a multiple of $5$ using our assumption regarding the difference between $a$ and $b{:}$\n\n> *Hence, we get*\n> $(a + 2) - (b + 2)|= a + 2 - b - 2; = a - b; = 5k$.\n\nSince $(a+2) - (b+2)$ is a multiple of $5$, this shows that $(a+2) \\equiv (b+2)$ modulo $5$. So, our proof is complete, and we write our conclusion.\n\n> *Therefore, according to the definition of congruence, we have*\n> $a+2 \\equiv b+2 \\quad (\\textrm{mod} \\: 5)$.\n\nNow that we've discussed all the details, let's write down our final proof." #edb/ref 13194139533347 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186060986 "In general, we have the following facts:\n\n- The contrapositive is logically equivalent to the original statement:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{A \\Rightarrow B}_{\\textrm{original}} \\equiv \\underbrace{\\neg B \\Rightarrow \\neg A}_{\\textrm{contrapositive}}\n$$\n\n- The inverse and the converse are logically equivalent to each other but not to the original statement:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\neg A \\Rightarrow \\neg B}_{\\textrm{inverse}} \\equiv \\underbrace{B \\Rightarrow A}_{\\textrm{converse}}\n$$\n\nWe can prove these results using a truth table, as shown below:\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\, A \\Rightarrow B \\,$ | $\\, \\neg A \\Rightarrow \\neg B \\,$ | $\\, B \\Rightarrow A \\,$ | $\\, \\neg B \\Rightarrow \\neg A \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ | $\\color{red} \\textrm T$ | $\\color{red} \\textrm T$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm F$ | $\\color{red} \\textrm T$ | $\\color{red} \\textrm T$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm F$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ | $\\color{red} \\textrm F$ | $\\color{red} \\textrm F$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ | $\\color{red} \\textrm T$ | $\\color{red} \\textrm T$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ |\n\nThis can be visualized using the following diagram.\n\n![](images/d9/d946d2a81d46cbde934ebbab54f9b69b514092e02da4f8a3b79768f218929449.png)" #edb/ref 13194139536456 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186060989 "To multiply powers of the same base, we simply *add* the exponents. This is called the **product rule for exponents**.\nFor example, in the expression \n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}3}^4 \\times {\\color{blue}3}^2,\n$$\n\nboth powers have the same base $({\\color{blue}3}).$ Therefore, we can simplify this expression by adding the exponents:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}3}^4 \\times {\\color{blue}3}^2 = {\\color{blue}3}^{4+2} = {\\color{blue}3}^6\n$$\n\n**Note:** The product rule for exponents is really just a quicker alternative to using repeated multiplication, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{4}\\times {3}^{2} & = \\\\ {{3\\times 3\\times 3\\times 3}_{⏟}}_{4\\text{ copies}}\\times {{3\\times 3}_{⏟}}_{2\\text{ copies}} & = \\\\ {{3\\times 3\\times 3\\times 3\\times 3\\times 3}_{⏟}}_{6\\text{ copies}} & = \\\\ {3}^{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139535213 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186060995 "Recall the following division properties of modular arithmetic for\n\n$$\nk\\neq 0{:}\n$$\n\n- The first division property states that\n$ka \\equiv kb \\quad(\\text{mod}\\,kn) \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad a \\equiv b \\quad(\\textrm{mod}\\,n)$.\n\n- The second division property states that\n$\\left(ka\\equiv kb\\:(\\textrm{mod} \\,n)\\right) \\:\\land\\: \\left(\\textrm{gcd}(k,n)=1\\right) \\quad \\Rightarrow\\quad a \\equiv b \\quad(\\textrm{mod}\\, n)$.\n\nLet's prove that the second property holds in the following case:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{3a \\equiv 3b}_{P(a,b)} \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad \\underbrace{a \\equiv b}_{Q(a,b)} \\qquad (\\textrm{mod} \\: 4)\n$$\n\nWe begin our proof by assuming $P$ is true.\n\n> *Assume* $3a \\equiv 3b\\:(\\textrm{mod} \\: 4)$.\n> *Then, by the definition of modular congruence, we have*\n> $3a≡3b(mod4) \\mid ⇔4∣(3a - 3b); ⇔4∣3(a - b)$.\n\nNow, we recall the following divisibility property of integers:\n\n$$\n(c \\mid ab) \\:\\land\\: (\\textrm{gcd}(a,c)=1) \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad (c \\mid b)\n$$\n\nUsing this fact, we have the following:\n\n> *Now, since $3(a-b)$ is divisible by $4$ and $\\textrm{gcd}(4,3)=1$, the difference $(a-b)$ must be divisible by $4$, i.e.,*\n> $4 \\mid (a-b)$.\n\nFinally, we use the definition of modular congruence one more:\n\n> *Therefore, according to the definition of modular congruence, we have*\n> $a \\equiv b \\qquad (\\textrm{mod} \\: 4)$.\n\nAnd we're done.\n\nLet's restate the full proof." #edb/ref 13194139533347 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186061006 "How do we find the determinant of a $3\\times3$ matrix such as\n\n<div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$A=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>\n\nOne way to do this is to take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as shown below:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}7 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix} & ={{(\\,7\\,)}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{11}}\\cdot \\overset{{M}_{11}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix}}}-{{(\\,1\\,)}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{12}}\\cdot \\overset{{M}_{12}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 4 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{vmatrix}}}+{{(-3)}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{13}}\\cdot \\overset{{M}_{13}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{vmatrix}}} \\\\  & =7(-2-0)-1(-3-20)-3(0-10) \\\\  & =-14+23+30 \\\\  & =39\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the above, we have denoted $\\color{blue}M_{1j}$ as the minor of the entry ${\\color{red}a_{1j}}.$\n\nIn general, the determinant of a $3\\times 3$ matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\det(A) = {\\color{red}a_{11}}{\\color{blue}M_{11}} - {\\color{red}a_{12}}{\\color{blue}M_{12}} + {\\color{red}a_{13}}{\\color{blue}M_{13}}.\n$$\n\nThat is, if $\\begin{bmatrix}a & b & c \\\\ d & e & f \\\\ g & h & i\\end{bmatrix}$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}a & b & c \\\\ d & e & f \\\\ g & h & i\\end{vmatrix} & =a\\begin{vmatrix}e & f \\\\ h & i\\end{vmatrix}-b\\begin{vmatrix}d & f \\\\ g & i\\end{vmatrix}+c\\begin{vmatrix}d & e \\\\ g & h\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =a(ei-fh)-b(di-fg)+c(dh-eg).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Important:** Do not forget about the *minus sign* in front of the *second term* in the formula!" #edb/ref 13194139536169 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186065338 "The **power rule for exponents** states that a power raised to another power is equal to the product of the two powers:\n\n$$\n\\left(a^m\\right)^n = a^{mn}\n$$\n\nFor example,\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\left(5^2\\right)^3 &= \\\n\t\t\t\t5^{2 \\cdot 3} &= \\\n\t\t\t\t5^6.\n\t}\n$$\n\nTo see why the power rule for exponents is true, let's take the above example and work it out again, the long way:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\left(5^2\\right)^3 &= \\[5pt]\n\t\\underbrace{5^2\\cdot 5^2\\cdot 5^2}_{3\\text{ times}} &= \\[5pt]\n(5 \\cdot 5) \\cdot (5 \\cdot 5) \\cdot (5 \\cdot 5) &= \\[5pt]\n\t\\underbrace{5\\cdot5\\cdot5\\cdot5\\cdot5\\cdot5}_{6\\text{ times}} &= \\[5pt]\n\t5^6\n}\n$$\n\nThe power rule for exponents is just a shortcut for the process above." #edb/ref 13194139535245 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186065344 "Suppose that we want to solve the quadratic equation\n\n$$\nx^2+5x+6=0\n$$\n\nThe square root method won't work here, since there is a linear term. We can't factor out the variable, either, since there is a constant term. So, what should we do?\n\nOne way to solve this is to factor the entire trinomial on the left-hand side. To do this, we need to find two numbers that\n\n- multiply to give $6$ (the constant term) and\n- add to give $5$ (the linear coefficient).\n\nThese two numbers are $2$ and $3$. So, we can factor the left-hand-side as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} + 5x + 6 &= 0 \\\\\nx^{2} + 2x + 3x_(⏟)_(5x) + 6 &= 0 \\\\\n(x^{2} + 2x) + (3x + 6) &= 0 \\\\\nx(x + 2) + 3(x + 2) &= 0 \\\\\n(x + 3)(x + 2) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the zero product rule, we have\n\n$$\nx+3=0 \\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad x+2=0\n$$\n\nwhich in turn gives\n\n$$\nx=-3 \\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad x=-2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=-3,-2$." #edb/ref 13194139533348 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186065400 "Let's assume that $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_n$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a population with a population mean $\\mu$ and population standard deviation $\\sigma.$ The central limit theorem states that, for sufficiently large $n,$ \n\n$$\n\\overline{X}\\approx N\\!\\left(\\mu,\\frac{\\sigma^2}{n}\\right).\n$$\n\nAn important consequence of the central limit theorem is that if $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_n$ are I.I.D, then\n\n$$\nX =\\sum_{i=1}^n X_i \\approx N(n\\mu, n\\sigma^2).\n$$\n\nIn other words, a sum of I.I.D random variables is approximately normally distributed. \n\n To see why, note that if \n\n$$\n\\overline{X}  = \\dfrac1n\\sum_{i=1}^n X_i \\approx N\\!\\left(\\mu,\\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{n}\\right),\n$$\n\n then the random variable\n\n$$\nX = n\\overline{X} = \\underbrace{\\overline{X} + \\overline{X}  + \\cdots + \\overline{X}}_{n\\,\\textrm{times}}\n$$\n\n is a sum of $n$ (approximately) normally distributed random variables and is therefore normally distributed. Therefore, the mean and variance of $X$ are given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm E[X] = n\\cdot \\mu, \\qquad \\textrm{Var}[X] = n^2\\cdot \\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{n} = n\\sigma^2.\n$$\n\n Writing this in terms of $\\overline{X},$ we have\n\n$$\nn\\overline{X} \\approx N\\!\\left(n\\mu,n\\sigma^2\\right).\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139534610 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186065425 "Subtracting a negative number is the same as adding a positive number. To remember this, just think that whenever you see a minus ($-$) sign followed by a negative ($-$) sign, they combine into a single plus $(+)$ sign, like so:\n![](images/54/54cf3d5630d0f6f43570b1c290019e92d035acaaac13b43ac40b7e3b093f988b.svg)\n\nA minus sign followed by a negative sign can be hard to make out, so it's considered better form to place parentheses around the negative number. For example, $9-(-4)$ is the same thing as $9 + 4.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9\\,\\overset{+}{\\overset{⏞}{-\\,(-}}\\,4) & = \\\\ 9\\,+\\,4 & = \\\\ 13 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, \n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n    \t\t- 16 -(-5) &= - 16 + 5.\n\t}\n$$\n\nTo compute the result of adding $5$ to $-16,$ we start at $-16$ and move $5$ places to the right:\n\n![](images/fa/fa636855c7ec14299ddcb750b21561799f2b76848d52ee492f0a868cd77bccf0.png)\n\n \n\nWhen we do this, we land at $-11$. Therefore, \n\n$$\n-16-(-5)=-16+5=-11.\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139534958 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186065444 "In this lesson, we introduce a method of integration that can be used in cases where integration by substitution fails. The method is known as **integration by parts**. The so-called **by-parts formula** is \n\n$$\n\\int u\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}x}\\,\\textrm{d}x= uv- \\int v\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}\\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nor simply\n\n$$\n\\int uv'\\,\\textrm{d}x= uv- \\int vu'\\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis method is handy for integrating products of functions where one factor can be easily integrated, and the other factor can easily be differentiated. \n\nLet's see how to apply it to evaluate the integral \n\n$$\n\\int xe^{2x} \\textrm{d}x\\,.\n$$\n\nThe idea is to call one of the factors $u,$ the other factor $v',$ and then apply the by-parts formula. We'll choose the following:\n\n$$\n\\int \\underbrace{\\color{blue}x}_{\\large\\color{blue}u}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\color{red}e^{2x}}_{\\large\\color{red}v'} \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nNow, if ${\\color{blue}u} = {\\color{blue}x}$ then we differentiate to get ${\\color{blue}u'} = {\\color{blue}1}.$ And if ${\\color{red}v'} = {\\color{red}e^{2x}}$ then we integrate to get ${\\color{red}v} = {\\color{red}\\dfrac{1}{2}e^{2x}}.$ Note that we don't bother with the constant of integration here.\n\nWe now have everything we need, so we can substitute into the \"by-parts\" formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {{x\\cdot {e}^{2x}}_{⏟}}_{u{v}^{′}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={{x\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2x}}_{⏟}}_{uv}-\\int {{\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2x}\\cdot 1}_{⏟}}_{v{u}^{′}}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x{e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}x{e}^{2x}-\\frac{1}{2}\\int {e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}x{e}^{2x}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2x}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}x{e}^{2x}-\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{2x}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{2x}(2x-1)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we're done! Notice that integration by parts allows us to take a complicated integral $\\displaystyle \\int xe^{2x}\\,\\textrm{d}x$ and replace it with the simpler $\\displaystyle \\int e^{2x}\\,\\textrm{d}x$ plus some additional terms." #edb/ref 13194139534658 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186069920 "Consider the plane region enclosed by the curve $y=\\sqrt{x},$ the curve $y=\\dfrac{1}{2}x,$ and the vertical line $x=2,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/65/651d5d0d9ccafdeb94608e74086136b121c394670afbea669c3e99606f9756aa.png)\n\nWe rotate this region $360^\\circ$ about the $x$-axis to get a solid of revolution, as shown below. How can we find the volume of this solid?\n\n![](images/7a/7a1e4b8d0a27283cad7a0b0e0f2b23f1a45d0e560582a61f84c0f90a4f61e039.png)\n\nTo find the volume of the solid generated when the region bounded by the outer curve $y=f(x),$ the inner curve $y=g(x),$ and the vertical lines $x=a$ and $x=b$ is rotated $360^\\circ$ about the $x$-axis, we use the following formula:\n\n$$\nV = \\pi \\int_{a}^{b} \\Big( \\big[ \\underbrace{f(x)}_\\text{outer} \\big]^2 - \\big[ \\underbrace{g(x)}_\\text{inner} \\big]^2 \\Big) \\:\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis method of computing the volume is called the **washer method** because it involves breaking up the region into many thin washers (one such washer is shown below). \n\n![](images/29/2920f308f15ef81eaff0ba431d0f4eec3ccd9826fbde8f126242b0493f1f8e32.png)\n\nThe cross-sectional area of each washer corresponds to the region between two discs of radius $f(x)$ and $g(x),$ so the area of each washer cross-section is \n\n$$\n\\pi [f(x)]^2 - \\pi [g(x)]^2,\n$$\n\nand this is what we integrate.\n\nIn our case, the outer curve is $f(x)=\\sqrt{x}$ and the inner curve is $g(x) = \\dfrac{1}{2}x,$ so the volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V=\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{(\\sqrt{x})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{2}x)}^{2}\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V=\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(x-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4})\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating this integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi ({\\int }_{0}^{2}x\\,\\text{d}x-\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\pi ({\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}|}_{0}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}|}_{0}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\pi ((\\frac{4}{2}-0)-\\frac{1}{4}(\\frac{8}{3}-0)) \\\\  & =\\pi (2-\\frac{2}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the volume of the solid is $\\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}$ cubic units." #edb/ref 13194139535797 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186069946 "Up to now, we've completed the square on quadratic expressions whose perfect square is already isolated. \n\nIn general, isolating the perfect square in a quadratic expression requires a trick. We'll now learn how this works.\n\nAs an example, let's complete the square on the following expression:\n\n$$\nx^2+10x+{\\color{purple}{15}}\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term ($10$) as a product with ${\\color{red}{2}}$ as a factor:\n\n$$\nx^2+{\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{5}}x+{\\color{purple}{15}}\n$$\n\nSo, the linear coefficient is now decomposed into two factors, $\\color{red}2$ and ${\\color{blue}{5}}$.\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and then *subtract* the square of the second factor ${\\color{blue}{5}}$, writing the constant term ${\\color{purple}{15}}$ last:\n\n$$\nx^2+{\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{5}}x + {\\color{blue}{5}}^2 - {\\color{blue}{5}}^2 + {\\color{purple}{15}}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the first three terms now form a perfect square!\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{x^2+{\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{5}}x + {\\color{blue}{5}}^2} - {\\color{blue}{5}}^2 + {\\color{purple}{15}}\n$$\n\nFactoring the perfect square, we get\n\n$$\n(x+{\\color{blue}{5}})^2 - {\\color{blue}{5}}^2 + {\\color{purple}{15}}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the last two terms as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x+5)^{2}-{5}^{2}+15 &= (x+5)^{2}-25+15 \\\\\n&= (x+5)^{2}-10.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we're done!\n\nWe can easily check that our answer is correct by expanding the parentheses and simplifying:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x+5)^{2}-10 &= {x}^{2}+2\\cdot 5\\cdot x+{5}^{2}-10 \\\\\n&= {x}^{2}+10x+25-10 \\\\\n&= {x}^{2}+10x+15\\,✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139536832 #edb/ref 17592186576411] [#edb/ref 17592186070005 "Suppose that we wish to find the area $\\mathcal{A}$ under the graph of $y=x^2$ between the $x$-values $x=1$ and $x=4,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/99/99cee7824a157e5a08292ebba9e60a5633c7a2f8a5ebb496fc1d5dbe14f858e3.png)\n\nWe can *approximate* the area using, for instance, the area of three rectangles, each with a width equal to $1.$\n\n![](images/8a/8aa7a3f9be8371b1459874cd2cb8c0c4caf1000d76b99d134fb6e806e638c548.png)\n\nSo, we find the area of each rectangle and then add them all together.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈\\overset{\\text{[math]1[/math]st rectangle}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\,\\overset{\\text{height}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(1)}}\\,\\cdot \\,{{1}_{⏟}}_{\\text{width}}\\,]}}+\\overset{\\text{[math]2[/math]nd rectangle}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\,\\overset{\\text{height}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(2)}}\\,\\cdot \\,{{1}_{⏟}}_{\\text{width}}\\,]}}+\\overset{\\text{[math]3[/math]rd rectangle}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\,\\overset{\\text{height}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(3)}}\\,\\cdot \\,{{1}_{⏟}}_{\\text{width}}\\,]}} \\\\  & =[{1}^{2}\\cdot 1]+[{2}^{2}\\cdot 1]+[{3}^{2}\\cdot 1] \\\\  & =1+4+9 \\\\  & =14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we approximated the area using a **left Riemann sum**, which means that the top-*left* corner of each rectangle touches the curve. \n\nBecause the function is *strictly increasing* over the interval $[1,4],$ the rectangles are entirely *below* the function, which means our approximation is an *underestimate* of the actual area." #edb/ref 13194139535868 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186070014 "How can we solve an integral like\n\n$$\n\\int 2x(x^2+1)^3\\,\\textrm{d}x \\,?\n$$\n\nNotice that if we let $u=x^2+1$, then the derivative is $u' = 2x,$ and both $u$ and $u'$ are present in the integrand:\n\n$$\n\\int \\underbrace{2x}_{u'}(\\overbrace{x^2+1}^{u})^3\\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nIntegrals like this can always be solved using substitution. So, let's proceed. If we let ${\\color{red}{u=x^2+1}},$ then differentiating with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = 2x\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\\color{blue}\\textrm{d}u=2x \\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe can now see that the change of variables from $x$ to $u$ is convenient because if we substitute, we get\n\n$$\n\\int {\\color{blue}{2x}}({\\color{red}{x^2+1}})^3\\,{\\color{blue}{\\textrm{d}x}} =  \\int \\color{red}(x^2+1)^{\\color{black}3}\\, \\color{blue}{2x\\,\\textrm{d}x} \\color{black}= \\int {\\color{red}u}^{\\color{black}3}\\, {\\color{blue}{\\,\\textrm{d}u}} .\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve the integral on the right since it's the integral of a power function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {u}^{3}\\,\\,\\text{d}u & =\\frac{{u}^{4}}{4}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the last step, we change the variable back to $x$ by replacing $u=x^2+1.$ So the final result is\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\int 2x(x^2+1)^3\\,\\textrm{d}x \n=\\dfrac{(x^2+1)^4}{4}+C.\n$$\n\n**Note:** After solving an integral using substitution, we should always double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[\\frac{({x}^{2}+1)^{4}}{4}+C] & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[({x}^{2}+1)^{4}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 4({x}^{2}+1)^{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}+1)+0 \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+1)^{3}\\cdot (2x) \\\\  & =2x({x}^{2}+1)^{3}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139533438 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186070017 "Suppose that we want to calculate $\\displaystyle \\int 2x \\cos{(x^2)}\\,\\textrm d x.$\n\nNotice that if we let $u = x^2,$ then we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\underbrace{2x}_{u'} \\cos({\\overbrace{x^2}^u})\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nIn other words, the factor multiplying the trigonometric function is the derivative of the argument! Integrals like this can always be solved using substitution.\n\nSo, let $u=x^2.$ Differentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d} u}{\\textrm{d}x } = 2x \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm d u = 2x\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we now substitute and evaluate, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})\\cdot 2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2})+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139534625 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186074949 "The method of subtracting fractions and whole numbers is very similar to the one we use for addition.\n\nFor example, suppose that we want to find the value of\n\n$$\n2-\\dfrac{5}{\\color{blue}6}\n$$\n\nWe first write $2$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{1}\n$$\n\nThen, we put this fraction over a denominator of $\\color{blue}6$ by multiplying the numerator and denominator by ${\\color{blue}{6}}$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2 \\times {\\color{blue}{6}}}{1\\times{\\color{blue}{6}}} = \\dfrac{12}{6}\n$$\n\nSo now, we have to find:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\dfrac{12}{6}}_{2} - \\dfrac{5}{6}\n$$\n\nTo subtract two fractions with like denominators, we subtract the numerators and keep the denominators the same.\n\n$$\n\\frac{12}{6} - \\frac{5}{6} = \\frac{7}{6}\n$$\n\nSo our final answer is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 7 {6}\n$$\n\nWe can always check our answer using a fraction model:\n\n![](images/e6/e67c582daf0ab40f08c2445b1f0527e4316d61114d12ce1aa56ede6c26f3b3a1.png)" #edb/ref 13194139533350 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186075011 "The formula for the distance between two points in three-dimensional Cartesian space is similar to the one we know for the two-dimensional Cartesian plane, only now we have three coordinates instead of two.\n\nSo, the distance $d$ between two points $A(x_1,y_1,z_1)$ and $B(x_2,y_2,z_2)$ is given by\n\n$$\nd = \\sqrt{(x_2-x_1)^2 + (y_2-y_1)^2 + (z_2-z_1)^2}\n$$\n\nLet's consider, for example, the points $A(2, 1, 3)$ and $B(5, 0, -7)$. Then, $A$ and $B$ have coordinates\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{({\\color{blue}2},{\\color{red}1},3)}_{\\!({\\color{blue}x_1},{\\color{red}y_1},z_1)}, \\qquad \\underbrace{({\\color{blue}5},{\\color{red}0},-7)}_{\\!({\\color{blue}x_2},{\\color{red}y_2},z_2)}\n$$\n\nrespectively. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nd &= \\sqrt{(5 - 2)^{2} + (0 - 1)^{2} + (-7 - 3)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{3^{2} + (-1)^{2} + (-10)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{9 + 1 + 100} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{110}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139533350 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186075057 "Suppose that we want to add a whole number and a fraction. If we want the final answer to be an improper fraction, then we can proceed by turning the whole number into an improper fraction.\n\nFor example, let's find\n\n$$\n2+\\dfrac{1}{\\color{blue}5}\n$$\n\nas an improper fraction.\n\nWe start by writing $2$ as an improper fraction with $1$ in the denominator:\n\n$$\n2 =\\dfrac{2}{1}\n$$\n\nThen, we put this fraction over a denominator of $\\color{blue}5$ by multiplying the numerator and denominator by ${\\color{blue}5}$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2\\times {\\color{blue}{5}}}{1\\times {\\color{blue}{5}}} = \\dfrac{10}{\\color{blue}5}\n$$\n\nNow, we can add the two numbers:\n\n$$\n2 + \\frac{1}{5} = \\frac{10}{5}_(⏟)_(2) + \\frac{1}{5} = \\frac{11}{5}\n$$\n\nAnd we're done!\n\nTo use some math jargon, we put the numbers $2$ and\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1{\\color{blue}5}\n$$\n\nover a **common denominator** of ${\\color{blue}5}$ before adding them.\n\nNotice that this process is very similar to using fraction models.\n\n![](images/cb/cbe49aa64f7d53911592001c5d93e8cde826ac46dabf2ba9c5e9579738ce6ebd.png)\n\nTo add $2$ to\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1{\\color{blue}5}\n$$\n\nusing a model, we split each whole into ${\\color{blue}5}$ equal parts. When we do, we see that there are $11$ shaded parts in total. So we arrive at the same answer:\n\n$$\n2 +\\dfrac{1}{\\color{blue}5} = \\dfrac{11}{{\\color{blue}5}}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139533350 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186079709 "The sum of the first $N$ terms of an arithmetic series with the initial term $a_1$ and the common difference $d$ can be found as\n\n$$\nS_N = \\frac{N}{2}\\big(2a_1+(N-1)d \\big)\n$$\n\nWe'll now prove this result.\n\nFirst, we recall that the $n$th term of the series is given by\n\n$$\na_n = a_1 + (n-1)d\n$$\n\nLet's write down the sum by placing the indices\n\n$$\nn=1,2,3,\\ldots, N\n$$\n\nin ascending order:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS_{N} &= a_{1} + a_{2} + ⋯ + a_{N - 1} + a_{N} \\\\\n&= a_{1} + (a_{1} + d)_(⏟)_(a_{2}) + ⋯ + (a_{1} + (N - 2)d)_(⏟)_(a_{N - 1}) + (a_{1} + (N - 1)d)_(⏟)_(a_{N})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's write down the same sum but place the indices in descending order:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS_{N} &= a_{N} + a_{N - 1} + ⋯ + a_{2} + a_{1} \\\\\n&= (a_{1} + (N - 1)d)_(⏟)_(a_{N}) + (a_{1} + (N - 2)d)_(⏟)_(a_{N - 1}) + ⋯ + (a_{1} + d)_(⏟)_(a_{2}) + a_{1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy adding the two expressions, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}S_{N} &= & a_{1} & + & (a_{1} + d) & + & ⋯ & + & (a_{1} + (N - 1)d) \\\\ S_{N}|= & (a_{1} + (N - 1)d) & + & (a_{1} + (N - 2)d) & + & ⋯ & + & a_{1} \\\\ 2S_{N}|= & (2a_{1} + (N - 1)d) & + & (2a_{1} + (N - 1)d) & + & ⋯ & + & (2a_{1} + (N - 1)d)\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe expression on the right contains $N$ copies of $2a_1+(N-1)d$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n2S_{N} = N \\cdot (2a_{1} + (N - 1)d)⟹S_{N} = \\frac{N}{2}(2a_{1} + (N - 1)d)\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139533351 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186084446 "Given a collection of $n$ objects, there are $n!$ ways to arrange those objects in a sequence.\n\n- For example, suppose we have $n=2$ objects: $A$ and $B.$ Then there are $2!=2 \\cdot 1 = 2$ arrangements:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{A,B}_{1\\text{st way}}, \\quad \\underbrace{B,A}_{2\\text{nd way}}\n$$\n\n- Likewise, suppose we have $n=3$ objects: $A,$ $B,$ and $C.$ Then there are $3!=3 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 1 = 6$ arrangements:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{A,B,C}_{1\\text{st way}},\\quad\n\\underbrace{A,C,B}_{2\\text{nd way}}, \\quad \n\\underbrace{B,A,C}_{3\\text{rd way}}, \\quad \n\\underbrace{B,C,A}_{4\\text{th way}}, \\quad \n\\underbrace{C,A,B}_{5\\text{th way}},\\quad \n\\underbrace{C,B,A}_{6\\text{th way}}\n$$\n\nThis pattern extends to any number of objects." #edb/ref 13194139533438 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186084465 "Here, we'll derive the formula\n\n$$\n\\sum_{n=1}^N n = \\dfrac 1 2 N (N+1)\n$$\n\nFirst, let's represent the sum with the letter $S$. This way, it will be easier to manipulate the sum algebraically.\n\n$$\nS = \\sum_{n=1}^N n\n$$\n\nNext, let's write out $S$ twice. The first time, we'll write the numbers in ascending order, and the second time in descending order:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}S = 1 & + 2 + ⋯ + (N - 1) + N \\\\ S = N & + (N - 1) + ⋯ + 2 + 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, if we add the two sums above, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2S &= (N + 1) + (2 + N - 1) + ⋯ + (N - 1 + 2) + (N + 1) \\\\\n&= (N + 1) + (N + 1) + ⋯ + (N + 1) + (N + 1)_(⏟)_(Ntimes) \\\\\n&= N(N + 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, solving for $S$, we reach the desired result:\n\n$$\nS = \\dfrac 1 2 N (N+1)\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139533352 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186088854 "We can use Euler's formula to construct straightforward proofs of some trigonometric identities.\n\nFor example, let's prove the following:\n\n$$\n\\cos(x+y) = \\cos x\\cos y - \\sin x\\sin y, \\qquad \\sin(x+y) = \\sin x\\cos y + \\cos x\\sin y\n$$\n\nBy the rules of exponents, we have that\n\n$$\ne^{\\textrm i (x+y)} = e^{\\textrm i x} \\cdot e^{\\textrm iy}.\n$$\n\nUsing Euler's formula, this can be written as\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\cos(x+y) + \\textrm i\\sin(x+y)}_{e^{\\textrm i (x+y)} } = \\underbrace{\\left(\\cos x + \\textrm i\\sin x\\right)}_{e^{\\textrm i x}}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\left(\\cos y + \\textrm i\\sin y\\right)}_{e^{\\textrm iy}}.\n$$\n\n \n\nIf we expand the parentheses on the right-hand side and group real and imaginary parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}(x+y)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}(x+y) & =(\\operatorname{cos}x+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}x)\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}y+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}y) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}x\\operatorname{cos}y+\\text{i}\\operatorname{cos}x\\operatorname{sin}y+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{cos}y+{\\text{i}}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{sin}y \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}x\\operatorname{cos}y+\\text{i}\\operatorname{cos}x\\operatorname{sin}y+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{cos}y-\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{sin}y \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}x\\operatorname{cos}y-\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{sin}y+\\text{i}(\\operatorname{cos}x\\operatorname{sin}y+\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{cos}y).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\cos(x+y) + \\textrm i\\sin(x+y) = \\cos x\\cos y - \\sin x\\sin y  + \\textrm i\\left(\\cos x\\sin y + \\sin x\\cos y\\right).\n$$\n\nEquating the real parts of this equation gives\n\n$$\n\\cos(x+y) = \\cos x\\cos y - \\sin x\\sin y\n$$\n\nand equating the imaginary parts gives\n\n$$\n\\sin(x+y) =  \\sin x\\cos y + \\cos x\\sin y\n$$\n\nas required." #edb/ref 13194139533439 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186088893 "Given the scalar $a,$ the **multiplicative inverse** of $a$ is defined as the scalar $a^{-1}$ such that\n\n$$\na \\cdot a^{-1} = a^{-1} \\cdot a = 1.\n$$\n\nFor example, the multiplicative inverse of $2$ is the number $2^{-1} = \\dfrac{1}{2}$ because \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2}\\cdot 2 =  2\\cdot \\dfrac{1}{2} = 1.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, given a square $n \\times n$ matrix $A,$ the **inverse** of $A$ is defined as the $n \\times n$ matrix $A^{-1}$ such that\n\n$$\nA \\cdot A^{-1} = A^{-1} \\cdot A = I_n,\n$$\n\nwhere $I_n$ is the $n\\times n$ identity matrix.\n\nFor example, suppose we're given the following $2\\times 2$ matrix:\n\n<div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$A=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>\n\nThe inverse of the above matrix is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo verify that the above matrix is in fact the inverse of $A,$ first observe that if we multiply $A$ by the above matrix $A^{-1},$ then we get the identity matrix $I_2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{A}{{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{A}^{-1}} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1\\cdot (-2)+(-1)\\cdot 1 & -1\\cdot (-1)+(-1)\\cdot 1 \\\\ 1\\cdot (-2)+2\\cdot 1 & 1\\cdot (-1)+2\\cdot 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={I}_{2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLikewise, if we multiply $A^{-1}$ by $A,$ then we also get the identity matrix $I_2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}{{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{A} & ={I}_{2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe do not yet know how to find the inverse of a matrix. However, we can verify that one matrix is the inverse of the other by carrying out the same procedure as we did here." #edb/ref 13194139536169 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186088991 "The **standard form** equation of a line is \n\n$$\nax+by=c,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $a,b,$ and $c$ are all integers,\n\n- $a \\geq 0,$ and\n\n- $a,b,$ and $c$ have no common factors.\n\nNote that we always write $x$ *before* $y$ on the left-hand side!\n\nLet's see some examples:\n\n- The equation $2x - y= -5$ is in standard form. Indeed, the variables are on the left-hand side, while the constant term is on the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{2x - y}_{\\textrm{variables}} \\quad = \\underbrace{-5\\phantom{y}}_{\\textrm{constant}}\n$$\n\n \nAll coefficients are integers (namely, $2,$ $-1,$ and $-5$) and don't have common factors greater than $1.$ Also, the coefficient of $x$ is non-negative ($2$).\n\n- The equation $-3x + y = 1$ is *not* in standard form because the coefficient of $x$ is negative ($-3$).\n\n- The equation $2x+4y = -10$ is *not* in standard form because the coefficients $(2,4,$ and $-10)$ have a common factor of $2.$\n\n- The equation $\\dfrac12x+\\dfrac13y = 6$ is *not* in standard form because the coefficients of $x$ and $y$ are not integers." #edb/ref 13194139535314 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186088992 "A **quadratic equation** is an equation that can be written in the form\n\n$$\nax^2+bx+c=0\n$$\n\nwhere $a$, $b$, and $c$ are constants and\n\n$$\na \\neq 0\n$$\n\nThe above form is called the **standard form** of a quadratic equation. The left-hand side is a quadratic polynomial, and the right-hand side is zero.\n\nFor example, the equation\n\n$$\n4x^2=5-2x\n$$\n\nis a quadratic equation because it can be rearranged into the standard form of a quadratic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4x^{2} &= 5 - 2x \\\\\n4x^{2} + 2x &= 5 - 2x + 2x \\\\\n4x^{2} + 2x &= 5 \\\\\n4x^{2} + 2x - 5 &= 5 - 5 \\\\\n4x^{2} + 2x - 5 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEach of the terms in a quadratic equation has a particular name:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\color{red}4\\,x^2}_{\\color{red} \\large\\text{quadratic}} \\overbrace{\\color{black}+\\,2\\,x}^{\\color{black} \\large\\text{linear}} \\underbrace{\\color{blue}-\\,5}_{\\color{blue} \\large\\text{constant}} = \\: 0\n$$\n\nSo in our case,\n\n- the **quadratic term** is $4x^2$, and its coefficient is $4$\n- the **linear term** is $2x$, and its coefficient is $2$\n- the **constant term** is $-5$." #edb/ref 13194139533353 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186093377 "Suppose that we have $10$ stamps worth $\\color{blue}3$ cents each and $4$ stamps worth $\\color{blue}5$ cents each.\nWe can compute the total value of our stamps by multiplying the value of each stamp by its quantity and then adding the totals.\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\hspace{.25cm} \\color{blue}3 \\text{cents} \\hspace{.25cm}}_{\\large\\color{blue}\\textrm{value}} \\times \\overbrace{10}^{\\large\\textrm{quantity}} + \\underbrace{\\hspace{.25cm} \\color{blue}5 \\text{cents} \\hspace{.25cm}}_{\\large\\color{blue}\\textrm{value}} \\times \\overbrace{4}^{\\large\\textrm{quantity}} = 50 \\text{cents}\n$$\n\nWe can use this principle to set up equations for problems involving items with monetary values.\n\nFor example, if we have $a$ stamps worth $3$ cents each and $b$ stamps worth $5$ cents each, and the total cost of all the stamps is $80$ cents, then\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\color{blue}3}_{\\large\\color{blue}\\textrm{value}} \\times \\overbrace{a}^{\\large\\textrm{quantity}} + \\underbrace{\\color{blue}5}_{\\large\\color{blue}\\textrm{value}} \\times \\overbrace{b}^{\\large\\textrm{quantity}} = 80\n$$\n\nAlso, if we know that there are $16$ stamps in total, then we have\n\n$$\na+b=16\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can represent this problem as the following system of equations in the unknowns $a$ and $b$:\n\n$$\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}3 a + 5 b = 80 \\\\ a + b = 16\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nThis system can be solved using substitution or elimination." #edb/ref 13194139536831 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [#edb/ref 17592186093381 "In the previous problem, we had the following expression for the trapezoidal rule sum:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} \\approx \\dfrac{ {\\color{red}f(1)}+{\\color{red}f(2)} }{2}\\cdot {\\color{blue}\\Delta x} + \\dfrac{ {\\color{red}f(2)}+ {\\color{red}f(3)} }{2}\\cdot {\\color{blue}\\Delta x} + \\dfrac{ {\\color{red}f(3)}+ {\\color{red}f(4)} }{2}\\cdot {\\color{blue}\\Delta x}\n$$\n\nNotice that we can simplify the above by factoring out $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ and $\\Delta x,$ and also collecting like terms:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} \\approx \\dfrac{1}{2} [ \\overbrace{{\\color{red}f(1)} + 2\\left( {\\color{red}f(2)}+{\\color{red}f(3)}\\right) +{\\color{red}f(4)}}^{\\color{red}\\large\\text{bases}} ] \\underbrace{\\color{blue}\\Delta x}_{\\color{blue}\\large\\text{height}}\n$$\n\nIn general, we can compute the trapezoidal rule sum for a function $y=f(x)$ over an interval $[a,b]$ using the **general formula** as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} \\approx \\dfrac{1}{2}\\cdot [\\overbrace{{\\color{red}f(a)} + 2({\\color{red}f(x_1)}+{\\color{red}f(x_2)}+ \\ldots +{\\color{red}f(x_{n-1})}) +{\\color{red}f(b)} }^{\\color{red}\\large\\text{bases}} ] \\cdot\\!\\! \\underbrace{\\color{blue}\\Delta x}_{\\color{blue}\\large\\text{height}},\n$$\n\nHere, $n$ is the number of trapezoids, and $\\Delta x$ is the step size given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac{b-a}{n}.\n$$\n\n**Note:** If we look carefully at the formula, we realize that the trapezoidal rule is an average of the left and right Riemann sums!" #edb/ref 13194139533442 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186093398 "Suppose now we have a geometric series\n$2 + 4 + 8 + 16 + 32+ \\dots$,\n\nand we want to find the least value $N$ so that the sum of the first $N$ terms exceeds $620$. That is, we want to find the least value of $N$ such that\n$S_N = \\underbrace{2 + 4 + 8 + 16 + \\ldots + a_N}_{ { {N} \\textrm{ terms}}} > 620.$\n\nWe can do this in a similar way to before, as if the sum were *equal to* $620$, but using an inequality in place of an equals sign.\n\nFirst, we determine the common ratio $r$ for this geometric series:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr &= \\frac{a_{2}}{a_{1}} = \\frac{4}{2} = 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe sum of the first $N$ terms of a geometric series can be determined by the formula\n\n$$\nS_N = \\dfrac{a_1 \\left(1-r^N\\right)}{1-r}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $a_1= 2$ and $r =2$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS_{N} &= (2(1 - 2^{N}))/(1 - 2) \\\\\n&= 2(2^{N} - 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf this is to exceed $620$, then the following must be true:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2(2^{N} - 1) & > 620 \\\\ 2^{N} - 1 & > 310 \\\\ 2^{N} & > 311\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we take $\\log$ of both sides of the equation and use the power rule to solve for $N$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\log (2^{N}) & > \\log (311) \\\\ N\\log 2 & > \\log (311) \\\\ N & > (\\log (311))/(\\log 2) \\\\ N & > 8.281…\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSince $N$ must be an integer, we conclude that there need to be at least $9$ terms for the given sum to exceed $620$." #edb/ref 13194139536831 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [#edb/ref 17592186093416 "Now that we've had some practice using the power-reducing formulas, let's see where they come from.\n\nFirst, let\n\n$$\nz = \\cos \\theta + \\textrm{i} \\sin \\theta\n$$\n\nUsing De Moivre's theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nz^{n} &= (\\cos θ + \\textrm{i}\\sin θ)^{n} \\\\\n&= \\cos (nθ) + \\textrm{i}\\sin (nθ)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's simplify the expression for\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{z^n}\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{z^{n}} &= (1)/(\\cos (nθ) + \\textrm{i}\\sin (nθ)) \\\\\n&= (1)/(\\cos (nθ) + \\textrm{i}\\sin (nθ)) \\cdot (\\cos (nθ) - \\textrm{i}\\sin (nθ))/(\\cos (nθ) - \\textrm{i}\\sin (nθ)) \\\\\n&= (\\cos (nθ) - \\textrm{i}\\sin (nθ))/(\\cos^{2} (nθ) + \\sin^{2} (nθ)) \\\\\n&= (\\cos (nθ) - \\textrm{i}\\sin (nθ))/(1) \\\\\n&= \\cos (nθ) - \\textrm{i}\\sin (nθ)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write down the power-reducing formulas. The power-reducing formula for cosine is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(z^{n} + \\frac{1}{z^{n}}) &= [\\cos (nθ) + \\textrm{i}\\sin (nθ)]^(⏞)^(z^{n}) + [\\cos (nθ) - \\textrm{i}\\sin (nθ)]^(⏞)^(\\frac{1}{z^{n}}) \\\\\n&= 2\\cos (nθ)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the power-reducing formula for sine is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(z^{n} - \\frac{1}{z^{n}}) &= [\\cos (nθ) + \\textrm{i}\\sin (nθ)]^(⏞)^(z^{n}) - [\\cos (nθ) - \\textrm{i}\\sin (nθ)]^(⏞)^(\\frac{1}{z^{n}}) \\\\\n&= 2\\textrm{i}\\sin (nθ)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139533354 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186093461 "One particular type of geometric series modeling problem involves bouncing balls. Let's walk though an example of this type of problem.\n\nA ball is dropped from a height of $16$ meters. Then, it bounces to a height of $8$ meters and continues to bounce to heights that follow a geometric sequence. Assuming that the ball's motion is vertical throughout, what is the total distance the ball travels between when it is dropped and when it hits the ground for the fourth time?\n\nLet's start by visualizing how the height of the ball changes with time $t$:\n\n![](images/76/7626cb0067ab591a64fc95fcd19fbc277ed4287bd694e937e395c1ad9618ca72.png)\n\nLet $a_n$ denote the maximum height of the ball between the $(n-1)$th and $n$th bounces. Then, we have\n\n$$\na_1 = 16, \\qquad a_2 = 8, \\qquad \\ldots\n$$\n\nWe're told that $a_n$ is geometric. The common ratio is\n\n$$\nr = \\dfrac{a_2}{a_1} = \\dfrac{8}{16} = \\dfrac12\n$$\n\nWe break down the motion of the ball as follows:\n\n- Before the first bounce, the ball travels $a_1$ meters.\n- Between the $1$st and $2$nd bounce, the ball travels $a_2 + a_2 = 2a_2$ meters.\n- Between the $2$nd and $3$rd bounce, the ball travels $a_3 + a_3 = 2a_3$ meters.\n- Between the $3$rd and $4$th bounce, the ball travels $a_4 + a_4 = 2a_4$ meters.\n\nTherefore, the total distance $d$ traveled between when the ball is dropped and when it hits the ground for the fourth time is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nd &= a_{1} + 2a_{2} + 2a_{3} + 2a_{4} \\\\\n&= 2a_{1} - a_{1}_(⏟)_(= a_{1}) + 2a_{2} + 2a_{3} + 2a_{4} \\\\\n&= 2a_{1} + 2a_{2} + 2a_{3} + 2a_{4} - a_{1} \\\\\n&= 2(a_{1} + a_{2} + a_{3} + a_{4}) - a_{1} \\\\\n&= 2S_{4} - a_{1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $S_4$ is the sum of the first $4$ terms of the geometric sequence.\n\nTo calculate $S_4$, we use the formula\n\n$$\nS_N = a_1\\left(\\dfrac{1-r^N}{1-r} \\right)\n$$\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\na_1 = 16, r = \\dfrac12\n$$\n\nand $N=4$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS_{4} &= 16((1 - (\\frac{1}{2})^{4})/((1 - \\frac{1}{2}))) \\\\\n&= 16 \\cdot \\frac{15}{8} \\\\\n&= 30\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ball travels a total distance of\n\n$$\nd = 2 \\cdot 30 - 16 = 44 \\,\\textrm m\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139533354 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186093486 "Suppose we wish to approximate the area $\\mathcal{A}$ under the curve\n\n$$\ny=1+x^2\n$$\n\nover the interval $[1,3]$.\n\n![](images/51/51961229e4e31dc9cac2e12e90b965898ab8887fc922d5efddbd60a21de9cecf.png)\n\nWe could use the left or right Riemann sum. However, there is a method that usually gives us an even better approximation.\n\nThis method is called the **midpoint Riemann sum**, which means that the curve passes through the top *midpoint* of each rectangle.\n\nLet's approximate the area under the curve using a midpoint Riemann sum with two rectangles, as shown below.\n\n![](images/d0/d0b87f94eafabc820c6e7b60e393448c3135f3fba30f381e09b8c3c7926fb98d.png)\n\nAdding the areas of the two rectangles, we get\n\n$$\nA \\mid \\approx [f(1.5)^(⏞)^(height) \\cdot 1_(⏟)_(width)]^(⏞)^([math]1[/math]st rectangle) + [f(2.5)^(⏞)^(height) \\cdot 1_(⏟)_(width)]^(⏞)^([math]2[/math]nd rectangle); = [(1 + 1.5^{2}) \\cdot 1] + [(1 + 2.5^{2}) \\cdot 1]; = 3.25 + 7.25; = 10.5\n$$\n\nBecause the function is *concave up* over the interval $[1,3]$, the \"missing\" area between the rectangles and the function is greater than the \"extra\" area, which means our approximation is an *underestimate* of the actual area." #edb/ref 13194139533354 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186093487 "The general formula for a midpoint Riemann sum for $y=f(x)$ over the interval $[a,b]$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} \\approx (\\overbrace{ {\\color{red}f(x_{1}^\\ast)} + {\\color{red}f(x_{2}^\\ast)} + \\ldots + {\\color{red}f(x_{n}^\\ast)} }^{\\large{ { {\\color{red}\\text{heights}}}}} ) \\cdot\\!\\! \\underbrace{\\color{blue}\\Delta x}_{\\large{\\textrm{ } { {\\color{blue}\\text{width}}}}} ,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of rectangles, $x_i^\\ast$ is the midpoint of the $i$th subinterval, and the step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta x=\\dfrac{b-a}{n}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139536831 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [#edb/ref 17592186093497 "Let's prove that the following statement is true using mathematical induction:\n\n$n^2+3n > 2n-1$ for all $n\\in\\mathbb N$\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove is the following:\n\n$P(n): \\quad n^2+3n > 2n-1$ for all $n \\in\\mathbb N$\n\nWe start by proving the so-called base case. Since we are required to prove the statement for $n \\geq 1,$ the base case is $P(1).$ So, we substitute $n=1$ into both the left-hand side and right-hand side and show that the left-hand side is greater than the right-hand side:\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove our statement is true for the base case $n=1.$\nFor the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}^{2}+3(1)=4,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand for the right-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(1)-1=1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, since $4 > 1,$ our statement is true for $n=1.$\n\nNext, we state our inductive hypothesis. Here, we assume that the given statement is true for some $n=k\\geq 1.$ \n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=k\\geq 1,$ we have\n\n$$\nk^2+3k > 2k-1.\n$$\n\nWe must show that $P(k)\\Rightarrow P(k+1).$ Let's start by writing down $P(k+1).$ This is the first part of the so-called **inductive step**.\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n(k+1)^2+ 3(k+1) > 2(k+1)-1 = 2k+1 \\qquad\\qquad (\\ast)\n$$\n\nThe idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of $(\\ast)$ *using the inductive hypothesis* and apply algebraic techniques to show that this is greater than the right-hand side. We do this as follows:\n\nWe write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(k+1)^{2}+3(k+1) & ={k}^{2}+2k+1+3k+3 \\\\  & ={k}^{2}+3k+2k+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the inductive hypothesis, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{k}^{2}+3k+2k+4 & ={{({k}^{2}+3k)}_{⏟}}_{>2k-1}+2k+4 \\\\  & >2k-1+2k+4 \\\\  & =(2k+2)+(2k+1) \\\\  & ={{2k+2}_{⏟}}_{>0}+2k+1 \\\\  & >2k+1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nSince we've shown that the left-hand side of $(\\ast)$ is greater than the right-hand side, our proof is complete. So, let's write down our conclusion. \n\nTherefore, by the principle of induction, $n^2+3n > 2n-1$ for all $n \\in \\mathbb N.$" #edb/ref 13194139534203 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186097905 "Sequences involving factorials often occur in advanced math. For that reason, we need to know how to analyze them.\n\nLet's consider the sequence $a_n$ given below.\n\n$$\na_n = \\dfrac {n!}{(n + 1)!},\\qquad n\\geq 1\n$$\n\nDoes this sequence converge or diverge? If it converges, what is its limit?\n\nTo evaluate the limit, note the following:\n\n- The trick is to simplify $a_n$ by writing the *larger* factorial in terms of the smaller one.\n- In this case, $(n+1)$! is the larger factorial, and $n$! is the smaller.\n- So, we need to write $(n+1)$! in terms of $n$!\n\nLet's remind ourselves of the definition of the factorial function:\n\n$$\n{n!} = {\\color{blue}{1\\cdot 2\\cdot 3\\cdots (n-1)\\cdot n}}\n$$\n\nUsing the same definition, we can write $(n+1)$! as\n\n$$\n(n+1)! = \\underbrace{{\\color{blue}{1\\cdot 2\\cdot 3\\cdots (n-1)\\cdot n}}}_{n!} \\cdot (n+1)\n$$\n\nReplacing ${\\color{blue}{1\\cdot 2\\cdot 3\\cdots (n-1)\\cdot n}}$ with $n$! in the above expression, we have\n\n$$\n(n+1)! = n!\\cdot (n+1)\n$$\n\nWe can now use our expression for $(n+1)$! in terms of $n$! to simplify our expression for $a_n$, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle {a}_{n} & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{n !}{( n + 1 ) !}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{n !}{n ! \\cdot  ( n + 1 )}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\cancel{n !}}{\\cancel{n !} \\cdot  ( n + 1 )}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{n + 1}}\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a_n\\to 0$ as $n\\to\\infty$ because the numerator is constant, yet the denominator is a linear polynomial. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{n \\to \\infty} a_n = \\lim\\limits_{n \\to \\infty} \\left(\\dfrac{1}{n+1}\\right) = 0\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $a_n$ is convergent, and it converges to $0$." #edb/ref 13194139536831 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [#edb/ref 17592186097920 "How can we calculate  $\\displaystyle{\\int} \\left(3\\sin^2 x + 3\\cos^2 x\\right ) \\, \\textrm{d}x?$\n\nWe can compute integrals like this using the Pythagorean trigonometric identity, \n\n$$\n\\sin^2 x + \\cos^2 x=1.\n$$\n\nSo, we evaluate our integral as follows: \n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\int \\left(3\\sin^2 x+  3\\cos^2 x\\right ) \\, \\textrm{d}x  &=  \\int 3(\\underbrace{\\sin^2 x +\\cos^2 x }_1 )\\, \\textrm{d}x\\\n\t\t\t\t                                                                &=\\int 3(1) \\, \\textrm{d}x\\\n\t\t\t\t                                                                &=\\int 3  \\, \\textrm{d}x\\\n\t\t\t\t                                                                &=3x+C\n}\n$$\n\nLet's look at some more examples." #edb/ref 13194139533439 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186097927 "Suppose that we want to evaluate the integral\n\n$$\n\\int x\\ln{x}\\, \\textrm{d}x \\,.\n$$\n\nThe method of integration by parts can help us solve this problem. Let's remind ourselves of the \"by-parts\" formula,\n\n$$\n\\int u v' \\textrm{d}x = uv - \\int v u'\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe usually let $u = x,$ but that would leave us with $v' = \\ln(x)$ which is hard to integrate. So instead, we're going to choose our $u$ and $v'$ slightly differently, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ {v}^{′}=x\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow the formula of integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}_{⏟}}_{u}\\cdot {{x}_{⏟}}_{{v}^{′}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,{x}^{2}-\\int \\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-\\frac{1}{2}\\int x\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen we apply integration by parts to integrals containing logarithmic functions, we should *always* identify the logarithmic function with $u,$ not with $v'.$" #edb/ref 13194139534542 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186101922 "Recall that we can approximate a function $f(x)$ around the point $x=a$ using a linear approximation $L(x)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nf(x)\\approx L(x) = f(a) + f'(a)(x-a).\n$$\n\nThis approximates the function $f(x)$ using a straight line. But we can do even better! We can approximate $f(x)$ using a quadratic curve instead.\n\nThe quadratic approximation is called the **second-degree Taylor polynomial** $P_2(x),$ and is given by\n\n$$\nP_2(x) =  \\underbrace{f(a) + f'(a)(x-a)}_{\\text{linear approximation}} + \\underbrace{\\dfrac{1}{2}f''(a)(x-a)^2}_{\\textrm{new quadratic term}}.\n$$\n\nNotice that we added another term to the linear approximation to get the second-degree Taylor polynomial. This new term is called the ** second-degree term** of the Taylor polynomial. \n\nThe *coefficient* of the second-degree term is $\\dfrac 1 2 f''(a).$ We'll discuss why the second-degree term is of this form at the end of the lesson.\n\nIn general, $P_2(x)$ is a more accurate estimate of the function $f(x)$ compared with $L(x),$ provided that $x$ is close to the value of $a.$\n\n**Note:** The phrase \"Taylor polynomial\" refers to a polynomial that's used to approximate a function. The first degree Taylor polynomial is just the linear approximation, meaning $L(x) = P_1(x),$ and the second degree Taylor polynomial is the quadratic approximation. In the future, we will encounter higher-degree approximations as well." #edb/ref 13194139533440 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186101923 "Recall that we can approximate a function $f(x)$ using a second-degree Taylor polynomial. To get this second-degree Taylor polynomial, we add a second term to the linear approximation:\n\n$$\nP_2(x) =  \\underbrace{f(a) + f'(a)(x-a)}_{\\text{linear approximation}} + \\underbrace{\\dfrac{1}{2}f''(a)(x-a)^2}_{\\textrm{new quadratic term}}.\n$$\n\nAs it turns out, we can keep on adding more and more terms, each time getting better and better approximations. The **third-degree Taylor polynomial** approximation to $f(x),$ denoted $P_3(x),$ is given by\n\n$$\nP_3(x) =  \\underbrace{f(a) + f'(a)(x-a) + \\dfrac{1}{2!}f''(a)(x-a)^2}_{\\text{quadratic approximation}} + \\underbrace{\\dfrac{1}{3!}f^{(3)}(a)(x-a)^3}_{\\text{new cubic term}}.\n$$\n\nNote that $f^{(3)}$ represents the third derivative of $f.$ In other words, $f^{(3)} = f'''.$\n\nIn general, $P_3(x)$ is a better approximation to $f(x)$ compared to $P_2(x),$ provided that $x$ is close to $a.$\n\n**Note:** The phrase \"Taylor polynomial\" refers to a polynomial that's used to approximate a function.\n\n- The first-degree Taylor polynomial is the linear approximation.\n\n- The second-degree Taylor polynomial is the quadratic approximation.\n\n- The third-degree Taylor polynomial is the cubic approximation." #edb/ref 13194139533440 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186101956 "To multiply a matrix by a column vector, we multiply each row in the matrix by the column vector using the dot product.\n\nFor example, suppose we would like to multiply the matrix $A$ by the vector $\\mathbf{x}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA&=\n\t\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 6 & 7\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n\\mathbf{x}&=\n\t\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we write the multiplication as follows:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\n\t\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 6 & 7\\end{bmatrix}}_{A}\n\t\\underbrace{\n\t\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}}_{\\mathbf{x}}\n\t=\n\t\\underbrace{\n\t\\begin{bmatrix}\\ast \\\\ \\ast\\end{bmatrix}}_{\\mathbf{y}}\n$$\n\nThe result of this multiplication is another column vector $\\mathbf{y}$ with the same dimensions as $\\mathbf{x}.$\n\nNow, we calculate the entries of the product (shown as $\\ast$'s in the equation above).\n\nTo find $y_1$, the first component of $\\mathbf{y}$, we calculate the dot product of the first row of $A$ and the vector $\\mathbf{x}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA\\mathbf{x} &=\\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{#4169E1}1} & {\\color{#4169E1}3} \\\\ 6 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{{\\color{black}-3}} \\\\ \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{{\\color{black}4}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&=\\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{#4169E1}1}\\cdot (\\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{{\\color{black}-3}})+{\\color{#4169E1}3}\\cdot \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{{\\color{black}4}} \\\\ \\ast\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&=\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ \\ast\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find $y_2$, the second component of $\\mathbf{y}$, we calculate the dot product of the second row of $A$ and the vector $\\mathbf{x}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA\\mathbf{x} &=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ {\\color{#4169E1}6} & {\\color{#4169E1}7}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{{\\color{black}-3}} \\\\ \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{{\\color{black}4}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&=\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ {\\color{#4169E1}6}\\cdot (\\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{{\\color{black}-3}})+{\\color{#4169E1}7}\\cdot \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{{\\color{black}4}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&=\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nA\\mathbf{x}=\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139536582 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [#edb/ref 17592186101959 "When multiplying two matrices in general, we apply the same approach as usual:\n\n- First, we verify that the two matrices are conformable for multiplication.\n- Then, we multiply each row in the first matrix by each column in the second.\n\nFor example, let's find $AB$, where\n\n<div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$A= \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n<div>$B= \\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.$</div>\n</div>\n\nFirst, we verify the conformability for multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\underset{\\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{{\\color{black}2}} \\times \\color{#2E8B57}{\\fbox{3}}}{A} \\underset{\\color{#2E8B57}\\checkmark}{\\cdot} \\underset{{\\color{#2E8B57}\\fbox{3}} \\times \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{{\\color{black}2}}}{B} = \n\t\\underset{\\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{{\\color{black}2}} \\times \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{{\\color{black}2}}}{C}\n$$\n\nSo the matrices are conformable for multiplication, and the product is a $2 \\times 2$ matrix. Let's set up the multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\underbrace{\n    \\begin{bmatrix}\n        -2 & 2 & 1 \\\\\n\t1 & -1 & 3 \n    \\end{bmatrix}}_{A}\n\n\t\\underbrace{\n    \\begin{bmatrix}\n        -5 & 2  \\\\\n\t2 & -2  \\\\\n\t-4 & 1 \n    \\end{bmatrix}}_{B} \\\\ &=\\underbrace{\n    \\begin{bmatrix}\n        \\ast & \\ast  \\\\\n        \\ast & \\ast \n    \\end{bmatrix}\n\t}_{C}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the entries of the product matrix (shown as $\\ast$'s in the equation above).\n\nTo find $c_{11}$, the entry at the intersection of the first row and first column, we calculate the dot product of the first row of $A$ and the first column of $B$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\begin{bmatrix}\n        \\color{#4169E1}-2 & \\color{#4169E1}2 & \\color{#4169E1}1 \\\\\n\t\\color{gray}1 & \\color{gray}-1 & \\color{gray}3 \n    \\end{bmatrix}\n\n    \\begin{bmatrix}\n         \\color{red}-5 & \\color{gray}2  \\\\\n\t\\color{red}2 & \\color{gray}-2  \\\\\n\t\\color{red}-4 & \\color{gray}1\n    \\end{bmatrix} \\\\ &=\\begin{bmatrix}\n        \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{{\\color{black}10}} & \\ast  \\\\\n        \\ast & \\ast\n    \\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{{\\color{black}10}} = ({\\color{#4169E1}{-2}}) \\cdot ({\\color{red}{-5}}) + {\\color{#4169E1}{2}} \\cdot {\\color{red}{2}} + {\\color{#4169E1}{1}} \\cdot ({\\color{red}{-4}})\n$$\n\nTo find $c_{12}$, we calculate the dot product of the first row of $A$ and the second column of $B$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\begin{bmatrix}\n        \\color{#4169E1}-2 & \\color{#4169E1}2 & \\color{#4169E1}1 \\\\\n\t\\color{gray}1 & \\color{gray}-1 & \\color{gray}3 \n    \\end{bmatrix}\n\n    \\begin{bmatrix}\n        \\color{gray}-5 & \\color{red}2  \\\\\n\t\\color{gray}2 & \\color{red}-2  \\\\\n\t\\color{gray}-4 & \\color{red}1\n    \\end{bmatrix} \\\\ &=\\begin{bmatrix}\n        10 & \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{{\\color{black}-7}}  \\\\\n        \\ast & \\ast\n    \\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{{\\color{black}-7}} = ({\\color{#4169E1}{-2}}) \\cdot {\\color{red}{2}} + {\\color{#4169E1}{2}} \\cdot ({\\color{red}{-2}}) + {\\color{#4169E1}{1}} \\cdot {\\color{red}{1}}\n$$\n\nTo find $c_{21}$, we calculate the dot product of the second row of $A$ and the first column of $B$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\begin{bmatrix}\n        \\color{gray}-2 & \\color{gray}2 & \\color{gray}1 \\\\\n\t\\color{#4169E1}1 & \\color{#4169E1}-1 & \\color{#4169E1}3 \n    \\end{bmatrix}\n\n    \\begin{bmatrix}\n         \\color{red}-5 & \\color{gray}2  \\\\\n\t\\color{red}2 & \\color{gray}-2  \\\\\n\t\\color{red}-4 & \\color{gray}1\n    \\end{bmatrix} \\\\ &=\\begin{bmatrix}\n        10 & -7  \\\\\n        \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{{\\color{black}-19}} & \\ast\n    \\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{{\\color{black}-19}} = {\\color{#4169E1}{1}} \\cdot ({\\color{red}{-5}}) + ({\\color{#4169E1}{-1}}) \\cdot {\\color{red}{2}} + {\\color{#4169E1}{3}} \\cdot ({\\color{red}{-4}})\n$$\n\nFinally, to find $c_{22}$, we calculate the dot product of the second row of $A$ and the second column of $B$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\begin{bmatrix}\n        \\color{gray}-2 & \\color{gray}2 & \\color{gray}1 \\\\\n\t\\color{#4169E1}1 & \\color{#4169E1}-1 & \\color{#4169E1}3 \n    \\end{bmatrix}\n\n    \\begin{bmatrix}\n        \\color{gray}-5 & \\color{red}2  \\\\\n\t\\color{gray}2 & \\color{red}-2  \\\\\n\t\\color{gray}-4 & \\color{red}1\n    \\end{bmatrix} \\\\ &=\\begin{bmatrix}\n        10 & -7  \\\\\n        -19 & \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{{\\color{black}7}}\n    \\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{{\\color{black}7}} = {\\color{#4169E1}{1}} \\cdot {\\color{red}{2}} + ({\\color{#4169E1}{-1}}) \\cdot ({\\color{red}{-2}}) + {\\color{#4169E1}{3}} \\cdot {\\color{red}{1}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nAB = \n\t\\begin{bmatrix}10 & -7 \\\\ -19 & 7\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139536598 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [#edb/ref 17592186101969 "To raise a power to another power, we multiply the exponents. This is the **power rule for exponents**. \n\nTo demonstrate, let's simplify the expression $\\left( 3^5\\right)^4$ by applying the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\left( 3^5\\right)^4 = 3^{5\\cdot 4} = 3^{20}\n$$\n\n**Note:** The power rule for exponents is just a quicker alternative to using repeated multiplication, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({3}^{5})}^{4} & = \\\\ {{{3}^{5}\\times {3}^{5}\\times {3}^{5}\\times {3}^{5}}_{⏟}}_{4\\text{ copies}} & = \\\\ {3}^{5+5+5+5} & = \\\\ {3}^{5\\cdot 4} & = \\\\ {3}^{20}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139535218 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186101981 "To compute the quotient of two complex numbers\n\n$$\nz_1 = r_1 \\left[ \\cos(\\theta_1) + \\textrm{i} \\sin(\\theta_1) \\right]\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nz_2 = r_2 \\left[ \\cos(\\theta_2) + \\textrm{i} \\sin(\\theta_2) \\right]\n$$\n\nexpressed in polar form, we have been using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\frac{z_{1}}{z_{2}} = \\frac{r_{1}}{r_{2}}[\\cos (θ_{1} - θ_{2}) + \\textrm{i}\\sin (θ_{1} - θ_{2})]\n$$\n\nTo see why the formula works, let's compute the quotient manually:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{z_{1}}{z_{2}} &= (r_{1}[\\cos (θ_{1}) + \\textrm{i}\\sin (θ_{1})])/(r_{2}[\\cos (θ_{2}) + \\textrm{i}\\sin (θ_{2})]) \\\\\n&= \\frac{r_{1}}{r_{2}} \\cdot (\\cos (θ_{1}) + \\textrm{i}\\sin (θ_{1}))/(\\cos (θ_{2}) + \\textrm{i}\\sin (θ_{2})) \\cdot (\\cos (θ_{2}) - \\textrm{i}\\sin (θ_{2}))/(\\cos (θ_{2}) - \\textrm{i}\\sin (θ_{2})) \\\\\n&= \\frac{r_{1}}{r_{2}} \\cdot ([\\cos (θ_{1})\\cos (θ_{2}) + \\sin (θ_{1})\\sin (θ_{2})]^(⏞)^(\\cos (θ_{1} - θ_{2})) + \\textrm{i}[\\sin (θ_{1})\\cos (θ_{2}) - \\cos (θ_{1})\\sin (θ_{2})]^(⏞)^(\\sin (θ_{1} - θ_{2})))/(\\cos^{2} (θ_{2}) + \\sin^{2} (θ_{2})) \\\\\n&= \\frac{r_{1}}{r_{2}} \\cdot (\\cos (θ_{1} - θ_{2}) + \\textrm{i}\\sin (θ_{1} - θ_{2}))/(1) \\\\\n&= \\frac{r_{1}}{r_{2}}[\\cos (θ_{1} - θ_{2}) + \\textrm{i}\\sin (θ_{1} - θ_{2})]\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, this formula is a consequence of the difference formulas for cosine and sine!\n\nBy working out the product manually, we get the same result, but with a lot more effort and calculations. The formula is the quickest way to go." #edb/ref 13194139533356 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186102001 "Suppose we have a convergent series whose sum equals the finite number $S{:}$\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{S = \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n}\n$$\n\nLet $s_N$ be the $N$th partial sum of this series. Then, we can write the sum $S$ as\n\n$$\nS = s_N + R_N\n$$\n\nwhere $R_N$ is called the **remainder** (or **error**) of $s_N$.\n\nThe remainder $R_N$ is simply the difference between the \"true\" value of the series and its $N$th partial sum:\n\n$$\nR_N = S - s_N\n$$\n\nSince finding the exact sum of an infinite series is often impossible, we often wish to approximate $S$ by $s_N$ and determine upper and lower bounds for the error $R_N$.\n\nNow, suppose we have a function $f(x)$ such that\n\n$$\na_n = f(n),\\qquad n=1,2,3,\\ldots\n$$\n\nIf $f(x)$ satisfies the conditions for the integral test (i.e., it is positive, continuous, decreasing), then we have the following lower and upper bounds for the remainder:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\int_{N+1}^\\infty f(x)\\,\\textrm d x}_{\\textrm{lower bound}} \\leq R_N \\leq \\underbrace{\\int_{N}^\\infty f(x)\\,\\textrm d x}_{\\textrm{upper bound}}\n$$\n\nWe call this condition the **remainder estimate for the integral test**.\n\nWe'll build some intuition behind the remainder estimate at the end of the lesson. But for now, let's consider a concrete example." #edb/ref 13194139533356 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186102002 "Earlier, we determined that the remainder $R_5$ for the series\n\n$$\nS = \\sum_{n = 1}^\\infty \\dfrac 1 {n^2}\n$$\n\nshould satisfy the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\int_{6}^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{x^2} \\,\\textrm d x}_{\\textrm{lower bound}} \\leq R_5 \\leq \\underbrace{\\int_{5}^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{x^2} \\,\\textrm d x}_{\\textrm{upper bound}}\n$$\n\nTo see why this is true, first recall that the function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\dfrac{1}{x^2}, \\qquad x\\geq 1\n$$\n\nsatisfies the conditions for the integral test.\n\nLet's visualize $f(x)$ and the corresponding terms\n\n$$\na_n = \\dfrac{1}{n^2}\n$$\n\nfor\n\n$$\nn\\geq 1\n$$\n\nusing a diagram.\n\n![](images/e0/e05dfba3ddf77605991d30a440fb40bd21f264329264369f3ba6ca283842bc34.png)\n\nWe can represent the sum of our series using the areas of vertical bars with a width of $1$ in the following two ways:\n\n![](images/ac/acff3370099b31f0675fdb31c8670c7ff09e26c0af23f3ad41705db59b8f27b4.png)\n\nNote the following:\n\n- On the left diagram, the *top-left* corner of the $n$th bar coincides with $a_n$.\n- On the right diagram, the *top-right* corner of the $n$th bar coincides with $a_n$.\n\nIn both cases, the partial sum $s_5$ is represented by the combined area of the first $5$ bars, and the remainder $R_5$ is represented by the combined area of the remaining bars.\n\nFinally, consider the following two diagrams.\n\n![](images/a4/a403f48a51f50a57f698ae9e86dbdfdcb1c239f96f1f8f58872a3c3bf24cb2b8.png)\n\nNotice that:\n\n- On the left diagram, the bars corresponding to $R_5$ contain the area below the curve for $x\\geq 6$. This means that\n$\\displaystyle \\int_6^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{x^2} \\, \\textrm{d}x \\leq R_5$.\n- On the right diagram, the area below the curve contains the bars corresponding to $R_5$ for $x\\geq 5$. This means that\n$R_5 \\leq \\displaystyle \\int_5^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{x^2} \\, \\textrm{d}x$.\n\nCombining these two inequalities, we arrive at\n\n$$\n\\int_{6}^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{x^2} \\,\\textrm d x \\leq R_5 \\leq \\int_{5}^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{x^2} \\,\\textrm d x\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139533356 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186102016 "Suppose we have the points $A(-1,0)$ and $B(3,0)$ in the plane. \n\nRecall that the *perpendicular bisector* of the segment $\\overline{AB}$ is the line perpendicular to $\\overline{AB}$ that bisects it.  \n\nThe points $A, B,$ and the perpendicular bisector of $\\overline{AB}$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/52/52fb46efe17119f3299831a8947777b4528d8a677ced46d49e8084d1efab3c1c.png)\n\nThe *perpendicular bisector theorem* tells us that every point on this line is *equidistant* to $A$ and $B.$ In other words, if we pick a point $Q(x,y)$ on the line, then $AQ = BQ$ for every point $Q.$\n\n![](images/05/054d51662ad486b97bbacacc6dc8eac4389788480c2e1a08e9c2769490170527.png)\n\nNow, suppose we pick the point $P(2,3)$ that does not lie on the perpendicular bisector. What point on our line is closest to $P?$\n\n![](images/3c/3cb1c0280c74ba3b347764b493ae438b04c6812c6bdd9498024d3f99860af012.png)\n\nTo answer this, we first create a function $S$ representing the squared distance between the point $P(2,3)$ and a general point $Q(x,y){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x,y) & =(x-2)^{2}+(y-3)^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-4x+4+{y}^{2}-6y+9 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4x-6y+13\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe cannot differentiate this function because it contains two variables. However, we can eliminate one by noting that $Q$ lies on the perpendicular bisector.\n\nThe required point $Q(x,y)$ is equidistant to the points $A(-1,0)$ and $B(3,0).$ So, by writing down $(QA)^2 = (QB)^2,$ we have the following constraint equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{(x-(-1))^{2}+(y-0)^{2}}_{⏟}}_{(QA)^{2}} & ={{(x-3)^{2}+(y-0)^{2}}_{⏟}}_{(QB)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =(x-3)^{2}+{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe make $x$ the subject in the constraint equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =(x-3)^{2}+{y}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2x+1 & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-6x+9 \\\\ \\cancel{{x}^{2}}+2x+1+\\cancel{{y}^{2}} & =\\cancel{{x}^{2}}-6x+9+\\cancel{{y}^{2}} \\\\ 2x+6x & =9-1 \\\\ 8x & =8 \\\\ x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our expression for $S(x,y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(y) & ={1}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4(1)-6y+13 \\\\  & =1+{y}^{2}-4-6y+13 \\\\  & ={y}^{2}-6y+10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the first derivative,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{'}(y) & =2y-6,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solve $S'(y)=0$ to find the stationary point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{'}(y) & =0 \\\\ 2y-6 & =0 \\\\ y & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's verify that this stationary point is a minimum. Taking the second derivative, we find\n\n$$\nS''(3) =2>0.\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the stationary point $y=3$ indeed minimizes the squared distance. \n\nTherefore, the required point is $\\left(1,3\\right).$" #edb/ref 13194139533444 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186102017 "Let's consider the following series:\n\n$$\nS = \\sum_{n = 1}^\\infty \\dfrac 1 {n^2}\n$$\n\nSuppose we approximate $S$ by its $5$th partial sum $s_5$. What are the lower and upper bounds of the remainder $R_5$ in this case?\n\nFirst, notice that the function $f(x)$, defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)= \\dfrac{1}{x^2}\n$$\n\nis positive, continuous, and decreasing for\n\n$$\nx \\geq 1\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ satisfies the conditions for the integral test.\n\nNow, the remainder estimate for the integral test states that\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\int_{6}^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{x^2} \\,\\textrm d x}_{\\textrm{lower bound}} \\leq R_5 \\leq \\underbrace{\\int_{5}^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{x^2} \\,\\textrm d x}_{\\textrm{upper bound}}\n$$\n\nComputing the two improper integrals, we obtain the following bounds on the remainder:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{6} \\leq R_5 \\leq \\dfrac{1}{5}\n$$\n\nLet's check this result by computing the exact value of the remainder in this case.\n\nIt can be shown that\n\n$$\nS = \\sum_{n = 1}^\\infty \\dfrac 1 {n^2} = \\dfrac{\\pi^2}{6} \\approx 1.644\\,934\n$$\n\nrounded to six decimal places.\n\nMoreover, the $5$th partial sum is\n\n$$\ns_5 = \\sum_{n = 1}^5 \\dfrac 1 {n^2} \\approx 1.463\\,611\n$$\n\nTherefore, the true value of the remainder $R_5$ in this case is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nR_{5} &= S - s_{5} \\\\\n&\\approx 1.644934 - 1.463611 \\\\\n&\\approx 0.181\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places. So, we see that $R_5$ indeed lies between\n\n$$\n\\dfrac16\\approx 0.16\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\dfrac15 = 0.25\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139533356 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186102018 "Suppose we are given the graph of the function $y=f(x)$ defined on the interval $[-2,2]$ as shown below. How can we calculate the integral $\\displaystyle \\int_{-2}^{2} f(x)\\: \\textrm{d}x?$\n\n![](images/1e/1e98d2090150d84908fd17b7e86fffef5bb3c085f52e26272cda579c15126cce.png)\n\nFirst, we notice that the graph consists of two parts: the line segment for $x \\in [-2,0]$ and the line segment for $x \\in [0,2].$ Therefore, using the additive property of the definite integral, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\int_{-2}^{2} f(x)\\: \\textrm{d}x = \\underbrace{\\int_{-2}^{0} f(x)\\: \\textrm{d}x}_{A_1} + \\underbrace{\\int_{0}^{2} f(x)\\: \\textrm{d}x}_{A_2}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that the area of the region between the graph and the $x$-axis can be represented using definite integrals. We divide the region between our curve and the $x$-axis as follows:\n\n![](images/3e/3eabe4f363f93b7e4d005c6c1f0beba8e9ff9e74ebae5410930565baa8569e41.png)\n\nThe first integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x \\in [-2,0].$ The corresponding region is a trapezoid, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}(a+b)h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1+2)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe second integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x \\in [0,2].$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}ab \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2} \\\\  & =3+2 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139533440 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186102019 "Suppose the function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on the interval $[0,5].$ The graph of $y=f'(x),$ which consists of two line segments, is shown below. Additionally, we know that ${f(0)=2}.$ How can we find, for example, the value of $f(4)?$\n\n![](images/fe/feb899a5b4728254a153d40194220c5237eb4ef84a537e22c094cbf1639ab3c2.png)\n\nThe fundamental theorem of calculus states that\n\n$$\n\\int_{0}^{4} f'(x) \\: \\textrm{d}x = f(4) - f(0),\n$$\n\nand we know that $f(0)=2,$ so we can find $f(4)$ if we can figure out the value of the integral. \n\nRemember that the integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis. To compute the area under the curve, let's split up the integral into two integrals which represent areas that are easier to compute:\n\n$$\n\\int_{0}^{4} f'(x)\\: \\textrm{d}x = \\underbrace{\\int_{0}^{2} f'(x)\\: \\textrm{d}x}_{A_1} + \\underbrace{\\int_{2}^{4} f'(x)\\: \\textrm{d}x}_{A_2}.\n$$\n\nThe two corresponding areas $A_1$ and $A_2$ shown in the graph below:\n\n![](images/1f/1feed4138e13b671489583ec10658b377e578d50a25a3d6be5a19d8c22d85706.png)\n\nThe $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x \\in [0,2].$ The corresponding region is a trapezoid, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}(a+b)h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (1+2)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x \\in [2,4].$ The corresponding region is a rectangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =ab \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the value of the original integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{4}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2} \\\\  & =3+4 \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the value above and the fact that $f(0)=2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{4}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =f(4)-f(0) \\\\ 7 & =f(4)-2 \\\\ f(4) & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139535455 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186102023 "To divide powers of the same base, we simply *subtract* the exponents. This is known as the **quotient rule for exponents**. For instance, in the expression \n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}3}^5 \\div {\\color{blue}3}^2,\n$$\n\nboth powers have the same base $({\\color{blue}3})$. Therefore, we can simplify this expression by subtracting the exponents:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}3}^5 \\div {\\color{blue}3}^2 = {\\color{blue}3}^{5-2} = {\\color{blue}3}^3.\n$$\n\n**Note:** The quotient rule for exponents is really just a quicker alternative to the following process:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{5}÷{3}^{2} & = \\\\ {3}^{3+2}÷{3}^{2} & = \\\\ {3}^{3}\\times {{{3}^{2}÷{3}^{2}}_{⏟}}_{=1} & = \\\\ {3}^{3}\\times 1 & = \\\\ {3}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139535245 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186102029 "Let's think about how to calculate an integral like $\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac {\\ln x} {x} \\,\\textrm d x.$\n\nNotice that this integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac {\\ln x} {x} \\,\\textrm d x = \\int \\underbrace{\\ln x}_{u} \\cdot\\overbrace{\\dfrac {1} {x}}^{u'} \\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nWe've seen integrals like this before, where the integrand consists of a function $u(x)$ multiplied by its derivative $u'(x).$ Such integrals can be solved by substitution.\n\nSo, we let $u = \\ln x,$ which gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}= \\dfrac 1 x\\quad   \\Longrightarrow \\quad \\dfrac 1 x \\,\\textrm{d}x={\\textrm{d}u}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we can calculate our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n       \\int \\dfrac{\\ln x}{x}\\, \\textrm{d}x \t&=\\int \\ln x\\cdot \\dfrac {1} {x} \\,\\textrm d x \\[5pt]\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t& = \\int u \\, \\textrm{d} u \\[5pt]\n                                                                &=  \\dfrac{1}{2}u^2  +C \\[5pt]   \n                                                                &= \\dfrac{1}{2}\\ln^2 x +C}\n$$\n\nAfter solving an integral using substitution, we should always double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{ln}^{2}⁡x+C) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({ln}^{2}⁡x)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{2}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+0 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139534869 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186102031 "Suppose we have the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = 2y.\n$$\n\nHow can we verify whether a given function is a **solution** of this equation? For example, is $y=e^{2x}$ a solution?\n\nTo check whether a given function $y$ satisfies a differential equation, we calculate the derivative and plug the result into the equation.\n\nFor $y=e^{2x},$ the derivative is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = 2e^{2x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nWe now substitute the above and our expression for $y,$ into the original equation and see if we get a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2y \\\\ {{2{e}^{2x}}_{⏟}}_{{y}^{'}} & =2\\cdot {{{e}^{2x}}_{⏟}}_{y} \\\\ 2{e}^{2x} & =2{e}^{2x}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe get a true statement! Therefore, $y=e^{2x}$ is a solution to the original equation.\n\n**Note:** There are often infinitely many solutions to a given differential equation! We will learn how to solve differential equations like the one shown above in future lessons." #edb/ref 13194139533439 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186107001 "Suppose that the base of a solid is the region enclosed by the line\n\n$$\ny=-2x+2\n$$\n\nand the $x$-axis for\n\n$$\n0 \\leq x \\leq 1\n$$\n\nas shown below. If cross-sections perpendicular to the $x$-axis are semicircles, how do we calculate the volume of the solid?\n\n![](images/04/04e6da85e925bbb49f9d1e1e67fb08ecf932fa9dcda7bd0a376bb16ac13d73d2.png)\n\nA sketch with a few cross-sections is shown in the picture below.\n\n![](images/0a/0af3b8f2977733dcd444507a14876674cdcd6d1fb1792cc523ea332cc56cfb1b.png)\n\nConsider a cross-section at some point $x$, where $x \\in (0,1)$, and its projection onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/90/90972f47117425f9785c65b459afbbc4d82c5171841314768fbca03aeee34fb9.png)\n\nIf we project the rectangular cross-section onto the $xy$-plane, we get the picture below:\n\n![](images/89/892c744de907e9440608a3f00a1b871dcb444458636b814bd3b62a2cf104b873.png)\n\nThen, the base of the cross-section is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nd &= \\begin{vmatrix}y|= & -2x + 2\\end{vmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven the diameter $d$ of a semicircle, we can find the corresponding area using the formula\n\n$$\nA = \\overbrace{\\dfrac{1}{2} \\cdot \\underbrace{\\dfrac{1}{4}\\pi d^2}_{\\textrm{Full circle}}}^{\\textrm{Semicircle}} = \\dfrac{1}{8}\\pi d^2\n$$\n\nSo, the expression for the area of the cross-section is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA(x) &= \\frac{1}{8}πd^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{π}{8} \\mid - 2x + \\left.2\\right|^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{π}{8}(4x^{2} - 8x + 4) \\\\\n&= \\frac{π}{2}(1 - 2x + x^{2})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now calculate the volume of the solid, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV &= \\int_{a}^{b}A(x)dx \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}\\frac{π}{2}(1 - 2x + x^{2})dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{π}{2}\\int_{0}^{1}(1 - 2x + x^{2})dx \\\\\n&= \\left.\\frac{π}{2}(x - x^{2} + \\frac{x^{3}}{3})\\right|_{0}^{1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{π}{2}[(1 - 1 + \\frac{1}{3}) - 0] \\\\\n&= \\frac{π}{2} \\cdot \\frac{1}{3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{π}{6}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139533357 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186107093 "Suppose we have the letters $a,b,c$, and we want to count the number of two-letter permutations that can be formed, where each of the given letters can be repeated.\n\n- We have $3$ choices for the first letter: $a,b$, or $c$.\n- Likewise, we have $3$ choices for the second letter: $a,b$, or $c$.\n\nThe total number of two-letter permutations that can be formed is given by the product\n\n$$\n3 \\times 3 = 3^2 = 9\n$$\n\nTo verify that we are correct, we can list out the $9$ permutations below:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}aa & ab & ac \\\\ ba & bb & bc \\\\ ca & cb & cc\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nIn general, the number of permutations of $k$ items chosen from a set of $n$ with repetition is $n^k$.\n\nTo see why, remember that we have $n$ choices for the first item, $n$ choices for the second item, and so on for all $k$ items. So, by the multiplication principle, the total number of permutations is given by the following product:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{n \\times n \\times \\cdots \\times n}_{\\large k \\textrm{times}} = n^k\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139533357 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186107113 "We've been using the fact that the number of ways to place $r$ identical objects into $n$ categories is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{r}\\displaystyle \\left( \\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{r + n - 1}{r} \\right) = {\\displaystyle \\frac{( r + n - 1 ) !}{r ! ( n - 1 ) !}} .\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nBut where does this formula come from?\n\nTo understand where this formula comes from, it's important to understand one key insight:\n\n> Placing $r$ identical objects into $n$ categories is the same as placing $r$ identical objects around $n-1$ dividers.\n\nFor example, suppose we want to place $r=4$ stars stickers into $n=3$ boxes. Then this is the same as placing the stars around $3-1=2$ dividers. Several arrangements are as follows. Each divider is denoted with a $\"\\mid\"$.\n\n- The first box has $2$ stars, the second box has $1$ star, and the third box has $1$ star:\n$\\underbrace{\\quad \\star \\quad \\star \\quad}_{1\\textrm{st box}} \\mid \\underbrace{\\quad \\star \\quad}_{2\\textrm{nd box}} \\mid \\underbrace{\\quad \\star \\quad}_{3\\textrm{rd box}}$\n- The first box has $1$ star, the second box has $3$ stars, and the third box has no stars:\n$\\underbrace{\\quad \\star \\quad}_{1\\textrm{st box}} \\mid \\underbrace{\\quad \\star \\quad \\star \\quad \\star \\quad}_{2\\textrm{nd box}} \\mid \\underbrace{\\quad \\phantom{\\star} \\quad}_{3\\textrm{rd box}}$\n- The first box has no stars, the second box has no stars, and the third box has all $4$ stars:\n$\\underbrace{\\quad \\phantom{\\star} \\quad}_{1\\textrm{st box}} \\mid \\underbrace{\\quad \\phantom{\\star} \\quad}_{2\\textrm{nd box}} \\mid \\underbrace{\\quad \\star \\quad \\star \\quad \\star \\quad \\star \\quad}_{3\\textrm{rd box}}$\n\nTo count the number of ways to place $4$ stars around $2$ dividers, notice that there are\n\n$$\n4+2=6\n$$\n\nspots that can be filled with either a star or a divider. To illustrate, let's label those $6$ spots in each of the above arrangements.\n\n- The first box has $2$ stars, the second box has $1$ star, and the third box has $1$ star:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}⋆ & ⋆ & ∣ & ⋆ & ∣ & ⋆ \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 & 4 & 5 & 6\\end{bmatrix}$\n- The first box has $1$ star, the second box has $3$ stars, and the third box has no stars:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}⋆ & ∣ & ⋆ & ⋆ & ⋆ & ∣ \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 & 4 & 5 & 6\\end{bmatrix}$\n- The first box has no stars, the second box has no stars, and the third box has all $4$ stars:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}∣ & ∣ & ⋆ & ⋆ & ⋆ & ⋆ \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 & 4 & 5 & 6\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nThe number of ways to place $4$ stars around $2$ dividers is equal to the number of ways to choose $4$ of the $6$ spots (in which to place stars):\n\n$$\n\\binom{6}{4} = \\dfrac{6!}{4! \\, 2!} = 15\n$$\n\nMore generally, the number of ways to place $r$ stars around $n-1$ dividers is equal to the number of ways to choose $r$ of the $r+n-1$ spots (in which to place stars):\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{r}\\displaystyle \\left( \\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{r + n - 1}{r} \\right) = {\\displaystyle \\frac{( r + n - 1 ) !}{r ! ( n - 1 ) !}}\\end{array}}\n$$\n\n**Note:** The dividers are also known as \"bars,\" so this method of deriving the formula is often referred to as the \"stars and bars\" method." #edb/ref 13194139536831 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [#edb/ref 17592186107150 "Suppose that we wish to find the area $\\mathcal{A}$ under the curve $y=x^2$ between the $x$-values $x=1$ and $x=4,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/35/3538fb94de9aac062e7ed65b9496085d478be911e5da2bf8480b260cec70ae4f.png)\n\n \n\nWe can approximate the area using, for instance, the area of three rectangles, each with a width equal to $1.$\n\n![](images/cb/cbb7b5af27aa5ebd3b5bbb51026017454999682dcf7e172411df79278564a5ae.png)\n\nSo, we find the area of each rectangle and then add them all together.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈\\overset{\\text{[math]1[/math]st rectangle}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\,\\overset{\\text{height}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(2)}}\\,\\cdot \\,{{1}_{⏟}}_{\\text{base}}\\,]}}+\\overset{\\text{[math]2[/math]nd rectangle}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\,\\overset{\\text{height}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(3)}}\\,\\cdot \\,{{1}_{⏟}}_{\\text{base}}\\,]}}+\\overset{\\text{[math]3[/math]rd rectangle}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\,\\overset{\\text{height}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(4)}}\\,\\cdot \\,{{1}_{⏟}}_{\\text{base}}\\,]}} \\\\  & =[{2}^{2}\\cdot 1]+[{3}^{2}\\cdot 1]+[{4}^{2}\\cdot 1] \\\\  & =4+9+16 \\\\  & =29\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, we approximated the area using a **right Riemann sum**, which means that the top-*right* corner of each rectangle touches the curve.\n\nBecause the function is *strictly increasing* over the interval $[1,4],$ the rectangles reach *above* the function, which means our approximation is an *overestimate* of the actual area (see the picture above)." #edb/ref 13194139535876 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186107162 "Consider the polygon $ABCD$, where the side $\\overline{AB}$ is extended beyond the vertex $B.$\n\n![](images/fa/fa23c036920e2c506c38f1e7be8a498c0a349ef145e8c444201665345bc648e4.png)\n\nWe call $\\color{blue}\\angle{CBE}$ the **exterior angle** of the vertex $B$.\n\nGiven a polygon's vertex, the interior and exterior angles are supplementary. So, for the vertex $B,$ we have:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\color{YellowOrange}m\\angle{ABC}}_{\\large\\text{Interior}} \\:+\\: \\underbrace{\\color{blue}m\\angle{CBE}}_{\\large\\text{Exterior}} = 180^\\circ\n$$\n\nWe have the following theorem:\n\nThe measures of the exterior angles of any simple polygon sum to $360^\\circ.$\n\nWe'll show why this theorem is true at the end of the lesson.\n\nLet's label the exterior angles of our polygon below:\n\n![](images/ac/acc0ac90f27a78e83b6b14651f61ddfe6a87afa56b6e51a1af57f5e74c31689f.png)\n\nAccording to our theorem, these four angles sum to $360^\\circ.$\n\n$$\na+b+c+d = 360^\\circ\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139535456 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186111815 "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n2qx = 10x,\n$$\n\nwhere $q$ is a constant. If we're given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, how can we find the value of $q?$\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ax = bx{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\underbrace{2q}_{\\large a} \\ x = \\underbrace{10}_{\\large b} \\ x\n$$\n\nIn this case, \n\n$$\na = 2q, \\qquad b = 10.\n$$\n\nWe know that an equation of the form $ax = bx$ has infinitely many solutions whenever $a=b.$ Therefore, the equation has infinitely many solutions if\n\n$$\n2q = 10.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $q,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2q & =10 \\\\ \\frac{2q}{2} & =\\frac{10}{2} \\\\ q & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $2qx = 10x$ has infinitely many solutions if $q = 5.$\n\nWe can check that this answer is correct by substituting $q = 5$ into our original equation. Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2qx & =10x \\\\ 2(5)x & =10x \\\\ 10x & =10x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand simplifying this equation by subtracting $10x$ from both sides yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10x & =10x \\\\ 10x-10x & =10x-10x \\\\ 0 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is true for all values of $x.$\n\n**Note:** The constant $q$ in this problem is sometimes called a **parameter.**" #edb/ref 13194139535291 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186111817 "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n(s - 2)x + t = 6x + 9,\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ and $t$ are constants. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, how can we find the values of $s$ and $t?$\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ax+b = cx+d{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\underbrace{(s-2)}_{\\large a} \\ x + \\underbrace{t}_{\\large b} = \\underbrace{6}_{\\large c} \\ x + \\underbrace{9}_{\\large d}\n$$\n\nWe know that an equation of the form $ax+b = cx+d$ has infinitely many solutions when $a=c$ and $b=d.$\n\nSo, to determine the values of $s$ and $t$ such that the equation\n\n$$\n({\\color{blue}{{s - 2}}})x + {\\color{red}{{t}}} = {\\color{blue}{{6}}}x + {\\color{red}{{9}}}\n$$\n\nhas infinitely many solutions, we **equate the coefficients** of $x,$ and we **equate the constants:**\n\n- Equating the coefficients of $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{{s - 2}}} = {\\color{blue}{{6}}}.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $s,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Equating the constants, we get\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{{t}}} = {\\color{red}{{9}}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, if $s = 8$ and $t=9,$ our original equation has infinitely many solutions." #edb/ref 13194139533438 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186111830 "Suppose that we want to solve the quadratic equation\n\n$$\n2x^2-3x+1=0\n$$\n\nTo do this, we need to factor the trinomial on the left-hand side. We'll need to find two numbers such that\n\n- their product is equal to the product of the quadratic coefficient and the constant (which is $2 \\times 1 = 2$), and\n- their sum is equal to the linear coefficient (which is $-3$).\n\nThese two numbers are $-1$ and $-2$. So, we can factor the left-hand side as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x^{2} - 3x + 1 &= 0 \\\\\n2x^{2}- 1x - 2x_(⏟)_(-3x) + 1 &= 0 \\\\\n(2x^{2} - x) + (-2x + 1) &= 0 \\\\\nx(2x - 1) - 1(2x - 1) &= 0 \\\\\n(x - 1)(2x - 1) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the zero product rule, we have\n\n$$\nx-1=0 \\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad 2x-1=0\n$$\n\nwhich in turn gives\n\n$$\nx=1 \\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad x=\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{1}{2},1\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139533358 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186111836 "When two angles share a common side, they are called **adjacent** angles. When two angles are adjacent, we can add their measures to find the measure of the larger angle. \n\nFor example, consider the diagram below.\n\n![](images/f5/f562f3505de0dd2345b29e2baabaf51a7885cbccb5d95e6201b8bc9aec1b1986.png)\n\nThe angles $\\angle PSR$ and $\\angle RST$ are adjacent because they both have the side $\\overrightarrow{SR}$ in common.\n\nSince the angles $\\angle PSR$ and $\\angle RST$ are adjacent, we can find the measure of the combined angle $\\angle PST$ by adding the two angles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠PST & ={{m∠PSR}_{⏟}}_{\\text{orange angle}}+{{m∠RST}_{⏟}}_{\\text{blue angle}} \\\\  & ={95}^{∘}+{45}^{∘} \\\\  & ={140}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139534679 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186111889 "Let's recall the multinomial theorem:\n\n> *For any positive integer $n$, the terms of $(a+b+c)^n$ can be written as*\n> $\\binom{n}{k_1,\\,k_2,\\,k_3} a^{k_1} b^{k_2} c^{k_3}$,\n> *where $k_1 + k_2 + k_3 = n$ and the general formula for the multinomial coefficient is*\n> $\\binom{n}{k_1,\\,k_2,\\,k_3} = \\dfrac{n!}{k_1!\\, k_2! \\, k_3!}$.\n\nFor example, using the multinomial theorem, we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle ( a + b + c {)}^{2} & \\displaystyle  = \\left( \\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{2 , \\hspace{0.167em} 0 , \\hspace{0.167em} 0} \\right) {a}^{2} {b}^{0} {c}^{0} + \\left( \\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{0 , \\hspace{0.167em} 2 , \\hspace{0.167em} 0} \\right) {a}^{0} {b}^{2} {c}^{0} + \\left( \\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{0 , \\hspace{0.167em} 0 , \\hspace{0.167em} 2} \\right) {a}^{0} {b}^{0} {c}^{2} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\phantom{==} + \\left( \\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{1 , \\hspace{0.167em} 1 , \\hspace{0.167em} 0} \\right) {a}^{1} {b}^{1} {c}^{0} + \\left( \\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{1 , \\hspace{0.167em} 0 , \\hspace{0.167em} 1} \\right) {a}^{1} {b}^{0} {c}^{1} + \\left( \\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2}{0 , \\hspace{0.167em} 1 , \\hspace{0.167em} 1} \\right) {a}^{0} {b}^{1} {c}^{1} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{2 !}{2 ! \\hspace{0.167em} 0 ! \\hspace{0.167em} 0 !}} {a}^{2} + {\\displaystyle \\frac{2 !}{0 ! \\hspace{0.167em} 2 ! \\hspace{0.167em} 0 !}} {b}^{2} + {\\displaystyle \\frac{2 !}{0 ! \\hspace{0.167em} 0 ! \\hspace{0.167em} 2 !}} {c}^{2} + {\\displaystyle \\frac{2 !}{1 ! \\hspace{0.167em} 1 ! \\hspace{0.167em} 0 !}} a b + {\\displaystyle \\frac{2 !}{1 ! \\hspace{0.167em} 0 ! \\hspace{0.167em} 1 !}} a c + {\\displaystyle \\frac{2 !}{0 ! \\hspace{0.167em} 1 ! \\hspace{0.167em} 1 !}} b c \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {a}^{2} + {b}^{2} + {c}^{2} + 2 a b + 2 a c + 2 b c .\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nNotice that we have $6$ terms in the above expansion:\n\n$$\n(a + b + c)^{2} = a^{2} + b^{2} + c^{2} + 2ab + 2ac + 2bc_(⏟)_(6terms)\n$$\n\nIn general, the number of distinct terms in the multinomial expansion with $t$ variables raised to the $n$th power is given by\n$\\binom{n+t-1}{t-1}$.\n\nIn the case of $(a+b+c)^2$, we have a multinomial expansion with $t=3$ variables raised to the $n=2$nd power. Therefore, the number of distinct terms in the expansion of $(a+b+c)^2$ is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle \\left( \\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{2 + 3 - 1}{3 - 1} \\right) & \\displaystyle  = \\left( \\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{2} \\right) = 6 \\hspace{0.167em} .\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nIndeed, this matches up with what we saw in our expansion.\n\nNote that one of the variables can actually be a nonzero constant and the number of terms will still be the same. For example, we could take $c = 1$ above and we'd still get an expansion with $6$ terms:\n\n$$\n(a + b + 1)^{2} = a^{2} + b^{2} + 1 + 2ab + 2a + 2b_(⏟)_(6terms)\n$$\n\nHowever, at most one variable can be a nonzero constant; the rest must be distinct variables." #edb/ref 13194139536831 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [#edb/ref 17592186111899 "We can extend our rule relating sums of adjacent angles to more than two angles.\n\nFor example, let's consider the angles shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/32/3264d0ab1a40b02915a737dad57e9f26820193d9bdc6b42431ec1ab6508cc523.png)\n\nAccording to our rule, the measure of the reflex angle $\\angle QST$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠QST & ={{m∠QSP}_{⏟}}_{\\text{green angle}}+{{m∠PSR}_{⏟}}_{\\text{orange angle}}+{{m∠RST}_{⏟}}_{\\text{blue angle}} \\\\  & ={100}^{∘}+{95}^{∘}+{45}^{∘} \\\\  & ={240}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can also use this rule to determine the size of a smaller angle when we know a larger one. Let's see an example." #edb/ref 13194139534679 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186111918 "Suppose that we want to solve the following quadratic equation:\n\n$$\n(x+1)^2 = 4\n$$\n\nNotice that the left-hand side is a perfect square and the right-hand side is a constant:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{(x+1)^2}_{\\large\\color{blue}\\textrm{perfect square}} = \\overbrace{\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!4\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!}^{\\large\\color{red}\\textrm{constant}} \\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\n$$\n\nIn this case, we can solve the equation using the square root method. First, we take the square root of both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x + 1)^{2} &= 4 \\\\\n\\sqrt{(x + 1)^{2}} &= \\sqrt{4} \\\\\n\\mid x + 1 \\mid &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve the resulting absolute value equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid x + 1 \\mid &= 2 \\\\\nx + 1 &= ± 2 \\\\\nx &= -1 ± 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify the two solutions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= -1 + 2 = 1 \\\\\nx &= -1 - 2 =-3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=-3$ and $x=1$." #edb/ref 13194139533358 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186116437 "To find the limit of a vector function, we compute the limit of each component individually. So, if $\\mathbf f (t) = x(t) \\ \\mathbf {i} + y(t) \\ \\mathbf {j} +z(t) \\ \\mathbf {k}$ is our vector function, then the limit is computed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{t\\to a} \\mathbf f (t) = \\bigg[ \\lim_{t \\to a} x(t) \\bigg] \\mathbf {i} + \\bigg[ \\lim_{t \\to a}y(t) \\bigg] \\mathbf {j} +\\bigg[ \\lim_{t \\to a}z(t) \\bigg] \\mathbf {k}\n$$\n\nThe limit on the left exists *if and only if* all the limits on the right exist too.\n\nFor example, suppose we are given the vector function\n\n$$\n\\mathbf f (t) = \\left( 5+t \\right) \\ \\mathbf {i} +  (t^{2}-1) \\ \\mathbf {j} + \\sin{t} \\ \\mathbf {k},\n$$\n\nand we want to compute the limit $\\lim\\limits_{t \\to 0} \\mathbf f (t).$ To do this, we find the limit of each component function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}f(t) & =[\\,{{{lim}_{t\\to 0}(5+t)}_{⏟}}_{=\\,5}\\,]i+[\\,{{{lim}_{t\\to 0}({t}^{2}-1)\\,}_{⏟}}_{=\\,-1}]j+[\\,{{{lim}_{t\\to 0}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,}_{⏟}}_{=\\,0}]k \\\\  & =5\\text{ }i+(-1)\\text{ }j+0\\text{ }k \\\\  & =⟨5,\\,-1,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139536064 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186116474 "To see why the derivative of $\\sec x$ is $\\sec x \\tan x,$ we start by remembering that\n\n$$\n\\sec x = \\dfrac{1}{\\cos x}.\n$$\n\nIf we now differentiate the above relation using the quotient rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(1)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡x-1\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}{{[\\operatorname{cos}⁡x]}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡x-1\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{{cos}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{cos}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & ={{\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}_{⏟}}_{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}{{\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}_{⏟}}_{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can use the same method to find the derivative of the other two reciprocal trigonometric functions,\n\n$$\n\\csc x = \\dfrac{1}{\\sin x} \\qquad \\text{and} \\qquad \\cot x = \\dfrac{1}{\\tan x}.\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139536456 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186116488 "Powers of matrices are similar to powers of numbers. If $A$ is a matrix, then $A^n$ is $A$ multiplied by itself $n$ times: \n\n$$\nA^n = \\underbrace{A\\:\\cdot\\: A\\:\\cdot\\: \\ldots \\:\\cdot\\: A}_{\\Large{n\\text{ times}}}\n$$\n\nFor $A$ to be conformable with itself, $A$ needs to be a square matrix. So we can only take powers of square matrices.\n\nTo demonstrate, suppose that\n\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n\n\nThen we can calculate $A^2$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2} & =A\\cdot A \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3\\cdot 3+(-1)\\cdot 2 & 3\\cdot (-1)+(-1)\\cdot 1 \\\\ 2\\cdot 3+1\\cdot 2 & 2\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -4 \\\\ 8 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe say that $A^0 = I,$ the identity matrix of the same dimension as $A.$" #edb/ref 13194139536391 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186116531 "Recall the definition of variance:\n\n$$\n\\sigma^2 =  \\dfrac{1}{n} \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (x_i - \\overline{x})^2\n$$\n\nYou may wonder why the variance uses the *squared* difference, $(x_i - \\overline{x})^2,$ instead of just using the non-squared difference $x_i - \\overline{x}.$\n\nIt turns out that if we add up all the non-squared differences $x_i - \\overline{x},$ then the negatives will cancel out the positives, and the result will always come out to zero no matter how spread out or concentrated the distribution is.\n\nFor example, consider the dataset\n\n$$\n-2, \\: -2, \\: 0, \\: 2, \\: 2.\n$$\n\nWe have $\\overline{x}=0,$ and if we compute $\\displaystyle \\dfrac{1}{n} \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (x_i - \\overline{x}),$ we get a result of $0{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x}) & =\\frac{1}{5}[(-2-0)+(-2-0)+(0-0)+(2-0)+(2-0)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[-2-2+0+2+2] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis will happen every time, because the quantity $\\displaystyle \\dfrac{1}{n} \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (x_i - \\overline{x})$ always simplifies to $0{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x}) & ={{\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}}_{⏟}}_{\\overset{―}{x}}-\\frac{1}{n}{{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\overset{―}{x}}_{⏟}}_{n\\overset{―}{x}} \\\\  & =\\overset{―}{x}-\\frac{1}{n}\\cdot n\\overset{―}{x} \\\\  & =\\overset{―}{x}-\\overset{―}{x} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we need to perform some operation to the distances $x_i - \\overline{x}$ to make them non-negative. Squaring the distances, $(x_i - \\overline{x})^2,$ will accomplish this.\n\nYou might also wonder why we square the differences instead of taking the absolute value. The reason is that the sum of absolute values of the distances isn't guaranteed to increase if the data points are more spread out.\n\nFor example, consider the data set\n\n\\[\n-3, \\: -1, \\: 0, \\: 1, \\: 3,\n\\]\n\nwhich is more spread out than the earlier data set.\n\nEven though this data set is more spread out, the sum of absolute values of the distances is the same, which is not what we want. We want the variance to increase whenever the data set becomes more spread out.\n\n\\[\\begin{align*}\n-2, \\: -2, \\: 0, \\: 2, \\: 2 &\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad \\dfrac{1}{5}\\sum\\limits_{i=1}^5 \\left| x_i - \\overline{x} \\right| = \\dfrac{1}{5} \\left( 2+2+0+2+2 \\right) = \\dfrac{8}{5} \\quad \\color{red}\\times \\\\[5pt]\n-3, \\: -1, \\: 0, \\: 1, \\: 3 &\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad \\dfrac{1}{5}\\sum\\limits_{i=1}^5 \\left| x_i - \\overline{x} \\right| = \\dfrac{1}{5} \\left( 3+1+0+1+3 \\right) = \\dfrac{8}{5} \\quad \\color{red}\\times \\\\[5pt]\n\\end{align*}\\]\n\nIf we square the distances, though, we'll get a quantity that increases whenever the data set becomes more spread out.\n\n\\[\\begin{align*}\n-2, \\: -2, \\: 0, \\: 2, \\: 2 &\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad \\dfrac{1}{5}\\sum\\limits_{i=1}^5 \\left( x_i - \\overline{x} \\right)^2 = \\dfrac{1}{5} \\left( 2^2+2^2+0^2+2^2+2^2 \\right) = \\dfrac{16}{5} \\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark \\\\[5pt]\n-3, \\: -1, \\: 0, \\: 1, \\: 3 &\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad \\dfrac{1}{5}\\sum\\limits_{i=1}^5 \\left( x_i - \\overline{x} \\right)^2 = \\dfrac{1}{5} \\left( 3^2+1^2+0^2+1^2+3^2 \\right) = \\dfrac{20}{5} \\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark \\\\[5pt]\n\\end{align*}\\]" #edb/ref 13194139533438 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186116538 "Consider the $3 \\times 4$ matrix $M,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 0 & -5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can subdivide $M$ using horizontal and vertical lines, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 0 & -5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen partitioned in this way, we say that the matrix $M$ consists of four **blocks**, where each block is itself a matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{{M}_{11}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}}} & \\,\\,\\overset{{M}_{12}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}}} \\\\ {{\\begin{bmatrix}4\\, & 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{M}_{21}}\\, & \\,\\,\\,{{\\begin{bmatrix}0\\, & -5\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{M}_{22}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nQuite often, a matrix can be partitioned in various ways. Another partition of $M$ is shown below:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 0 & -5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUnder this partition, we obtain different blocks." #edb/ref 13194139535468 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186121024 "One final important property of the determinant is that it is **linear in each row and column**. For rows, for example, this means \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & det\\begin{bmatrix}⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮ \\\\ {◻}_{1}+{△}_{1} & {◻}_{2}+{△}_{2} & … & {◻}_{n}+{△}_{n} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & \\,\\,=det\\begin{bmatrix}⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮ \\\\ {◻}_{1} & {◻}_{2} & … & {◻}_{n} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮\\end{bmatrix}+det\\begin{bmatrix}⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮ \\\\ {△}_{1} & {△}_{2} & … & {△}_{n} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor instance, it's easy to check that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{-2}{\\overset{⏞}{det\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}} & =det\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0+3 & 4+0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={{det\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{4}+{{det\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{-6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that this property also holds for columns:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det\\begin{bmatrix}⋯ & {◻}_{1}+{△}_{1} & ⋯ \\\\ ⋯ & {◻}_{2}+{△}_{2} & ⋯ \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ ⋯ & {◻}_{n}+{△}_{n} & ⋯\\end{bmatrix}=det\\begin{bmatrix}⋯ & {◻}_{1} & ⋯ \\\\ ⋯ & {◻}_{2} & ⋯ \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ ⋯ & {◻}_{n} & ⋯\\end{bmatrix}+det\\begin{bmatrix}⋯ & {△}_{1} & ⋯ \\\\ ⋯ & {△}_{2} & ⋯ \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ ⋯ & {△}_{n} & ⋯\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor example, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{-2}{\\overset{⏞}{det\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}} & =det\\begin{bmatrix}1+0 & 2 \\\\ 0+3 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={{det\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{4}+{{det\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{-6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139535468 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186121028 "In the same way that we can factor numbers or polynomials, we can factor a matrix by writing it as a product of two or more matrices. In fact, there is one factorization technique that works for *all* matrices, called **LU factorization** (or **LU decomposition**).\n\nLU factorization involves writing a matrix $A$ as a product of two matrices $L$ and $U,$ where\n\n- ![](images/ad/adca162da15cab5989e5726acb495fc12533231c2e38b0392d68388aeb18493b.svg) is a *unit lower triangular matrix* (a lower triangular matrix that has only $1$'s on the main diagonal), and\n\n- ![](images/93/93338df4d1ee1791dc5b1679dbddff34d94d05677c362b1a41dcb925ca83a1dc.svg) is an *upper triangular matrix*.\n\nFor example, we can write the matrix ![](images/83/835b8ad372998cbb35be4c1998d236fe96f1d1b03ee1ad7062bdbbb77e254bf6.svg) in LU factored form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 6 & -6\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nYou may be wondering how we arrived at this factorization. Shortly, we'll discuss an algorithm that explains how to compute $L$ and $U$ for any $2\\times2$ matrix $A.$ But for now, let's focus on recognizing LU factored forms." #edb/ref 13194139536001 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186121029 "Recall that LU factorization involves writing a matrix $A$ as a product of two matrices $L$ and $U,$ where\n\n- ![](images/2e/2e6c0b6fdaf90e3a3e23ed91ffe09cb360bb39325b9a630f1b7219b3188a25db.svg) is a *unit lower triangular matrix* (a lower triangular matrix that has only $1$'s on the main diagonal), and\n\n- ![](images/0c/0c5d7337e47a53dfa5bdce8c6f4a96bb1e52ef6054349011a6cc38f29701cc61.svg) is an *upper triangular matrix*.\n\nFor example, suppose we're given the matrix ![](images/22/22dba4fe66ac5478f5d81a68342a01809a360de8e722e809998655e7740bafe8.svg) To perform LU factorization, we need to write $A$ as \n\n![](images/d4/d4c92b2e8252619f9a1511d166be884b9a2bdb4311eea8e408e8fe75eed6f36c.svg)\n\nAs usual, to find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we can reduce the matrix $A$ to *echelon form* while simultaneously performing the same elementary row operations on the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 7\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{1} & ⇔ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]=U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since $E_1A=U,$ we can invert $E_1$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Caution:** In order to get a unit lower triangular matrix at the end, we have the following restriction while reducing a matrix $A$ to its *echelon form*:\n\n- we can only add multiples of higher rows to lower rows ($R_j:=R_j+k R_i$ where $i < j$), and\n\n- the other elementary row operations (swapping or multiplying a row by a number) are not allowed." #edb/ref 13194139536011 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186121033 "In general, if we have two vectors $\\mathbf{u},\\mathbf{v}\\in\\mathbb{R}^n$ and a scalar $\\lambda\\in\\mathbb{R},$ then our vector operations are defined as follows:\n\n1\\. The **sum/difference** of the two vectors is defined as\n  ![](images/fc/fc78b2f4e9262108a217e57fec67e096b8b8b18e1486bec8e51df60b8afdfdfa.svg)\n\n2\\. **Scalar multiplication** of a vector is defined as\n  ![](images/e7/e78025f3481423365b2e924b6f8ba4e59f41e0c644ced7d2263238b219cba27a.svg)\n\n3\\. The **dot product** of two vectors is defined as\n\n  $$\n  \\quad \\mathbf{u}\\cdot\\mathbf{v} = \\underbrace{u_1 \\cdot v_1 + u_2 \\cdot v_2 + \\cdots + u_n \\cdot v_n}_{\\Large{n} \\,\\textrm{-terms}} = \\sum_{i=1}^n u_i v_i.\n  $$" #edb/ref 13194139536032 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186121034 "Consider a vector $\\mathbf{u}$ which is given in terms of the vectors $\\mathbf{v_1}$ and $\\mathbf{v_2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}3}{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v_1}}+({\\color{red}-2}){\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v_2}} = \\mathbf{u}\n$$\n\nHere, we say that $\\mathbf{u}$ is a **linear combination** of the vectors ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v_1}}$ and ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v_2}}$ with coefficients ${\\color{red}3}$ and ${\\color{red}-2}$ respectively. \n\nIn general, a linear combination of $n$ vectors $\\mathbf{v_1},\\mathbf{v_2},\\dots,\\mathbf{v_n}$ is the *weighted sum*\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}x_1}\\mathbf{\\color{blue}v_1}+{\\color{red}x_2}\\mathbf{\\color{blue}v_2}+\\cdots + {\\color{red}x_n}\\mathbf{\\color{blue}v_n} = \\mathbf{u},\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\color{red}x_1},{\\color{red}x_2},\\dots,{\\color{red}x_n}\\in\\mathbb{R}.$ We say that $\\mathbf{u}$ is a linear combination of the vectors $\\mathbf{\\color{blue}v_1},\\mathbf{\\color{blue}v_2},\\dots,\\mathbf{\\color{blue}v_n}$ with coefficients (or *weights*) ${\\color{red}x_1},{\\color{red}x_2},\\dots,{\\color{red}x_n},$ respectively.\n\nFor instance, \n\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}2} \\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{\\large\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_1} + {\\color{red}0} \\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}}_{\\large\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2} + {\\color{red}\\dfrac{1}{2}} \\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{\\large\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_3}\n$$\n\n\nis the linear combination of vectors $\\mathbf{\\color{blue}v_1},\\mathbf{\\color{blue}v_2},\\mathbf{\\color{blue}v_3}$ with coefficients ${\\color{red}2}, {\\color{red}0}, {\\color{red}\\dfrac{1}{2}},$ respectively." #edb/ref 13194139536391 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186121037 "The **column space** of a matrix $A$ is denoted $\\text{Col}(A)$ and is the span of the columns of $A$ (i.e., the set of all possible linear combinations of columns of $A$). For example, consider the matrix\n\n![](images/6c/6c03ab3dfd7b75c9a89defe102f33946da200fb88d26139e54c889a90467068a.svg)\nwhere we denote its columns by $\\color{red}\\mathbf{a}_1$ and ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_2},$ respectively. The column space of this matrix is given by\n![](images/5f/5faad23bc77f821d42223beaca06364e38c7f0ba8a4869cb138e0e923e90b8f7.svg)\n\nNow, suppose we would like to determine whether the vector ![](images/45/45349edee356334c54ad85a6d60757a6a7833a392962086815a2436446e323dc.svg) lies in $\\textrm{Col}(A).$ In other words, we need to check if $\\mathbf{b} \\in \\textrm{Span}\\{ {\\color{red}\\mathbf{a}_1}, {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_2} \\}.$ So, we need to determine whether $\\mathbf{b}$ can be written as some linear combination of $\\color{red}\\mathbf{a}_1$ and ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_2}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the equation above can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} & {a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =b \\\\ {{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{A}{{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{x}} & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{b}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation is equivalent to $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}.$ To solve the equation, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[A \\,|\\, \\mathbf{b}]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[A\\,|\\,b] & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1 & -3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -7 & -7\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2} &=2 \\\\ -7{x}_{2} &=-7.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get $x_2=1.$ Substituting into the first equation, we get $x_1=-1.$\n\nSince we have a solution, the equation $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ is consistent, which implies that $\\mathbf{b} \\in \\textrm{Col}(A).$\n\nMoreover, there is exactly one solution, so we have exactly one way to write $\\mathbf{b}$ as a linear combination of $\\color{red}\\mathbf{a}_1$ and ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_2}\\mathbin{:}$\n![](images/a3/a3965a53b3b3cfda2e11c4a68ba882631dc2763f32de67de30b4751aca76f2e5.svg)" #edb/ref 13194139535661 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186121067 "Let ![](images/ff/ff7b3f4bca1676cbe3fb78ae1c2a47d8fd0bc71dd98dee4c7b6fea4cc94ec7bd.svg) To perform LU factorization, we need to write $A$ as \n\n![](images/3f/3f74fdde04340f6a4dbd86962f5a31f8af22b5fb8053c66fa3e8be41629b12a1.svg)\n\nTo find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 6 & -6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since $E_1A=U,$ we can invert $E_1$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Caution:** To get a unit lower triangular matrix at the end, we have the following restriction while reducing a matrix $A$ to row echelon form:\n\n- we can only add a multiple of the $1$st row to the $2$nd row ($R_2:=R_2+k R_1$), and\n\n- the other elementary row operations (swapping or multiplying a row by a number) are not allowed." #edb/ref 13194139536001 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186125760 "The set of vectors $\\{ \\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2, \\ldots, \\mathbf{v}_n \\}$ in an abstract vector space $V$ is **linearly dependent** if there exist real scalars $x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n$ not all zero such that \n\n$$\nx_1\\mathbf{v}_1+x_2\\mathbf{v}_2+\\ldots+x_n\\mathbf{v}_n=\\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\nOtherwise, if $x_1 = x_2 = \\cdots = x_n = 0$ is the only solution, the set is **linearly independent**.\n\nLet's consider some examples.\n\n- First, let's consider the vector space $\\mathbb{R}[t]$ of all polynomials in variable $t,$ along with the set\n\n$$\nS = \\big\\{ \\underbrace{1}_{\\color{blue}p_1(t)}, \\: \\underbrace{t}_{\\color{blue}p_2(t)}, \\: \\underbrace{t^2}_{\\color{blue}p_3(t)} \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nTo determine whether $S$ is linearly dependent in $\\mathbb{R}[t],$ we must solve the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{p}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}{p}_{2}(t)+{x}_{3}{p}_{3}(t) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given expressions for $p_1(t),p_2(t)$ and $p_3(t)$ into the above equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}\\cdot 1+{x}_{2}\\cdot t+{x}_{3}\\cdot {t}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}_{1}+{x}_{2}t+{x}_{3}{t}^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final equation is true for every $t$ if and only if \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} &=0 \\\\ {x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ {x}_{3} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSince $x_1 = x_2 = x_3 = 0$ is the only solution, $S$ is linearly independent in $\\mathbb R[t].$\n\n- Let's consider the vector space $\\mathbb R[t]$ once more, along with the set \n\n$$\nT = \\big\\{ \\underbrace{t^2+1}_{\\color{red}q_1(t)}, \\: \\underbrace{-2t^2-2}_{\\color{red}q_2(t)} \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nTo determine whether $T$ is linearly dependent in $\\mathbb{R}[t],$ we must solve the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{q}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}{q}_{2}(t) & =0 \\\\ {x}_{1}({t}^{2}+1)+{x}_{2}(-2{t}^{2}-2) & =0 \\\\ ({x}_{1}-2{x}_{2})+({x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}){t}^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final equation is true for every $t$ if and only if \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}-2{x}_{2} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThis system has the nonzero solution $(x_1,x_2)=(2,1).$ As a result, we get\n\n$$\n2 \\cdot {\\color{red}q_1(t)} + 1 \\cdot {\\color{red}q_2(t)} = 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the set $\\big\\{ q_1(t), q_2(t) \\big\\}$ is linearly dependent in $\\mathbb R[t].$" #edb/ref 13194139535661 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186125762 "Given a vector space $V$, any subset $H$ of $V,$ which is itself a vector space, is called a **subspace** of $V$. \n\nFortunately, we do not need to check all vector space axioms to determine whether a subset of some vector space is a subspace. Instead, we only need to check the **subspace criterion**, which states the following:\n\n*A subset $H$ of a vector space $V$ is a subspace of $V$ if and only if $H$ is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication. That is to say, both of the following conditions hold:*\n\n- *If $\\mathbf{u}$ and $\\mathbf{v}$ lie in $H$, then their sum $\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v}$ also lies in $H$. Formally,*\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u},\\mathbf{v} \\in H \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad  \\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v} \\in H.\n$$\n\n- *If $\\mathbf{u}$ lies in $H$, then $k \\cdot \\mathbf{u}$ also lies in $H$ for any scalar  $k$. Formally,* \n\n$$\nk \\in \\mathbb{R}, \\,\\mathbf{u} \\in H \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad  k\\cdot\\mathbf{u} \\in H.\n$$\n\nFor example, consider the vector space $\\mathbb{R}_3[t]$ containing all polynomials of degree at most $3,$ along with the subset \n\n$$\nA = \\{ at^2 \\:|\\: a \\in \\mathbb{R} \\}.\n$$\n\nIs $A$ a subspace? Let's check the two subspace criterion conditions: \n\n- Suppose $p_1(t)=a_1 t^2$ and $p_2(t)=a_2 t^2$ are two polynomials from $A$. Summing the two polynomials, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{p}_{1}(t)+{p}_{2}(t) & =({a}_{1}{t}^{2})+({a}_{2}{t}^{2}) \\\\  & ={{({a}_{1}+{a}_{2})}_{⏟}}_{a\\in R}{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nThus, $p_1(t)+p_2(t)$ is a polynomial of the form $at^2,$ which belongs to $A$.\n\n- If $p_1(t)=a_1 t^2 \\in A$ and $k \\in \\mathbb{R},$ then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k{p}_{1}(t) & =k({a}_{1}{t}^{2}) \\\\  & ={{(k{a}_{1})}_{⏟}}_{a\\in R}{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nThus, $kp_1(t)$ is a polynomial of the form $at^2,$ which belongs to $A$.\n\nSince both conditions hold, we conclude that $A$ is a subspace $\\mathbb{R}_3[t].$" #edb/ref 13194139534975 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186125768 "Suppose that $\\mathbf x \\in V$ for some vector space $V,$ and that $\\mathcal B$ and $\\mathcal C$ are two bases of $V.$ \n\nTo find the coordinates of $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to $\\mathcal{C}$ given its coordinates relative to $\\mathcal B,$ we can use the **change-of-basis formula**\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{C}={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [\\,x\\,]_{B},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we recall that\n\n- $[\\,\\mathbf x\\,]_{\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}}$ and $[\\,\\mathbf x\\,]_{\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}}$ are the coordinates of $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to the bases $\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}$ and ${\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}},$ respectively, and\n\n- $P_{\\small{\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}}\\to{\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}}}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}$ to ${\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}},$ whose columns are the coordinates of vectors of the basis $\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}$ relative to the basis ${\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}}.$\n\nAs an example, suppose we are given two bases $\\mathcal{B}=\\left\\{ \\mathbf{b}_1, \\mathbf{b}_2 \\right\\}$ and $\\mathcal{C}=\\left\\{ \\mathbf{c}_1, \\mathbf{c}_2 \\right\\}$ of a vector space $V,$ and we already know that the change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\mathcal{C}$ is given by\n![](images/d9/d9388f46f84cc0c6b1d7e5cf43ee973085366ae4f44cfe74515c59bfc1994662.svg)\n\nLet $\\mathbf{v} \\in V$ and ![](images/f9/f99f37621c20057077eb7c6e23690a45159cfe1b665c6b88b4a604f8687728bf.svg) Then we can easily find $[\\,\\mathbf{v}\\,]_{\\mathcal{C}},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,v\\,]_{C} & =\\overset{{P}_{B\\to C}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot \\overset{[v]_{B}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139535471 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186125781 "Every linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ that acts on 2D-vectors has a $2 \\times 2$ matrix $T$ associated with it. The matrix is called the **standard matrix** of the linear transformation. \nFor any vector $\\mathbf{v},$ the standard matrix $T$ of a linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ must have the property\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{v}) = T \\cdot \\mathbf{v}\n$$\n\nwhere $T \\cdot \\mathbf{v}$ is the matrix product of the standard matrix $T$ and the vector $\\mathbf{v}.$\n\nTo illustrate, consider the following linear transformation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe standard matrix for the linear transformation $\\mathbf{S}$ is <div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$S=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 7 \\\\ 1 & -5\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div> since it has the following property:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{S}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 7 \\\\ 1 & -5\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3\\cdot x+7\\cdot y \\\\ 1\\cdot x+(-5)\\cdot y\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3x+7y \\\\ x-5y\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =S\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice here that $\\mathbf{S}$ (using a **bold** letter) denotes the transformation (function) itself, while $S$ (big *italic* letter) is a $2 \\times 2$ matrix that corresponds to the linear transformation.\n\nIn general, the standard matrix of the linear transformation $\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe can learn a lot about linear transformations by looking at their standard matrices." #edb/ref 13194139536169 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186125796 "What conclusions can we make regarding the characteristic polynomial of the general $2\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ given by\n\n![](images/8e/8e26937ef1981804a548e92a4041bd8672cc75c7c5cc6efa0c0c2694c0736791.svg)\n\nFirst, let's find the characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\left|\\begin{aligned}a-\\lambda  & b \\\\ c & d-\\lambda \\end{aligned}\\right| & =0 \\\\ (a-\\lambda )(d-\\lambda )-bc & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-(a+d)\\lambda +(ad-bc) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the constant term is the determinant of the matrix: \n\n$$\n\\det(A) = ad-bc\n$$\n\n \n\nThe coefficient of $\\lambda$ is the sum of the diagonal entries of $A$ taken with a negative sign. The sum of the diagonal entries of a matrix $A$ is called the **trace** of $A,$ and we denote it as \n\n$$\n\\mathrm{tr}(A) = a+d.\n$$\n\nSo, for any $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$, the characteristic polynomial is\n\n$$\np(\\lambda) = \\lambda^2 -\\mathrm{tr}(A)\\lambda + \\det(A).\n$$\n\n**Note:** This result can be extended to the general case of $n \\times n$ matrices. If $A$ is an $n \\times n$ matrix, then its characteristic polynomial takes the form\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(\\lambda ) & =det(A-\\lambda I) \\\\  & =(-1)^{n}{\\lambda }^{n}+{c}_{n-1}{\\lambda }^{n-1}+⋯+{c}_{1}\\lambda +{c}_{0} \\\\  & =(-1)^{n}{\\lambda }^{n}+{{(-1)^{n-1}\\text{tr}(A)}_{⏟}}_{{c}_{n-1}}{\\lambda }^{n-1}+⋯+{c}_{1}\\lambda +{{det(A)}_{⏟}}_{{c}_{0}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{n-1}=(-1)^{n-1}\\text{tr}(A)\\,\\text{and}\\,{c}_{0}=det(A).\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139534636 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186130340 "To raise the product of two numbers to a power, we can raise each number to that power, and then multiply. This is the **power of product rule for exponents**. \n\nTo demonstrate, let's simplify the expression $(5 \\times 3)^4$ by applying the power of product rule:\n\n$$\n(5\\times 3)^4=5^4\\times 3^4\n$$\n\n**Note:** The power of product rule for exponents is really just a quicker alternative to using repeated multiplication, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(5\\times 3)^{4} & = \\\\ {{(5\\times 3)\\times (5\\times 3)\\times (5\\times 3)\\times (5\\times 3)}_{⏟}}_{4\\text{ copies}} & = \\\\ {{(5\\times 5\\times 5\\times 5)}_{⏟}}_{4\\text{ copies}}\\times {{(3\\times 3\\times 3\\times 3)}_{⏟}}_{4\\text{ copies}} & = \\\\ {5}^{4}\\times {3}^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139535185 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186130378 "Double summations satisfy the following properties:\n\n**The Constant Multiple Rule** \n\nWe can factor out a constant multiple from a double summation:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^m\\sum_{j=1}^n k a_{ij} = k\\sum_{i=1}^m\\sum_{j=1}^n a_{ij}\n$$\n\n**The Sum Rule** \n\nWe can distribute a double summation over a sum of terms.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^m\\sum_{j=1}^n (a_{ij} + b_{ij}) = \\sum_{i=1}^m\\sum_{j=1}^n a_{ij} + \\sum_{i=1}^m\\sum_{j=1}^n b_{ij}\n$$\n\n**The Double Sum of Units**\n\nRecall that for single summations, we have\n\n$$\n\\sum_{j=1}^{n} 1 = \\underbrace{1+1+\\cdots+1}_{n\\textrm{ times}}  = n.\n$$\n\nAnalogously for double summations, we have that\n\n$$\n\\sum_{i=1}^m\\sum_{j=1}^n 1 = mn.\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139533440 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186130459 "Consider a plane lamina that occupies the region $D \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^2$ of mass $M$ where the center of mass is at $\\left(\\bar{x},\\bar{y}\\right).$\n\n![](images/4c/4c2c7e3525dfe8aea3cce69a7332ccf5ca82ddfe37657071ddef35650bcea383.png)\n\nWe know that the lamina has some moment of inertia $I_M$ about the line $l_M$ that passes through the center of mass and is perpendicular to the lamina.\n\n![](images/96/9638e9d47ef3c9b2fab47f39cf70e2ba07863c6e949755c8378e5737caa0a350.png)\n\nNow, if $l$ is a line parallel to $l_M,$ then the lamina also has moment of inertia about $l,$ say $I_l.$\n\n![](images/06/06819ca45bd176ff9d36b07c73574bc9f2911a7b0b96b5b916ba76f8606d2f60.png)\n\nGiven the value of $I_M,$ we can find $I_l$ using the **parallel axis theorem**, which states that\n\n$$\nI_l = I_M + d^2M,\n$$\n\nwhere $d$ is the distance between the lines.\n\nThe term $d^2M$ is the moment of inertia about $l$ of the lamina when its entire mass is concentrated at the center of mass.\n\n![](images/fb/fbab9fab43dd45fd80008704f406f94864cac57d636320d250dca86ff465f196.png)\n\nSo, our interpretation of the parallel axis theorem is the following:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\text{moment of inertia of }D \\ \\text{about a parallel line }l}_{I_l}\n\\:=\\:\n\\underbrace{\\text{moment of inertia of }D \\ \\text{about the center of mass}}_{I_M}\n\\:+\\:\n\\underbrace{\\text{moment of inertia of point mass }M \\ \\text{about the parallel line } l}_{d^2M}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $d^2M$ is always positive, we have $I_M \\leq I_l$ for any line $l$ perpendicular to the lamina. In other words, the moment of inertia about the line through its center of mass is the *smallest* moment of inertia about any line in that direction." #edb/ref 13194139534664 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186135012 "The expression below gives the cost, in dollars, of sending a letter to Spain: \n\n$$\n2w + 1\n$$\n\nHere, $w$ is the letter's weight in ounces.\n\n$$\n\\overbrace{\\:{\\color{blue}2}\\!\\!\\!{\\underset{\\underset{\\Large\\textrm{weight}}{\\large\\uparrow}}{w}}\\!\\!\\!+{\\color{red}1}\\:}^{\\large\\textrm{total cost}}\n$$\n\nThe coefficient of $w$ is the cost for each additional ounce of weight.\n\nTo illustrate:\n\n- If $w=1$ (we have a $1$-ounce letter), the cost is ${\\color{blue}2} \\cdot 1 + {\\color{red}1} = \\[math]3.$\n\n- If $w=2$ (we have a $2$-ounce letter), the cost is ${\\color{blue}2} \\cdot 2 + {\\color{red}1} = \\[/math]5.$\n\n- If $w=3$ (we have a $3$-ounce letter), the cost is ${\\color{blue}2} \\cdot 3 + {\\color{red}1} = \\[math]7.$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The number $\\color{blue}2$ in this expression represents a *unit rate.* Each time we increase the letter's weight by $1$ ounce, the cost increases by $\\color{blue}\\[/math]2$.\n\n- There is also a **base fee** of $\\color{red}\\[math]1$, which does not depend on the weight of the letter. We can think of this as the cost of sending a zero-ounce letter." #edb/ref 13194139535319 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186135047 "We define the **distance** between the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ and the vector space $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}\\}$ to be\n\n$$\n\\Vert \\mathbf{x} - \\text{proj}_{\\mathbf{a}}\\,\\mathbf{x} \\Vert.\n$$\n\nWe can also define the **angle** $\\theta$ between the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ and the subspace $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}\\}$ to be the acute angle between the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ and its orthogonal projection $\\text{proj}_{\\mathbf{a}}\\,\\mathbf{x},$ as shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/a2/a2a2670a6df46d3cac249b5c84b960c3877542be0dc656ad64840ef42ce025c4.png)\n\nFinally, we now can represent the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ as a sum of two orthogonal vectors\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x} = \\underbrace{\\textrm{proj}_{\\mathbf{a}}\\mathbf{x}}_{\\large \\mathbf{y}} + \\underbrace{(\\mathbf{x}-\\textrm{proj}_{\\mathbf{a}}\\mathbf{x})}_{\\large \\mathbf{z}},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{y} \\in \\text{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}\\}$ and $\\mathbf{z} \\perp \\text{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}\\}.$\n\n**Note:** The vector $\\textbf{x}-\\textrm{proj}_{\\mathbf{a}}\\mathbf{x},$ whose norm $\\| \\textbf{x}-\\textrm{proj}_{\\mathbf{a}}\\mathbf{x} \\|$ represents the distance from $\\mathbf{x}$ to $\\mathbf{a},$ is sometimes called the **vector rejection of $\\mathbf{x}$ from $\\mathbf{a}.$**" #edb/ref 13194139533444 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186135064 "When carrying out a linear regression in two variables, say $x$ and $y,$ our task is to find a straight line that best fits a set of data points $(x_i,y_i).$ We can easily extend this technique to situations involving three variables. \n\nThe following table shows the number of units $x_1$ and $x_2$ (in hundreds) of two goods produced by a small company in several production runs and the respective profits $y$ (in thousands of dollars) obtained by the company.   \n\n| Good Type 1 $(x_1)$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Good Type 2 $(x_2)$ | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$6$** |\n| Profit $(y)$ | $6$ | $15$ | $18$ | $24$ |\n\nSince we now have three variables, $x_1, x_2,$ and $y,$ our task is to find a *plane* of the form\n\n$$\ny = \\beta_0+ \\beta_1x_1 + \\beta_2 x_2\n$$\n\nthat best fits the data.\n\n![](images/ce/ced34ebf098fd20194fafd74f4f75bf855707ad1457e706264c9e922a7efa706.png)\n\n  \n\nSubstituting the values of $x_1$ and $x_2$ into the regression equation and equating the results to the corresponding observed values of $y,$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}+2{\\beta }_{2}=6 \\\\ {\\beta }_{0}+2{\\beta }_{1}+3{\\beta }_{2}=15 \\\\ {\\beta }_{0}+3{\\beta }_{1}+4{\\beta }_{2}=18 \\\\ {\\beta }_{0}+4{\\beta }_{1}+6{\\beta }_{2}=24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis system can be expressed in matrix notation as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 6\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 15 \\\\ 18 \\\\ 24\\end{aligned}]}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nAs usual, $X$ is the design matrix, $\\boldsymbol{\\beta}$ is the coefficient vector, and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector. \n\nSince our points do not all lie on a plane, there will be no parameters $\\beta_0,$ $\\beta_1,$ and $\\beta_2$ such that this equation is true. So instead, we seek parameters $\\beta_0,$ $\\beta_1,$ and $\\beta_2$ that best approximate the solution.\n\nIn other words, we seek to find a column vector $\\boldsymbol\\beta$ such that\n\n$$\n\\| \\mathbf{y} - X\\boldsymbol{\\beta}\\|\n$$\n\nis as small as possible.\n\nNotice that this is exactly the least-squares problem for the system $X \\boldsymbol{\\beta} = \\mathbf{y}.$ Therefore, the coefficients of the plane that best fits the data can be found as the solution to the corresponding least-squares problem! \n\nPre-multiplying the equation $X \\boldsymbol{\\beta} = \\mathbf{y}$ by $X^T,$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\nX^T \\! X {\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}} = X^T \\mathbf{y}.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X^T}X$ is invertible, and \n\n$$\n{\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}} = (X^T \\! X )^{-1} X^T \\mathbf{y}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $X$ and $\\mathbf{y}$ into the formula above and simplifying, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{\\beta }=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the plane of best fit (regression plane) is \n\n$$\ny = 2+ 7x_1-x_2.\n$$\n\n    \n\nNote that we can use the same technique to fit data involving more variables. For example, if we have four variables $x_1, x_2, x_3,$ and $y,$ then we can find a four-dimensional **hyperplane** of the form\n\n$$\ny = \\beta_0+ \\beta_1x_1 + \\beta_2 x_2 + \\beta_3 x_3\n$$\n\nthat best fits a set of data points $(x_{1i},x_{2i}, x_{3i}, y).$  The coefficients of this hyperplane are found by solving the corresponding least-squares problem. \n\nFinally, applying linear regression to situations involving three or more variables is called **multiple linear regression**." #edb/ref 13194139536171 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [#edb/ref 17592186135072 "A **type I region** in three-dimensional space is a region that can be represented in the form\n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{ (x,y,z) \\: : \\: (x,y) \\in D, \\:\\: f_1(x,y) \\leq z \\leq f_2(x,y) \\big\\},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a plane region in the $xy$-plane. \n\nA typical type I region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](images/05/05f940e247c6cd5d862d81fc3a34658353bef5513dfe862bbc67a120c0345838.png)\n\nThere is a geometrical way to check if a particular three-dimensional region is a type I region: \n\nFor any type I region, if we draw a line parallel to the $z$-axis that passes through the region, its intersection with the region must be a single (connected) interval, represented by the dashed orange segment in our diagram\n\nWe say that $D$ is the **projection** of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane. We can think of $D$ as the shadow made by $R$ in the $xy$-plane due to a light source placed directly above $R.$ \n\nWe can further refine our type I representation of $R$ depending on whether the projection $D$ is a type I or type II *plane* region:\n\n- If the projection $D$ of $R$ is a type I *plane* region, then we can express $R$ as\n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{ (x,y,z) \\: : \\: \\underbrace{a \\leq x\\leq b, \\:\\: g_1(x) \\leq y \\leq g_2(x)}_{\\text{Type I plane region}}, \\:\\: f_1(x,y) \\leq z \\leq f_2(x,y) \\big\\}.\n$$\n\n- If the projection $D$ of $R$ is a type II *plane* region, then we can express $R$ as\n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{ (x,y,z) \\: : \\: \\underbrace{c \\leq y\\leq d, \\:\\: h_1(y) \\leq x \\leq h_2(y)}_{\\text{Type II plane region}}, \\:\\:f_1(x,y) \\leq z \\leq f_2(x,y) \\big\\}.\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139533441 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186135088 "Let $n,m$ be positive integers such that $n\\leq m.$ Given an orthogonal set $\\big\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 , \\ldots, \\mathbf{a}_n \\big\\} \\subset  \\Bbb R^m$ and a vector $\\mathbf x \\in \\Bbb R^m,$ we define the **distance between the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ and the vector space $S=\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2,  \\ldots, \\mathbf{a}_n \\}$** as\n\n$$\n\\Vert{ \\color{purple} \\mathbf{x} - \\text{proj}_{S}\\,\\mathbf{x} }\\Vert.\n$$\n\nWe can also define the **angle between the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ and the subspace $S$** to be the acute angle $\\theta$ between the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ and its orthogonal projection $\\text{proj}_{S}\\,\\mathbf{x},$ as shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/07/07fa0d011bbfd49f89ddc5035a27839640a0c2624046d39fbfe8f57c476b5b25.png)\n\n \n\nFinally, notice that we can represent the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ as a sum of two orthogonal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x} = \\underbrace{\\textrm{proj}_{S}\\mathbf{x}}_{\\large \\in S} + \\underbrace{(\\mathbf{x}-\\textrm{proj}_{S}\\mathbf{x})}_{\\large \\in S^\\perp}\n$$\n\n \n\n**Note:** The vector $\\textbf{x}-\\textrm{proj}_{S}\\,\\mathbf{x},$ whose norm $\\| \\textbf{x}-\\textrm{proj}_{S}\\,\\mathbf{x} \\|$ represents the distance from $\\mathbf{x}$ to $S,$ is sometimes called the **vector rejection of $\\mathbf{x}$ from $S.$**" #edb/ref 13194139535224 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186135098 "Let's consider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown below:\n\n![](images/ee/ee731d95c111101559ad135429198dd7e0b14313272f0933b1c706006da2fbd2.svg)\n\nSuppose also that we are given the following system of equations that connects the columns of $Q$ and the columns of $A{:}$  \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}{a}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{2}=\\frac{1}{3}{a}_{2}+3{q}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{3}=\\frac{1}{4}{a}_{3}-2{q}_{1}+{q}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHow do we find $R?$\nFirst, let's rewrite the vectors $\\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2, \\mathbf{a}_3$ as a linear combination of the vectors $\\mathbf{q}_1, \\mathbf{q}_2, \\mathbf{q}_3 \\mathbin{:}$\n![](images/82/824c39c97bb115768856d672b39be8f65d8b4ecbc2ee5d6ccc9f4943594cbacd.svg)\n\nIn this new system, the coefficients of $\\mathbf{q}_1$ correspond to the first-row entries of $R,$ the coefficients of $\\mathbf{q}_2$ correspond to the second-row entries of $R,$ and the coefficients of $\\mathbf{q}_3$ correspond to the third-row entries of $R.$ Therefore,\n\n![](images/5b/5ba9887a4d9b47963af171e9275319a2564eca5a4ef8b3ef44978e2ff4b111d9.svg)\n\nTo see why this is, we can take the transpose of both sides of $A=QR{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =QR \\\\ {A}^{T} & =(QR)^{T} \\\\ {A}^{T} & ={R}^{T}{Q}^{T} \\\\ \\overset{{A}^{T}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}-\\,\\,\\,\\, & {a}_{1} & \\,\\,\\,\\,- \\\\ -\\,\\,\\,\\, & {a}_{2} & \\,\\,\\,\\,- \\\\ -\\,\\,\\,\\, & {a}_{3} & \\,\\,\\,\\,-\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{{R}^{T}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -9 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 8 & -4 & 4\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{{Q}^{T}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}-\\,\\,\\,\\, & {q}_{1} & \\,\\,\\,\\,- \\\\ -\\,\\,\\,\\, & {q}_{2} & \\,\\,\\,\\,- \\\\ -\\,\\,\\,\\, & {q}_{3} & \\,\\,\\,\\,-\\end{aligned}]}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we were to expand this matrix product, we would obtain our system of equations." #edb/ref 13194139536011 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186135112 "Consider an $m \\times n$ matrix $A$ with rank $r.$ The dimensions of the four fundamental subspaces of $A$ are as follows:\n\n- $\\text{dim}(\\text{Col}(A)) = r.$ Since the column space is the span of the columns of $A,$ its dimension is the number of linearly independent columns which, by Gaussian elimination, is $\\text{rank}(A)=r.$\n\n- $\\text{dim}(\\text{Col}(A^T)) = r.$ It can be shown that the dimension of the row space of $A$ also equals $r.$ Therefore, $\\text{rank}(A^T)=\\text{rank}(A)=r.$\n\n- $\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(A)) = n-r.$ Since $\\text{Null}(A)$ is the orthogonal complement of $\\text{Col}(A^T)$ in $\\mathbb{R}^n,$ we have that\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(A)) = \\underbrace{\\text{dim}(\\mathbb{R}^n)}_{n} - \\underbrace{\\text{dim}(\\text{Col}(A^T))}_{r} = n-r.\n$$\n\n- $\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(A^T)) = m-r.$ Since $\\text{Null}(A^T)$ is the orthogonal complement of $\\text{Col}(A)$ in $\\mathbb{R}^m,$ we have that\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(A^T)) = \\underbrace{\\text{dim}(\\mathbb{R}^m)}_{m} - \\underbrace{\\text{dim}(\\text{Col}(A))}_{r} = m-r.\n$$\n\nLet's add these dimensions to the diagram below.\n\n![](images/1c/1c550b87048bba6a7c362f00f09faca74ca9b056a93698436d0994c120971356.png)" #edb/ref 13194139535160 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186135117 "We can only compute the curl of a *vector* field, and a vector field's curl is also a vector field. So if $\\mathbf G = \\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf F,$ then we have the following map:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\,\\,\\,\\mathbf F(x,y,z)\\,\\,\\,}_{\\textrm{vector field}} \\mapsto\\underbrace{\\,\\,\\,\\mathbf G(x,y,z)\\,\\,\\,}_{\\textrm{vector field}}\n$$\n\nIn contrast, if $f(x,y, z)$ is a scalar field, then the statement $\\textrm{curl}\\, f$ has no meaning.\n\nThe curl of a vector field at a point $P$ gives us helpful information about the rotational behavior of the vector field at $P.$ We'll describe this in more detail shortly." #edb/ref 13194139533442 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186135121 "There are a few things we should note about the divergence:\n\n- We can only compute the divergence of a *vector* field, and a vector field's divergence is a *scalar* field. So if $G(x,y) = \\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F,$ then we have the following map:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\,\\,\\,\\mathbf F(x,y)\\,\\,\\,}_{\\textrm{vector field}} \\mapsto\\underbrace{\\,\\,\\,G(x,y)\\,\\,\\,}_{\\textrm{scalar field}}\n$$\n\nIn contrast, if $f(x,y)$ is a scalar field, then the statement $\\textrm{div}\\, f$ has no meaning.\n\n- We can easily extend the notion of divergence to three dimensions. So, if $\\mathbf F(x,y,z) = P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j + R\\,\\mathbf k,$ then $\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F = \\nabla\\cdot \\mathbf F = \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y} + \\dfrac{\\partial R}{\\partial z},\n$$\n\nwhere the three-dimensional differential operator $\\nabla$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\nabla =  \\mathbf i\\,\\dfrac{\\partial }{\\partial x} +  \\mathbf j\\,\\dfrac{\\partial }{\\partial y} + \\mathbf k\\,\\dfrac{\\partial }{\\partial z}.\n$$\n\n- The divergence of a vector field at a point $P$ gives us helpful information about the tendency of the vector field to behave like a source or a sink at $P.$ We'll describe this in more detail shortly." #edb/ref 13194139533442 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186135140 "When proving Green's theorem, we typically start by assuming that $D$ is a so-called **simple region** (that is, a region that is both type I and type II). Then, Green's theorem is *extended* to include regions that are not simple.\n\nLet's suppose that we've proved that Green's theorem is true for type I and type II regions. How can we use this to show that Green's theorem is also true for regions containing holes?\n\nTo illustrate how this is done, let's consider the vector field $\\mathbf F = P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j,$ and the line integral \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\oint\\limits_{\\partial D} \\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $D$ is the annular region below.\n\n![](images/57/57c22c3d7a92ce4a1a1a798f7e6a861ded2e9a6be4d8d98c36869fe29e2783d0.png)\n\nNote that $\\partial D= C_1\\cup C_2$ is positively oriented. \n\nNow, imagine splitting $D$ into two regions $D_1$ and $D_2$ by means of *cuts* along the segments $[-4, -2]$ and $[2,4]$ on the $x$-axis, as shown below \n\n![](images/e7/e7f0d41c3fb909073cdaf4ea23f4ab617032e0d24bdc8d9b9e3724e7d56abe3b.png)\n\nNotice that $D_1$ and $D_2$ are positively oriented type I regions. In addition, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1} & ={C}_{3}\\cup {C}_{8} \\\\ {C}_{2} & ={C}_{5}\\cup {C}_{10} \\\\ ∂{D}_{1} & ={C}_{3}\\cup {C}_{4}\\cup {C}_{5}\\cup {C}_{6}, \\\\ ∂{D}_{2} & ={C}_{8}\\cup (-{C}_{6})\\cup {C}_{10}\\cup (-{C}_{4})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now write the circulation of $\\mathbf F$ over $\\partial D$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={∮}_{{C}_{1}\\cup \\,{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & ={∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{8}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{5}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{10}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & +{{{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{-{C}_{4}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{6}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{∮}_{-{C}_{6}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r}_{⏟}}_{0} \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{5}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{6}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & +{\\int }_{{C}_{8}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{-{C}_{6}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{10}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{∮}_{-{C}_{4}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & ={∮}_{∂{D}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{∮}_{∂{D}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & ={∮}_{∂{D}_{1}}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y+{∮}_{∂{D}_{2}}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∬}_{{D}_{1}}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{{D}_{2}}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTo summarize,\n\n$$\n\\oint \\limits_{\\partial D}\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r = \\iint_\\limits{D}\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\textrm{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is just Green's theorem for the region $D.$" #edb/ref 13194139534659 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186135143 "Let's find the least-squares solution to $A\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b},$ where $A = QR,$ and\n![](images/4e/4e2433312f3a424aa161d5aeec63df38787c43435a9c2af44ba6b3d63f9ef065.svg)\n\nPreviously, we showed that the normal equation corresponding to $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b},$ where $A=QR,$ can be reduced to\n\n$$\nR \\mathbf{x} = Q^T \\mathbf{b}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting our matrices into this equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]}}x & =\\overset{{Q}^{T}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]}} \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]x & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}=2 \\\\ 3{x}_{2}=6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get $x_2=2.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-4(2) & =2 \\\\ 2{x}_{1} & =10 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the least-squares solution to $A\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b}$ is ![](images/72/723835f5ecb612e7d79c29ada9cc05241ffd4f233e29c9ed9a643994580a8c36.svg)" #edb/ref 13194139536071 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186135145 "Sometimes, we might need to place a constraint on the regression plane that forces it to pass through the origin. \n\nLet's once again consider our small company. The following table shows the number of units $x_1$ and $x_2$ (in hundreds) of two goods produced by the company in several production runs and the respective profits $y$ (in thousands of dollars) obtained by the company.   \n\n| Good Type 1 $(x_1)$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Good Type 2 $(x_2)$ | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$6$** |\n| Profit $(y)$ | $6$ | $15$ | $18$ | $24$ |\n\nIt's known that the profit equals zero when the company does not produce any goods, so $y=0$ when $x_1=x_2 = 0.$ The company wishes to find a plane that best fits the data *subject to* the constraint that it should pass through the point $(0,0, 0).$\n\nTherefore, we want to find the plane\n\n$$\ny = \\beta_1 x_1 + \\beta_2 x_2\n$$\n\nthat best fits the given data points. \n\nSubstituting the values of $x_1$ and $x_2$ into the regression equation and equating the results to the corresponding observed values of $y,$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 6\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 15 \\\\ 18 \\\\ 24\\end{aligned}]}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\boldsymbol{\\beta}$ is the parameter vector, and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector. \n\nThe rest of the arguments are the same as before. Since not all points lie on a plane, there will be no parameters $\\beta_1$ and $\\beta_2$ such that this equation is true. So instead, we seek parameters $\\beta_1$ and $\\beta_2$ that best approximate the solution." #edb/ref 13194139535981 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186135162 "Calculating the circulation of a vector field along a closed, piecewise smooth curve $C$ can be time-consuming because it often requires splitting $C$ into several parts, evaluating a line integral along each one, and calculating their sum. \n\nHowever, in such cases, we can often use Stokes' theorem to reduce the amount of work required by converting this sum of line integrals to a single surface integral. \n\nFor example, suppose that $C$ is the intersection of the paraboloid $z=x^2 + y^2$ and the boundary of the infinite vertical column enclosed by the planes $x = \\pm 1$ and $y=\\pm 1.$ Suppose also that $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above, and \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F} = \\bigg\\langle 0, \\: \\dfrac{1}{2}z^2, \\: xz \\bigg\\rangle.\n$$\n\nLet's use Stokes' theorem to find the circulation of $\\mathbf{F}$ along $C$.\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C} \\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}\n= \\iint\\limits_S \\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of $z=g(x,y){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2x \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy computing the curl of $\\mathbf{F},$ we obtain\n\n![](images/fd/fd25c80fd757477b724b320d1941c57630ec1c64f36bdb44d4c1c70c526fa2a9.svg)\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into expression for $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf{F}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F\\,{|}_{z=g(x,y)} & =⟨-z,\\,-z,\\,0⟩{|}_{z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =⟨{{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}_{⏟}}_{P},\\,{{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}_{⏟}}_{Q},\\,{{0}_{⏟}}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr & ={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\cdot 2x+({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\cdot 2y+0)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2({x}^{3}+{x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2}+{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}A,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](images/dc/dcf7ba14ac2c0f3da12efed6d539817ab1708273f5e8e665fdde14fee28bb4e3.png)\n\nEvaluating this integral using the usual methods, we get\n\n$$\n2\\int_{-1}^{1} \\int_{-1}^{1} x^3 + x^2 y + x y^2 + y^3 \\: \\textrm{d}y  \\: \\textrm{d}x = 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C} \\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r} = 0.\n$$\n\n**Note**: Stokes theorem tells us that \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_S \\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n$$\n\n depends only on the circulation of $\\mathbf F$ at the boundary of $S.$ Therefore, we could have evaluated our line integral by selecting *any* surface with $C$ as its bounding curve and integrating $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf F$ over this surface." #edb/ref 13194139534656 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186135165 "A **type II region** in three-dimensional space is a region that can be represented in the form\n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{ (x,y,z) \\: : \\: (y,z) \\in D, \\:\\: f_1(y,z) \\leq x \\leq f_2(y,z) \\big\\},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a plane region in the $yz$-plane. \n\nA typical three-dimensional type II region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](images/e4/e4c9cbbad2f13e9cc125ddbb89125ed2d734eee3f80737125961f4af1846a972.png)\n\nFor any type II region, if we draw a line parallel to the $x$-axis that passes through the region, its intersection with the region must be a single (connected) interval.\n\nWe can further refine our type II representation of $R$ depending on whether the projection $D$ is a type I or type II *plane* region:\n\n- If the projection $D$ of $R$ is a type I *plane* region, then we can express $R$ as\n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{ (x,y,z) \\: : \\: \\underbrace{a \\leq y\\leq b, \\:\\: g_1(y) \\leq z \\leq g_2(y)}_{\\text{Type I plane region}}, \\:\\: f_1(y,z) \\leq x \\leq f_2(y,z) \\big\\}.\n$$\n\n- If the projection $D$ of $R$ is a type II *plane* region, then we can express $R$ as\n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{ (x,y,z) \\: : \\: \\underbrace{c \\leq z\\leq d, \\:\\: h_1(z) \\leq y \\leq h_2(z)}_{\\text{Type II plane region}}, \\:\\: f_1(y,z) \\leq x \\leq f_2(y,z) \\big\\}.\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139533441 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186135166 "A **type III region** in three-dimensional space is a region that can be represented in the form\n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{ (x,y,z) \\: : \\: (x,z) \\in D, \\:\\: f_1(x,z) \\leq y \\leq f_2(x,z) \\big\\},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a plane region in the $xz$-plane. \n\nA typical three-dimensional type III region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](images/c3/c3333ded3e00e7d7408f0dbd37807f1adec0301691cc6311871bfea6d7b9fa15.png)\n\nFor any type III region, if we draw a line parallel to the $y$-axis that passes through the region, its intersection with the region must be a single (connected) interval.\n\nWe can further refine our type III representation of $R$ depending on whether the projection $D$ is a type I or type II *plane* region:\n\n- If the projection $D$ of $R$ is a type I *plane* region, then we can express $R$ as\n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{ (x,y,z) \\: : \\: \\underbrace{a \\leq x\\leq b, \\:\\: g_1(x) \\leq z \\leq g_2(x)}_{\\text{Type I plane region}}, \\:\\: f_1(x,z) \\leq y \\leq f_2(x,z) \\big\\}.\n$$\n\n- If the projection $D$ of $R$ is a type II *plane* region, then we can express $R$ as\n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{ (x,y,z) \\: : \\: \\underbrace{c \\leq z\\leq d, \\:\\: h_1(z) \\leq x \\leq h_2(z)}_{\\text{Type II plane region}}, \\:\\: f_1(x,z) \\leq y \\leq f_2(x,z) \\big\\}.\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139533441 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186135183 "To **cube** a number means to multiply that number by itself and then multiply the result by the original number once more.\n\nFor example, to \"cube $2$\", we need to multiply $2$ by $2$ to make $4,$ and then multiply this result by $2$ once more. We write the \"cube of $2$\" as $2^3,$ so we have\n\n$$\n2^3 = \\underbrace{2\\cdot 2}_{\\large 4} \\cdot 2 = 4 \\cdot 2 = 8.\n$$\n\nWe would then say that \"the cube of $2$ is $8$\". We could also say \"$2$ cubed equals $8$\".\n\nThe number ${\\color{blue}3}$ in the expression $2^{\\color{blue}3}$ is called the power (or exponent).\n\nThe act of cubing a number can be represented geometrically. For example, let's start by representing the number $2$ with the following collection of two items:\n\n![](images/b6/b6a13017dcbcf25a66ab576189690e8879a4ccbb3c4c5562b129f4dd15af4e85.png)\n\nThen we can picture $2^3$ by creating a bigger shape (called a **cube**) with length, width and height all equal to $2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/30/305e02bdaa4c3efcb9d8047eeb3d3573f39c08714e30ba6053cfc4441053156b.png)\n\nThere are $8$ items in total in our big cube (one little cube is hiding behind the others), indicating that $2^3=8.$\n\nClearly, $1^3=1\\cdot1\\cdot1={\\color{blue}1}.$ The next few first basic cubes are shown below:\n\n| $2^3=2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 2={\\color{blue}8}$ | $3^3=3 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 3={\\color{blue}27}$ | $4^3=4 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 4={\\color{blue}64}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| ![](images/30/305e02bdaa4c3efcb9d8047eeb3d3573f39c08714e30ba6053cfc4441053156b.png) | ![](images/cb/cb3171dc7f7a2e9838c3e8b46c3ce385be28624b4474391b9bb211b5a599fa55.png) | ![](images/65/6546ffc5df407b29df601252e1f5afa8e01b17dca5c7169a6321d550d86255c3.png) |\n\nThe cubes of our usual counting numbers are called **cube numbers** or **perfect cubes**. We can write them as follows:\n\n$$\n1^3, 2^3, 3^3, 4^3, 5^3, 6^3, \\ldots \\qquad \\text{or} \\qquad {\\color{blue}1}, {\\color{blue}8}, {\\color{blue}27}, {\\color{blue}64}, {\\color{blue}125}, {\\color{blue}216}, \\ldots\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139535148 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186135184 "Cubing a negative number *always* results in another negative number.\n\nThis is because a negative times a negative gives a positive value, and then a positive times a negative is a negative again:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-)\\,\\cdot \\,(-)\\,\\cdot \\,(-)\\,=\\,{{(-)\\,\\cdot \\,(-)}_{⏟}}_{(+)}\\,\\cdot \\,(-)\\,=\\,(+)\\,\\cdot \\,(-)\\,=\\,(-)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor example,\n\n$$\n({\\color{red}-5})^3 = \\underbrace{({\\color{red}-5}) \\cdot ({\\color{red}-5})}_{\\large {\\color{blue}25}} \\cdot ({\\color{red}-5}) = {\\color{blue}25} \\cdot ({\\color{red}-5}) = {\\color{red}-125}.\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139535148 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186139608 "How many times greater is the size of the digit $\\color{blue}2$ in ${\\color{blue}2}3.5$ than the size of the digit $\\color{red}2$ in $98.{\\color{red}2}$?\n\nLet's start by writing down the place values for ${\\color{blue}{2}}3.5$:\n\n| **tens** | **ones** |  | **tenths** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\color{blue}2$ | $3$ | **.** | $5$ |\n\nThen we write the place values for $98.{\\color{red}{2}}$:\n\n| **tens** | **ones** |  | **tenths** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $9$ | $8$ | **.** | $\\color{red}2$ |\n\nNow, let's jump from the tenths place to the tens place. Remember that the decimal does not correspond to a place value, so we jump right over it.\n\n| **tens** | **ones** |  | **tenths** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\color{blue}2$ |  | **.** | $\\color{red}2$ |\n| ![](images/6b/6bbc60ef774908b54aed0086a14932024fc75326befe76fe0e9ac3ab95f4b0e3.png) |  |  |  |\n\nWe have that\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{10\\times10}_{\\color{black}2\\,\\text{steps}}=100\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of the digit $\\color{blue}2$ in the tens place is $100$ times larger than the value of the digit $\\color{red}2$ in the tenths place." #edb/ref 13194139533364 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186139610 "How many times *smaller* is the value of the digit $5$ in the tenths place than the value of the digit $5$ in the tens place?\n\nLet's move from the tens place to the tenths place in the place value chart:\n\n| **hundreds** | **tens** | **ones** |  | **tenths** | **hundredths** | **thousandths** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n|  | $\\color{blue}5$ |  | **.** | $\\color{red}5$ |  |  |\n| ![](images/48/48a2d7c663340521f05e0a728d92e28a252259d5907d77f94f41f86b0d5caf4c.png) |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nWe see that the value of the digit $5$ in the tenths place is $100$ times *smaller* than the value of the digit $5$ in the tens place, because\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{10\\times10}_{\\color{black}2\\,\\text{steps}}=100\n$$\n\nThis is the same as saying that the size of the digit $\\color{red}5$ in the tenths place is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{100}\n$$\n\nthe size digit $\\color{blue}5$ in the tens place." #edb/ref 13194139533364 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [#edb/ref 17592186139626 "A **power of ten** is a number where the leading digit is $1$, and the remaining digits are all zero.\n\nFor example, the following numbers are all powers of ten:\n\n$$\n10, \\qquad 100, \\qquad 1,000, \\qquad 10,000, \\qquad 100,000, \\qquad \\dots\n$$\n\nAny power of ten can be written as $10^{\\,\\boxed{ {\\phantom{0}}}},$ where the missing number is equal to the number of zeros in the power of $10$.\n\nFor example, notice that the number $10,000$, which is a power of ten, has a block of $\\color{blue}4$ zeros:\n$1\\!\\underbrace{\\color{blue}0000}_{\\large{ { {\\color{blue}4}} \\text{ zeros}}}\\!\\!\\!\\!$\n\nTherefore, we can write the number $10,000$ as follows:\n\n$$\n10^{\\color{blue}4}\n$$\n\nWe say that the number $10,000$ is expressed in **exponent form.** The number ${\\color{blue}4}$ is called the **exponent**, and we say that the **base** is $10$." #edb/ref 13194139536831 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [#edb/ref 17592186139627 "Remember that when we multiply a decimal by a power of ten, we shift the decimal place to the right by the number of zeros in the power of ten.\n\nWhen the power of ten is written in exponent form, the exponent tells us the number of places to shift the decimal point.\n\nFor instance, to find $47 \\times 10^{1}$, we first rewrite $10^1$ as $1$ followed by a block of $1$ zero:\n$10^{\\color{red}1} = 1\\!\\!\\underbrace{0}_{\\large{ { {\\color{blue}1}} \\text{ zero}}}\\!\\!\\!$\n\nSince we're multiplying by $10$ and $10$ has $\\color{blue} 1$ zero, we move the decimal point $\\color{blue}1$ place to the right.\n\n$$\n47.0 \\rightarrow 470\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n47 \\times 10^{1} = 470 \\,\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139536831 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [#edb/ref 17592186139688 "A **power of ten** is any whole number where the leading digit is $1$, and the remaining digits are all zeros.\n\nFor example, the following numbers are all powers of ten:\n\n$$\n10, \\qquad 100, \\qquad 1,000, \\qquad 10,000, \\qquad 100,000, \\qquad 1,000,000\n$$\n\nWe can multiply a number by a power of ten by adjoining the number of zeros in the power of ten to the number.\n\nFor example, to calculate $5 \\times 10,000$, we first count the number of zeros in the power of ten.\n\n$$\n1\\underbrace{\\color{blue}0000}_{\\large{ { {\\color{blue}4}} \\text{ zeros}}}\\!\n$$\n\nNow, to find ${\\color{red}5} \\times 10,000$, we join ${\\color{red}5}$ with the block of $\\color{blue}4$ zeros:\n${\\color{red}5}\\underbrace{\\color{blue}0000}_{\\large{ { {\\color{blue}4}} \\text{ zeros}}} = 50,000$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n5 \\times 10,000 = 50,000\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139536831 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [#edb/ref 17592186139708 "To multiply a decimal by a multi-digit whole number, such as $28 \\times 6.2$, we can use the usual method of removing and replacing the decimal point.\n\n**Step 1.** First, ignore the decimal point and multiply as if both numbers were whole numbers:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\begin{array}{ccccc}\\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} {\\color{lightgray}\\begin{array}{c}\\displaystyle \\boxed{ {\\color{blue}4}} \\\\ \\displaystyle \\boxed{ {\\color{blue}1}}\\end{array} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em}} & \\displaystyle  \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} 2 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\displaystyle \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} 8 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\\\ \\displaystyle \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\times  \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\displaystyle \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\phantom{0} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\displaystyle \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\phantom{0} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\displaystyle \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} 6 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\displaystyle \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} . 2 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\\\ \\hline \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\displaystyle \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} 5 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\displaystyle \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} 6 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\\\ \\displaystyle \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} + \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\displaystyle \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} 1 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\displaystyle \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} 6 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\displaystyle \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} 8 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\displaystyle \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} 0 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\\\ \\hline \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} 1 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\displaystyle \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} 7 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\displaystyle \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} 3 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} & \\displaystyle \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} 6 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em} \\hspace{-0.167em}\\end{array}\\end{array}}\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** Then, we insert the decimal point to have the same number of decimal places as the decimal factor.\n\nHere, there is $\\color{blue}1$ decimal place in $6.2$, so the product will also have ${\\color{blue}{1}}$ decimal place. We take our value of $1736$ and insert a decimal point to make a number with $\\color{blue}1$ decimal place:\n\n$$\n173\\,\\overset{\\color{red}\\downarrow}{\\color{red}\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{.}}\\!\\!\\!\\underbrace{6}_{\\large{ { {\\color{blue}1}} \\text{ digit}}}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n28 \\times 6.2 = 173.6 \\,\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139536831 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [#edb/ref 17592186139709 "Suppose we want to compute the sum of two numbers with different numbers of decimal places, such as\n\n$$\n19+5.6\n$$\n\nHere, there are no decimal places in $19$, but there is $\\color{blue}1$ decimal place in $5.6$.\n\nIn order to add the two numbers, we need the two numbers to have the same number of decimal places. We can achieve this by attaching an extra zero behind the decimal place in the first number, so that it has $\\color{blue}1$ decimal place:\n\n$$\n19\\,\\overset{\\color{red}\\downarrow}{\\color{red}\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{.}}\\!\\!\\underbrace{0}_{\\large{ { {\\color{blue}1}} \\text{ zero}}}\\!\\!\n$$\n\nRemember that whenever we attach an extra zero behind the decimal place, the number remains unchanged. So the number $19.0$ is equivalent to the original number $19$.\n\nNow the addition problem reads\n\n$$\n19.0 + 5.6\n$$\n\nand we can add the decimal numbers using the usual method. First, we line up the decimal points:\n\n$$\n1\\begin{vmatrix}9 &. & 0 \\\\ + & 5 &.\\end{vmatrix}6;\n$$\n\nThen, we proceed by adding the two numbers just as we would with whole numbers:\n\n$$\n1; 1\\begin{vmatrix}9 &. & 0 \\\\ + & 5 &.\\end{vmatrix}6; 2\\begin{vmatrix}4 &.\\end{vmatrix}6\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n19 + 5.6 = 24.6\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139536831 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [#edb/ref 17592186139721 "There's a way to write repeated multiplications like $2\\times 2\\times 2$ and $5\\times 5$ in a more compact way.\n\nFor the expression $2\\times 2\\times 2$, we have that $\\color{blue}2$ is multiplied by itself $\\color{red}3$ times:\n$\\underbrace{ {\\color{blue}2} \\times {\\color{blue}2} \\times {\\color{blue}2}}_{\\large{ { {\\color{red}3}} \\text{ times}}}$\n\nTo write this more compactly, we can use the **exponent form**\n${\\color{blue}2}^{\\color{red}3}$,\npronounced as \"two to the power of three\" or \"two to the third power\".\n\nHere, the whole expression ${\\color{blue}2}^{\\color{red}3}$ is a **power**. We call $\\color{blue}2$ the **base**, and $\\color{red}3$ the **exponent**.\n\nLikewise, for the expression $5\\times 5$, we have that $\\color{blue}5$ is multiplied by itself $\\color{red}2$ times:\n$\\underbrace{ {\\color{blue}5} \\times {\\color{blue}5}}_{\\large{ { {\\color{red}2}} \\text{ times}}}$\n\nSo, in exponent form, the base must be $\\color{blue}5$ and the exponent must be ${\\color{red}2}$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}5} \\times {\\color{blue}5} = {\\color{blue}5}^{\\color{red}2}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139536831 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [#edb/ref 17592186139722 "When a power of ten is written in exponent form, we can express it as a $1$ followed by a block of zeros. The number of zeros in the block should equal the exponent. For example,\n\n$$\n10^{\\color{red}2} = 1\\!\\!\\!\\!\\underbrace{\\color{blue}00}_{\\large{ { {\\color{red}2}} \\text{ zeros}}}\\!\\! .\n$$\n\nWe can use this idea to calculate products involving powers of ten in exponent form.\n\nTo illustrate, let's work out how to compute $6 \\times 10^2$.\n\nFirst, we can expand $10^2$ by writing it as $1$ followed by a block of $2$ zeros:\n$6 \\times 10^{\\color{red}2} = 6 \\times 1\\!\\!\\!\\!\\underbrace{\\color{blue}00}_{\\large{ { {\\color{red}2}} \\text{ zeros}}}\\!\\!$\n\nNow, to find $6 \\times 100$, we join $6$ with the block of zeros:\n\n$$\n6 \\times 1{\\color{blue}00} =6 {\\color{blue}{00}}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n6 \\times 10^2 = 600\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139536831 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [#edb/ref 17592186139723 "To multiply two-digit decimals by three-digit decimals, we can use the usual method of removing and replacing the decimal point.\n\nAs an example, let's compute $0.5 \\times 0.07$. We proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1.** First, ignore the decimal points and multiply as if both numbers were whole numbers. We ignore any leading zeros during the multiplication:\n\n$$\n5 \\times 7 = 35\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** Then, insert the decimal point in the result, so that number of decimal places is equal to the total number of decimal places in *the two factors combined.*\n\nHere, there is $\\color{blue}1$ decimal place in $0.5$ and there are $\\color{blue}2$ decimal places in $0.07$, so their product will have\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}1} + {\\color{blue}2} = 3\n$$\n\ndecimal places.\n\nWe take our value of $35$ and insert a decimal point to make a number with $3$ decimal places. To do this, we need to add some leading zeros:\n\n$$\n0\\,\\overset{\\color{red}\\downarrow}{\\color{red}\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{.}}\\!\\!\\!\\underbrace{035}_{\\large{ {3} \\text{ digits}}}\\!\\!\\!\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n0.5 \\times 0.07 = 0.035 \\,\n$$" #edb/ref 131941395368